__kbPart({"name":"Leadshine","content":{"Leadshine-Developer-Guide":"---\ntype: dev-guide\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, dev-guide]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. — Developer Guide (hackathon edition)\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](Leadshine.md) · [Product portfolio](Leadshine-Products.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](Leadshine-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n\n> Leadshine (雷赛智能, SZSE 002979) is a **component vendor**. The only products with a real programming stack are the **dexterous hands** (DH116 / DH116S / DH2116), built by its subsidiary **Shenzhen Lingqiao Drive and Control Technology** (深圳灵巧驱控, brand \"Leadtron\"). Everything below is about getting a hand moving in the first hour; joint modules and industrial drives are covered briefly at the end of each section.\n\n| DH116 | DH116S | DH2116 |\n|---|---|---|\n| ![DH116](assets/leadshine-dh116.jpg) | ![DH116S](assets/leadshine-dh116s.jpg) | ![DH2116](assets/leadshine-dh2116.jpg) |\n| 11 DoF / 6 active, 545 g | 11 DoF / 6 active, 359 g | 21 DoF / 16 active, 850 g |\n\n## TL;DR — how you program it\n\n- **One SDK for all hands: `LHandProLib`** — C++ library (namespace `lhplib`) with a C API and a Python `ctypes` wrapper. Current manual **v1.8 (2026-09-16)**, latest package **20261031**. Builds for Linux x86_64 / i386 / aarch64, Windows, macOS arm64.\n- **You own the bus, the SDK owns the protocol.** You open EtherCAT / CAN FD / RS485 (or plain CAN on the DH2116), register a *send callback*, and feed received bytes back into the SDK. Ready-made examples exist for every bus in C++ and Python.\n- **Control is per joint:** target angle (°) or stroke position (0–10000), speed, and a current limit in per-mille (‰). Modes: position, velocity, torque, and two hybrids. Built-in gestures via `play_gesture`.\n- **ROS 2 Humble on Ubuntu 22.04** is the supported robot middleware: a service-based control node, a `/joint_states` bridge for RViz2, tactile topics, C++ and Python demos.\n- **Simulation = URDF + MJCF** (Apache-2.0 repo) plus an SDK **dry-run mode**. There is **no AI stack** (no policies, datasets, VLA, LeRobot) and **no public SDK for the PJ3 / HJ2 joint modules**.\n\n## SDKs and interfaces\n\nStatus of \"official\": Leadshine has **no GitHub organisation**. The public release channel is a personal GitHub account of the SDK developer (`sorrowfeng`, Shenzhen). Its release notes point to the **same Feishu wiki page** that Leadshine's official manuals link as the \"SDK download page\", so it is treated here as the vendor's working channel — but confirm with staff on site.\n\n| SDK / repo | Language | What it controls | Link | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **LHandProLib SDK** (release mirror) | C++17 core, C API, Python wrapper | DH116, DH116 V2, DH116S (6 active DoF), DH2116 (16 active DoF), generic per-axis mode (up to 30 axes) | [sorrowfeng/LHandProLib-Releases](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/LHandProLib-Releases/releases) | ✅ Binary library + headers + example sources. **Core source is private** — Leadshine's \"open-source SDK\" wording means open examples, not an open library. Each release ships an English and a Chinese SDK manual. |\n| **LHandPro** host GUI (\"upper computer\") | Qt desktop app | Jogging, homing, gestures, teaching, tactile view, 3D preview, firmware OTA, node-ID / baud-rate setup | [sorrowfeng/LHandPro-Releases](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/LHandPro-Releases/releases) | ✅ **v1.4.5 (2026-10-31)**: Windows x64, macOS arm64 (.dmg, not notarized), Linux x86_64 / aarch64 (.deb / .tar.gz, built for Ubuntu 22.04). Earlier versions were Windows-only. |\n| **`lhandpro_ws`** — official ROS 2 example | C++ / Python | DH116 / DH116S over EtherCAT (SOEM) | Zip linked from the user manuals (Feishu); build notes: [ROS2 example PDF](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf) | ✅ Packages: `lhandpro_description`, `lhandpro_interfaces`, `lhandpro_service`, `sequence_demo_cpp`, `sequence_demo_py`. File names in the manuals: `lhandpro_ws_116_2026_02_10.zip`, `lhandpro_ws_DH116S_2026_1_6.zip`. |\n| **`hand_control_ws`** — newer ROS 2 workspace | C++17 / Python | Any LHandProLib hand over CAN FD, RS485 or EtherCAT; one or several hands | [sorrowfeng/hand_control_ws](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/hand_control_ws) | 🧪 White-labelled successor of `lhandpro_ws` (README in Chinese). Adds YAML bus/hand topology, real-time state topic, tactile topic, multi-hand. Check that `thirdparty/lib` contains the SDK binaries; otherwise copy them from the SDK package. |\n| **`leadtron_hand_descriptions`** | URDF / MJCF / Python tools | Models for DH116, DH116S, DH116 V2.0 (left + right) | [sorrowfeng/leadtron_hand_descriptions](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/leadtron_hand_descriptions) | ✅ **Apache-2.0**. Includes mesh, launch and config folders; DH116 V2.0 ships an interactive MuJoCo script `sim_hand.py`. |\n| **`RaspberryPiDemo`** | Python | Hand on a Raspberry Pi over CAN FD / EtherCAT / RS485 | [sorrowfeng/RaspberryPiDemo](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/RaspberryPiDemo) | 🧪 Motion sequences, grasp cycles, GPIO triggers, **data-glove input over UDP**. Contains a full Python wrapper class (`PyLHandProLib`). SDK binaries are not in the repo. |\n| **`gsusb-canfd`** | C++17 / Python (PyPI `gsusb-canfd`) | USB-CAN FD adapters (candleLight / gs_usb type) from userspace | [sorrowfeng/gsusb-canfd](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/gsusb-canfd) | ✅ **MIT**. Default CAN FD backend of the ROS 2 workspace and the Pi demo; no kernel driver, works on Windows / macOS / Linux. |\n| DH116 \"deployment kit\" (older bundle) | — | DH116: SDK 20251128, URDF 2025-11-24, ROS 2 source, `Protocol.xlsx`, EtherCAT ESI `LSLQ_DH116.xml`, STEP/DXF | [carolyang823 repo](https://github.com/carolyang823/Leadshine-Cost-Effective-Dexterous-Hand-DH116-Designed-for-Humanoid-Robots-) | ⚠️ unverified as official; **old generation** (pre-2026-02-10 hardware). Useful mainly because it contains the protocol spreadsheet and PDF manuals in one place. |\n| Bus protocol documents | xlsx / Feishu doc | EtherCAT PDO map, CAN FD and RS485 frame formats | Feishu wiki links inside the manuals | Feishu pages redirect scripts to a login page; open in a browser or ask staff for a copy. |\n| PJ3 / HJ2 joint modules | — | — | [PJ3](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html) · [HJ2](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/hj2-series.html) | **No public SDK, ROS 2 driver, object dictionary or protocol document found.** Buses: EtherCAT or CAN FD. Protocol must come from Leadshine staff. |\n| Industrial servo / stepper drives | vendor Windows tools | EL7 / EL8 / ELD3 / EM3E / CS3E … | [Download centre](https://www.leadshine.com/downloads.html) | EtherCAT models follow CiA 402 over CoE (per the first-pass profile), so a generic EtherCAT master can drive them. Unlikely to be useful in 48 hours. One community ROS 2 package exists for EM2RS steppers: [openvmp/stepper_driver_em2rs](https://github.com/openvmp/stepper_driver_em2rs) (2023, third party). |\n\n**Feishu wiki pages linked from the manuals** (same space for all three hands): [host tool downloads](https://zcnrg1e5hv0a.feishu.cn/wiki/LxWzwOGdVi31Z1kKYe3cQ8PkneT) · [SDK downloads](https://zcnrg1e5hv0a.feishu.cn/wiki/LyKNwGTU4i82ZKkHf6mcW8iWnRh) · [URDF & MJCF downloads](https://zcnrg1e5hv0a.feishu.cn/wiki/P8j6wreHCiWFgZk4YIAcvPpxnBf) · [CAN FD protocol for DH116(S)](https://zcnrg1e5hv0a.feishu.cn/wiki/HtaywawGLiQCJokG196ckLwrn8f).\n\n## Control levels\n\nThere is **no EDU / developer edition** — every hand exposes the same interface. What differs per model is the bus and the sensor option.\n\n| Level | What you call | Units / range | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Scripted** | `play_gesture(gesture_id, velocity, current)`; GUI teaching; sequence demos | velocity in counts/s, current in ‰ | Gesture IDs are defined in firmware / GUI — ask staff or read them in the GUI. |\n| **Joint position** (default) | `set_target_angle(id, deg)` or `set_target_position(id, 0…10000)` → `move_motors(id)` | degrees, or stroke counts (0 = open, 10000 = full stroke) | `id = 0` broadcasts to all joints. Read limits with `get_limit_target_angle`. |\n| **Speed and force limit** | `set_angular_velocity(id, deg/s)` / `set_position_velocity(id, counts/s)`, `set_max_current(id, ‰)` | example values in vendor code: **200 °/s**, **20000 counts/s**, **1000 ‰** | Sent only when changed; `apply_motion_params()` forces an immediate update. `set_max_current` is your grip-force limiter. |\n| **Control mode** | `set_control_mode(id, mode)` | `LCM_POSITION`, `LCM_VELOCITY`, `LCM_TORQUE`, `LCM_VEL_TOR`, `LCM_POS_TOR`, `LCM_HOME` | Torque = motor-current control; the FOC current loop itself runs inside the hand and is not exposed. |\n| **Feedback** | `get_now_angle / position / angular_velocity / current / status / alarm`, `get_position_reached`, `get_torque_reached` | — | Alarm types: position error, over-speed, over-current, overload, over/under-voltage, encoder, stall. |\n| **Tactile** | `set_sensor_enable(1)`, `set_finger_pressure_reset()`, `get_finger_pressure`, `get_finger_normal_force[_ex]`, `get_finger_tangential_force[_ex]`, `get_finger_force_direction[_ex]`, `get_finger_proximity[_ex]` | N, degrees (0–360), proximity 0–1 | 11 sites: 5 fingertips + 5 finger pads + palm (`LSS_FINGER_1_1` … `LSS_HAND_PALM`). Only on -M / -S variants. |\n| **Device config** | node ID, CAN FD arbitration / data baud rate, RS485 baud rate, SDO read/write, firmware OTA | — | Saved to the drive; changing them mid-event can lock you out of the bus. |\n\n**Joint numbering (6-DoF hands, `LAC_DOF_6`, `LAC_DOF_6_V2`, `LAC_DOF_6_S`):** 1 thumb abduction · 2 thumb flexion · 3 index · 4 middle · 5 ring · 6 pinky.\n\n| Active joint | DH116 range | DH116S range |\n|---|---|---|\n| Thumb side swing | 0–60° | 0–91° |\n| Thumb flexion | 0–30° | 0–59° |\n| Index / middle / ring | 0–80° | 0–72° |\n| Little finger | 0–80° | 0–74° |\n\n**DH2116 (`LAC_DOF_16`):** 5 fingers × 3 motors + 1 extra thumb axis. Per finger, motors 1 and 2 work differentially (their mean bends joint 2, their difference swings joint 1 by ±20°), motor 3 drives joints 3 and 4 together. Motor commands run **0–4095**. Mapping from the manual: `M1 − M2 = θ1 · 4095/90`, `(M1 + M2)/2 = θ2 · 4095/90`, `M3 = θ3 · 4095/75`. Motors 1 and 2 constrain each other, so not every combination is reachable.\n\n**Rates (what the vendor code actually does — no kHz loop is documented):**\n\n| Path | Rate / timing | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| EtherCAT example: TPDO polling loop | every **10 ms** (100 Hz) | SDK manual example |\n| SDK inter-frame delay | unified at **10 ms** | SDK v1.8 changelog |\n| RS485 shared bus | one transaction window per **30 ms** by default (whole bus, all hands) | SDK manual |\n| ROS 2 `realtime_state` topic | **100 Hz** default | `hand_control_ws` config |\n| ROS 2 tactile topic | **50 Hz** default; one frame = 55 bus reads — lower it if the bus cannot keep up | `hand_control_ws` README |\n| CAN FD bit rates | **1 Mbps** arbitration / **5 Mbps** data (defaults in vendor config) | `hand_control_ws` config |\n\nThe \"100 MHz EtherCAT\" in the DH116 manual means a 100 Mbit/s link, not a 100 MHz control loop. Practical expectation: **~100 Hz joint commands and state**, fine for grasping and teleoperation, not for high-bandwidth finger impedance control.\n\n**Joint modules and drives:** PJ3 / HJ2 offer EtherCAT or CAN FD, but torque / position / MIT-style modes, rates and object dictionaries are **not published** ⚠️. Industrial EtherCAT drives use standard CiA 402 modes.\n\n## ROS 2 and middleware\n\n| Item | Official example `lhandpro_ws` | Newer `hand_control_ws` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Environment | Ubuntu 22.04, ROS 2 Humble, `colcon` | Same; C++17, CMake ≥ 3.8 |\n| Buses | EtherCAT (bundled SOEM master) | CAN FD (gs_usb userspace default, SocketCAN optional), RS485, EtherCAT (SOEM) |\n| Control interface | ROS 2 **services** mapped 1:1 to SDK calls | Services under `/hand_control/…` (`set_enable`, `home_motors`, `set_angle`, `set_position`, `set_max_current`, `move_motors`, `control_motors`, `play_gesture`, `get_now_*`, …) |\n| State | `lhandpro_state_publisher` → `/joint_states` | `/hand_control/realtime_state` (`HandJointState`) → `hand_state_publisher` → `/joint_states` |\n| Tactile | not in the example | `/hand_control/<hand>/tactile_state` (`TactileState`: pressure, normal force, tangential force, direction, proximity per site) |\n| Multi-hand | no | yes — same bus with different IDs, or separate buses; names like `/hand_control/left/…`, `/hand_control/right/…` |\n| Visualisation | `ros2 launch lhandpro_description display_lhandpro.launch.py` (RViz2 mirrors the real hand) | `ros2 launch hand_control_description display.launch.py` (single hand only) |\n| Demos | `sequence_demo_py`, `sequence_demo_cpp` | plus `tactile_demo_py`, `tactile_demo_cpp` |\n\n- **Not provided:** `ros2_control` hardware interface, MoveIt configuration, ROS 1 packages, DDS-level API, Isaac ROS integration. For arm + hand planning you write a thin action wrapper around the services yourself.\n- **DH2116:** no ROS 2 example was found in the manual's download list (SDK, STEP, URDF and protocol only). The newer workspace is hand-type driven (`hand_type: LAC_DOF_6_S` in YAML); whether `LAC_DOF_16` works out of the box is ⚠️ unverified.\n- **No ROS? Use the SDK directly** — Python on Windows / macOS / Linux needs no middleware.\n\n## Simulation and digital twins\n\n| Asset | Models | Where | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| URDF | DH116, DH116S, DH116 V2.0, left and right | [leadtron_hand_descriptions](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/leadtron_hand_descriptions) | Passive finger joints are coupled with `<mimic>` — a **linear** approximation (mid-stroke error up to about 3.8° on DH116). |\n| MJCF (MuJoCo) | same | same repo | Coupling modelled with `equality joint polycoef` (quadratic, closer to the real linkage). DH116 V2.0 folder has `sim_hand.py` for interactive MuJoCo control. |\n| URDF for DH2116 | DH2116 | `DH2116_URDF_Files_2026_3_16.zip`, linked in the DH2116 manual (Feishu) | MJCF for DH2116 not found ⚠️. |\n| STEP / CAD | all three hands | linked in the manuals | Use for designing the wrist flange adapter. |\n| Passive-vs-active joint tables | DH116, DH116S | spreadsheets linked in the manuals; formulas also in the description repo READMEs | Needed if you retarget human hand poses. |\n| RViz2 twin | DH116 / DH116S | both ROS 2 workspaces | Mirrors the physical hand from `/joint_states`. |\n| **SDK dry-run mode** | all | `set_dry_run_mode(true)` | Generates bus frames without hardware; `initial()` does not time out and `move_motors` skips the homing check. Good for coding before you get a hand. |\n\n- **Not found:** Isaac Sim / Isaac Lab USD assets, Gazebo worlds, Genesis or Webots models, contact-rich tactile simulation. MJCF can be imported into Isaac Lab or converted yourself.\n- Joint names in the models: active `finger11`, `finger12`, `finger21`, `finger31`, `finger41`, `finger51`; passive `finger13`, `finger22`, `finger32`, `finger42`, `finger52`.\n\n## AI stack\n\n**Leadshine offers none.** Plain statement of what exists and what does not:\n\n| Topic | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Pretrained manipulation policies, VLA / foundation models | ❌ none published |\n| Datasets | ❌ none published |\n| LeRobot / Hugging Face integration | ❌ none found |\n| Teleoperation | 🧪 Building blocks only: an optional **data glove (\"6248XL Super Sensing Data Gloves\", accessory \"for data acquisition\")** listed in the DH116S manual; a glove example in the SDK package (per the 20261031 release notes); glove input over UDP in the Raspberry Pi demo; \"glove linkage\" and camera-based hand / gesture detection inside the LHandPro GUI (per release notes). No documented end-to-end teleop pipeline. |\n| Data collection | DIY: record `/hand_control/realtime_state` and `tactile_state` with `ros2 bag`, or log from the SDK (`log_on`, `log_save`). |\n| \"Adaptive grasping algorithms\", \"multi-modal machine-learning tactile sensor\" | Vendor wording for firmware features and the sensor type; no model or training code is released. |\n| Higher-level motion intelligence | 🔭 A July 2026 investor note says the company is starting R&D on the robot \"cerebellum\" (motion-control layer). No product. |\n\n**Practical hackathon recipe (our suggestion, not a vendor feature):** webcam → MediaPipe Hands (or an XR headset's hand tracking) → map finger curl to the **6 active joints** → `set_target_position` at 20–50 Hz with a low `set_max_current`. Only six numbers per hand are needed, which makes retargeting and imitation learning unusually simple.\n\n## Compute, sensors and connectivity\n\n**Compute:** none on board. The hand is a bus slave; you bring a laptop or an SBC (x86_64, ARM64 such as Raspberry Pi or Jetson-class boards, Apple-silicon Mac, Windows PC).\n\n| Item | DH116 | DH116S | DH2116 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DoF (active) | 11 (6) | 11 (6) | 21 (16) |\n| Drive | brushless coreless motor + worm gear | same, miniaturised | brushless coreless + trapezoidal screw |\n| Size / weight | 545 g ± 10 g | 147.54 × 73.53 × 26.35 mm, **359 g ± 10 g** | 218 × 119 × 80.5 mm, **850 g ± 10 g** |\n| Supply | **12–48 V**, 2.5 A | **12–48 V**, idle 0.1 A, max 2.0 A | **24–60 V**, idle 0.16 A, rated 1 A, max 5 A (at 24 V) |\n| Buses (by model code) | CAN FD (`DH116-…`) or EtherCAT (`DH116EC-…`); RS485 mentioned | CAN FD (`DH116S-…`), EtherCAT (`DH116SEC-…`), RS485 (`DH116SRS-…`) | CAN 1 Mbps, CAN FD 1 / 5 Mbps, RS485 up to 5 Mbps (no EtherCAT listed) |\n| Tactile option | `-M`: multi-modal (normal, tangential, proximity) | `-S`: piezoresistive; `-M`: multi-modal; fingers + palm | not documented in the manual |\n| Load / speed | hook grip ≤ 30 kg, lateral grip ≤ 50 N, open-close ≥ 0.5 s | hook grip 15 kg, side grip 50 N, open-close 0.4 s | recommended load 5 kg, max five-finger grip 150 N (product page: 94 N), open-close 0.6 s |\n| Repeatability | ±0.2 mm | ±0.2 mm | ±0.6 mm |\n| Life | 1 million grasp cycles (claim) | 1 million cycles, 3-year design life | 300,000 cycles, 2-year design life |\n| Environment | −10…40 °C table value (precautions say 0–40 °C) | −10…45 °C, IP30 | — |\n| In the box / accessories | — | options: variable PSU (KJS-1509), 0.5 m EtherCAT cable, MINI USB-CANFD-I adapter, data glove | M9 debug cable + **PCAN-USB** module |\n\n**Tactile resolution (DH116S manual):** palm normal force 0.1 N over 0–25 N; fingers normal force 0.01 N over 0–20 N; tangential force 0.01 N with direction 0–360° at 1° resolution.\n\n**Wiring**\n\n| Hand | Pins / wire colours |\n|---|---|\n| DH116 (CAN FD) | red VDC · green GND · yellow CANH · black CANL |\n| DH116 (EtherCAT) | red VDC · green GND · brown RX+ · white RX− · yellow TX+ · black TX− |\n| DH116S | pins 1, 2 VDC · pins 5, 6 GND · pin 3 CANH / 485+ · pin 4 CANL / 485− (CAN FD or RS485, one or the other) |\n\n**Host adapters:** CAN FD via a gs_usb / candleLight-type USB adapter (vendor examples reference USB ID `a8fa:8598`, \"CANFD Analyser\") or any SocketCAN interface; standard CAN on Windows via PCAN; RS485 via any USB-serial adapter (vendor default 500 000 baud in ROS config); EtherCAT via a spare wired Ethernet port.\n\n## First 60 minutes checklist\n\n1. **Identify the unit (5 min).** Read the label: `DH116` / `DH116S` / `DH2116`, `EC` or `RS` in the code (bus), `L` / `R`, `-M` / `-S` (tactile). Ask whether it was built **before or after 2026-02-10** and which adapter, cable and power supply come with it.\n2. **Get the software from staff on a USB stick (5 min)** — or download: SDK package and manual from the [SDK releases](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/LHandProLib-Releases/releases), GUI from the [LHandPro releases](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/LHandPro-Releases/releases), models from [leadtron_hand_descriptions](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/leadtron_hand_descriptions). Do not rely on the Feishu links working on hackathon Wi-Fi.\n3. **Power and wire (5 min).** Power off first. 24 V bench supply with current limit about 2.5 A (DH116 / DH116S) or 5 A (DH2116). Connect the bus per the wiring table. Keep fingers clear.\n4. **Verify with the GUI (10 min).** Start LHandPro, select the bus and adapter, connect, check firmware version and serial number, **home** the hand, jog each joint, play a preset gesture. If the GUI cannot connect, code will not either — fix node ID / baud rate / adapter driver here.\n5. **Linux permissions (5 min).**\n   - CAN FD (userspace gs_usb): install the udev rule shipped in the workspace (`99-hand-control-usb.rules`), then re-plug the adapter. `LIBUSB_ERROR_ACCESS` means the rule is missing.\n   - RS485: `sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER`, then log in again.\n   - EtherCAT: `sudo setcap cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin+ep <service binary>`.\n6. **Hello-world motion, ROS 2 route (15 min).** Ubuntu 22.04 + Humble:\n   ```bash\n   cd hand_control_ws && source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash\n   colcon build --symlink-install && source install/setup.bash\n   # edit src/hand_control_service/config/service.yaml: bus type, address, hand_type\n   ros2 launch hand_control_service service.launch.py\n   ros2 service list | grep hand_control        # then: enable, home, move\n   ros2 service call /hand_control/set_enable hand_control_interfaces/srv/SetEnable \"{joint_id: 0, enable: 1}\"\n   ros2 service call /hand_control/set_position hand_control_interfaces/srv/SetPosition \"{joint_id: 3, position: 5000}\"\n   ros2 service call /hand_control/move_motors hand_control_interfaces/srv/MoveMotors \"{joint_id: 0}\"\n   ```\n   Call the `home_motors` service before the first move (look up its type with `ros2 service type`). With the official `lhandpro_ws` instead: `colcon build`, copy `libLHandProLib.so` to `/usr/local/lib`, `sudo ldconfig`, `ros2 launch lhandpro_service lhandpro.launch.py`, then `ros2 launch sequence_demo_py sequence.launch.py`.\n7. **Hello-world motion, SDK route (alternative).** Run the bus example from `share/LHandProLib/examples/` (`CANFD_cpp`, `RS485_cpp`, `EtherCAT_cpp`, or the `*_python` folders). The call order is always: register send callback → feed receive data → `initial(mode[, node_id])` → `set_control_mode(0, LCM_POSITION)` → `set_enable(0, 1)` → `home_motors(0)` → `set_target_angle(i, deg)` + `set_angular_velocity(i, 200)` + `set_max_current(i, 1000)` → `move_motors()`.\n8. **Read sensors (5 min).** `ros2 topic echo /hand_control/realtime_state` and `ros2 topic hz …` (expect about 100 Hz). Tactile: set `tactile_enabled: true` in the YAML, call `set_finger_pressure_reset`, then `ros2 topic echo /hand_control/hand/tactile_state`. SDK route: `get_now_angle`, `get_now_current`, `get_finger_normal_force`.\n9. **Start the twin (5 min).** `ros2 launch hand_control_description display.launch.py` — RViz2 should follow the real hand. No hardware yet? Load the MJCF in MuJoCo or enable SDK dry-run and keep coding.\n10. **Lock in a safe default.** Decide a conservative `set_max_current` (start at 300–500 ‰) and speed for anything near people, and write them into your launch config before building further.\n\n## Known gotchas and safety\n\n**Version and configuration traps**\n\n- **Generation split at 2026-02-10 (DH116):** the manual lists separate \"old version\" SDK, URDF, ROS 2 project, GUI and protocol files for units made earlier. Mixing generations is the most likely cause of a hand that connects but behaves wrongly.\n- **Hand type must match:** `LAC_DOF_6` (DH116), `LAC_DOF_6_V2` (DH116 V2), `LAC_DOF_6_S` (DH116S), `LAC_DOF_16` (DH2116). Over EtherCAT the SDK auto-detects type, side, firmware and serial via SDO; on other buses set it yourself.\n- **Homing first:** motion calls return `LER_NOT_HOME` until `home_motors` has run, unless you explicitly allow `set_move_no_home(1)`.\n- **Units are unusual:** current in **per-mille**, stroke position **0–10000**, DH2116 motor commands **0–4095**, speed in °/s or counts/s depending on the call.\n- **`LER_KEY_FUNC_UNINIT` from `initial()`** = send callback not registered. Sensor values frozen = you stopped feeding received frames into the SDK.\n- **ROS 2 parameters must be floats:** `tactile_publish_rate_hz: 50` (integer) crashes the node; write `50.0`.\n- **EtherCAT NIC index:** the official example hard-codes the network interface index in `hand_control_service.cpp`; change it and rebuild. The newer workspace takes it from YAML (`channel`).\n- **CAN FD driver conflict:** the userspace gs_usb backend and the Linux kernel `gs_usb` driver cannot both hold the adapter. Use one or the other (the workspace has a build switch for SocketCAN; clean-rebuild after switching).\n- **RS485 is half-duplex and slow:** about 33 transactions per second for the whole bus by default; two hands on one port need the SDK's `RS485SharedBus`, not two polling loops.\n- **Duplicate ROS nodes** (after Ctrl+C) make the RViz model jump between two states — check `ros2 topic info -v /joint_states` shows one publisher.\n- **The ROS 2 PDF suggests a third-party one-line installer for ROS**; use the standard `apt` install instead.\n- **Documentation inconsistencies** (do not quote blindly in your pitch): DH116S weight 359 g (manual) vs 379 g (trade-show material) vs \"< 380 g\" (store); DH116S load 15 kg hook grip (manual table) vs 10 kg \"light-load handling\" (manual text); DH2116 grip 150 N (manual) vs 94 N (product page); robot overview page says CANopen where the manuals say CAN FD.\n\n**Safety**\n\n- The hands are strong: DH116 hook-grip load up to 30 kg and 50 N lateral grip. **Pinch hazard** — keep `set_max_current` low and add a software stop (`stop_motors`) on a key.\n- **Worm-gear fingers are self-locking.** Do not force them by hand; they do not back-drive and reset on power-up. This also means a hand that loses power **keeps gripping** — plan how to release an object or a person's finger.\n- No functional-safety features (STO, certified force limiting) are documented for the hands; they are components, not certified for contact with patients. For T04 demos use dummies or keep contact forces minimal and supervised.\n- Power off before plugging or unplugging; do not open the housing or remove the tamper label; avoid dust, moisture and oil (DH116S is IP30); stop and let it cool if it gets hot.\n- Avoid collisions of the fingers with the arm or table — plan arm paths with the hand's open envelope.\n- macOS GUI build is ad-hoc signed and not notarized (Gatekeeper prompt). Firmware OTA exists in the GUI — **do not flash firmware on a loaned unit** without staff.\n\n## Community resources\n\n| Resource | Link | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| SDK releases + manuals (EN / CN) | [LHandProLib-Releases](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/LHandProLib-Releases/releases) | API reference, changelog, packages |\n| GUI releases | [LHandPro-Releases](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/LHandPro-Releases/releases) | bring-up tool |\n| ROS 2 workspace | [hand_control_ws](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/hand_control_ws) | services, topics, multi-hand configs, troubleshooting section |\n| URDF / MJCF | [leadtron_hand_descriptions](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/leadtron_hand_descriptions) | simulation, RViz |\n| Embedded Python demo | [RaspberryPiDemo](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/RaspberryPiDemo) | reference Python wrapper, sequences, glove input |\n| User manuals (PDF) | [DH116 / DH116P](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf) · [DH116S](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf) · [DH2116](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH2116_Series_Dexterous_Hand_User_Manual.pdf) | specs, pinouts, download links |\n| Downloads (drives, PLCs) | [leadshine.com/downloads](https://www.leadshine.com/downloads.html) | manuals, tuning software, 3D models |\n| Contacts | sales@leadshine.com · North America: sales@leadshineusa.com · China robot-components hotline 400-155-8078 | pre-sales and support |\n\n- **No forum, Discord, developer portal or official GitHub organisation** was found. Repositories above have near-zero stars — there is no community to ask; **the Leadshine / Leadtron engineers on site are the support channel**.\n- Questions worth asking them first: protocol documents for your bus; gesture ID list; whether a `ros2_control` driver or DH2116 ROS 2 package exists internally; PJ3 / HJ2 protocol and control modes; spare adapter and flange drawings.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Leadshine profile in this knowledge base (first pass)](Leadshine.md)\n- [DH116 / DH116P user manual (PDF, Leadshine store)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116S user manual (PDF, Leadshine store)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH2116 user manual (PDF, Leadshine store)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH2116_Series_Dexterous_Hand_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS2 example project description (PDF, Leadshine store)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [LHandProLib SDK releases and SDK manual v1.8 (GitHub release mirror)](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/LHandProLib-Releases/releases)\n- [LHandProLib release 20261031 (links the Feishu SDK page)](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/LHandProLib-Releases/releases/tag/20261031)\n- [LHandPro GUI releases (GitHub release mirror)](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/LHandPro-Releases/releases)\n- [hand_control_ws ROS 2 workspace README](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/hand_control_ws)\n- [leadtron_hand_descriptions (URDF / MJCF, Apache-2.0)](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/leadtron_hand_descriptions)\n- [RaspberryPiDemo README and Python wrapper](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/RaspberryPiDemo)\n- [gsusb-canfd README](https://github.com/sorrowfeng/gsusb-canfd)\n- [DH116 deployment kit repository (third-party upload)](https://github.com/carolyang823/Leadshine-Cost-Effective-Dexterous-Hand-DH116-Designed-for-Humanoid-Robots-)\n- [openvmp/stepper_driver_em2rs (third-party ROS 2 package)](https://github.com/openvmp/stepper_driver_em2rs)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [Leadshine robot components overview](https://www.leadshine.com/robot/index.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [HJ2 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/hj2-series.html)\n- [Leadshine offices and contacts](https://en.leadshine.com/contact/contact_leadshine_offices.html)\n- [Leadshine Chinese site — robot core components](https://www.leisai.com/product_1073.html)\n- [Investor research note, July 2026 (10jqka)](https://stock.10jqka.com.cn/20260723/c678395454.shtml)\n- [WeChat: Leadshine tactile dexterous hand on CCTV, Aug 2026](https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/BzrqQMtciwjRFgLOlOiouQ)\n","Leadshine-Papers-and-Research":"---\ntype: papers\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, papers]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. — Papers and Research\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](Leadshine.md) · [Solutions and projects](Leadshine-Solutions-and-Projects.md) · [Developer guide](Leadshine-Developer-Guide.md)\n\n> **Bottom line for a hackathon team:** Leadshine (雷赛智能, Shenzhen Stock Exchange 002979) is an engineering and manufacturing company, not a research lab. There is **no 2022–2026 paper, dataset, model or benchmark from the company**, and **no paper was found that uses its DH116 / DH116S / DH2116 hands or PJ3 / HJ2 joint modules**. What exists: the founder's classic robot-control papers (1987–1991), an SDK / ROS 2 / URDF package for the hands, and many third-party lab papers that use Leadshine **stepper motors, drives and motion cards** as building blocks.\n\n## Research footprint\n\n- **Founder is a control theorist.** Li Weiping (李卫平, \"Dr. Warren Li\" on the English site) holds an MIT PhD in robotics and automation (1990) and co-wrote the textbook *Applied Nonlinear Control* with Jean-Jacques Slotine; he later taught at a US state university (\"WSU\") and at HKUST before founding Leadshine in 1997. This is the company's only visible academic lineage (\"innovation gene originated from MIT\" in its own words).\n- **No current publication activity found.** No company-authored arXiv / ICRA / IROS / RSS / CoRL paper, no research or technology blog with papers, no citation blocks. The English blog holds product-selection explainers only (stepper vs. servo, integrated vs. separate drives).\n- **R&D is in-house and product-driven.** The site states about **10% of revenue** goes to R&D each year and that most R&D engineers hold a master's degree or PhD (page is dated; headcount there \"more than 100\" — the group has **1,580 employees** per current listing data). A Shenzhen-government-approved engineering laboratory for motion control (2015) and a \"Postdoctoral Innovation Practice Base\" plaque are listed, without published output. Board-level academic tie: independent director Wang Hong is a professor at Harbin Institute of Technology (Shenzhen).\n- **Hands come from a subsidiary.** The Chinese product page names 深圳市灵巧驱控技术有限公司 (Shenzhen Lingqiao Drive-Control Technology, a group company in which Li Weiping is manager and director) as developer of the DH116. No papers under that name were found either.\n- **Open-source posture: weak.** No official GitHub organisation. Hand SDK, ROS 2 example and URDF / MJCF are called \"open-source\" on the product page but ship as download archives (Feishu wiki links in the manuals) with **no licence text found**.\n- **How this doc was researched:** web search **was available** (2 extended searches, both used; neither returned a Leadshine-specific paper). Discovery then relied on direct fetches: arXiv listing search for \"Leadshine\" (0 hits — arXiv searches titles and abstracts only), GitHub repository listing, and Europe PMC open-access full-text search (37 hits for 2021–2026, the track-relevant ones opened and read). Chinese-language journals (CNKI) and IEEE Xplore full text were **not** searched, so Chinese-only papers by staff may exist.\n\n## Company-authored and company-affiliated papers\n\n**2022–2026: none found.** The only verifiable company-affiliated academic work is the founder's pre-company research at MIT. It is control theory, useful as background on adaptive / force control of manipulators, not as a build recipe.\n\n| Paper | Authors / organisation | Venue, year | What it contributes (one line) | Artifacts | Tracks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [Applied Nonlinear Control](https://openlibrary.org/works/OL4465059W) (textbook) | J.-J. E. Slotine, Weiping Li (MIT) | Prentice Hall, 1991 | Standard graduate text on Lyapunov methods, sliding-mode and adaptive control, with robot-manipulator examples; co-authorship is stated in Li's official executive résumé | — | — |\n| [Composite adaptive control of robot manipulators](https://doi.org/10.1016/0005-1098%2889%2990094-0) | J.-J. E. Slotine, Weiping Li (MIT) | Automatica 25(4), 1989 | Adaptive controller that uses both tracking error and prediction error to speed up parameter convergence | — | — |\n| [Adaptive manipulator control: A case study](https://doi.org/10.1109/9.14411) | J.-J. E. Slotine, Weiping Li (MIT) | IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control 33(11), 1988 | Experimental validation of the adaptive controller on a real manipulator | — | — |\n| [On the Adaptive Control of Robot Manipulators](https://doi.org/10.1177/027836498700600303) | J.-J. E. Slotine, Weiping Li (MIT) | International Journal of Robotics Research 6(3), 1987 | The \"Slotine–Li\" adaptive controller: globally convergent tracking without joint-acceleration measurement | — | — |\n\nNotes\n- Attribution of the three journal papers to Leadshine's founder is an inference from name, institution and period (MIT PhD completed 1990); the textbook co-authorship is explicit in his résumé. ⚠️ unverified by a source that names the papers directly.\n- The same résumé lists a Chinese monograph, 《运动控制系统原理与应用》 (roughly \"Principles and Applications of Motion Control Systems\"), and \"several academic papers\". ⚠️ unverified — publisher, year and co-authors not confirmed.\n\n## Datasets, models and benchmarks released\n\n**No dataset, AI model, foundation model or benchmark has been released by Leadshine.** The only public developer artifacts are hardware-support software for the dexterous hands:\n\n| Name | Type | Licence | Link | Useful for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| LHandProLib SDK + demos (DH116 / DH116S / DH2116) | C++ / Python SDK; Windows and Linux; x86 and ARM | Called \"open-source\" by Leadshine; no licence text found ⚠️ unverified | Download links inside the user manuals: [DH116S manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf), [DH2116 manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH2116_Series_Dexterous_Hand_User_Manual.pdf) | Position / force commands and sensor read-out from your own code |\n| LHandPro ROS 2 example project (DH116S) | ROS 2 packages (state publisher, interfaces, service node, C++ / Python sequence demos, RViz2 mirror) | not stated | [ROS 2 example description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf) | Fastest route to drive the hand from a ROS 2 stack |\n| Hand URDF and MJCF models | Robot description files for RViz / MuJoCo | not stated | Linked from the user manuals above (Feishu wiki; links could not be opened from here) | Simulation, retargeting, motion planning before hardware access |\n| CAN FD protocol document | Protocol specification | not stated | Linked from the user manuals (Feishu wiki) | Writing a driver for an MCU or a non-ROS controller |\n\nCommunity software for Leadshine industrial drives (not from Leadshine, no papers attached):\n\n| Name | Type | Licence | Link | Useful for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| openvmp/stepper_driver_em2rs | ROS 2 (Humble tag) ros2_control driver for EM2RS-series Modbus RTU stepper drives; author reports testing only on a compatible third-party drive | Apache-2.0 | [GitHub](https://github.com/openvmp/stepper_driver_em2rs) | Driving a Leadshine RS485 stepper drive from ROS 2 |\n| marcoreps/linuxcnc_leadshine_EL8 | LinuxCNC + EtherCAT configuration for EL8 servo drives | not stated | [GitHub](https://github.com/marcoreps/linuxcnc_leadshine_EL8) | Worked example of EL8 EtherCAT set-up on Linux |\n\n## Third-party papers using their hardware or software\n\nNone of these uses the dexterous hands or joint modules. They show the typical academic use of Leadshine: **low-cost stepper motors, stepper drives and PC motion cards inside custom rigs and robots**. All were opened in open-access full text and the Leadshine part quoted from the methods section.\n\n| Paper | Authors / organisation | Venue, year | Hardware / software used | What it shows | Tracks |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [A Spatiotemporally Coupled Carbon Flux Monitoring System for Salt Marsh Wetlands Based on Integrated Land–Air Collaborative Intelligence](https://doi.org/10.3390/s26102966) | Zha, Wang, Shi — Nanjing Normal University | Sensors, 2026 | Leadshine 42-frame stepper motor (with a third-party TB6600 driver, STM32 MCU) in an auto-lifting sensor mast, 0–1.5 m | Field system for long-term, low-intervention carbon-flux monitoring with cloud storage, multi-algorithm sensor fusion and a land–air architecture | T03 (T03.2, T03.7) |\n| [Influence of acupuncture twisting parameters on analgesic effects mediated by mast cells in AA rat models](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bj.2025.100876) | Yu, Wang, Qiao, Li, Liu, Huang, Yao — Shanghai Univ. of Medicine & Health Sciences / Fudan | Biomedical Journal, 2026 (DOI year 2025) | Leadshine 42CM08 stepper (0.8 N·m holding torque) as needle-twisting actuator | Robot-controlled twisting angle and frequency change the analgesic effect in an animal model; companion to the system paper below | T04 (T04.6) |\n| [A robot arm-assisted acupuncture system with motion and force monitoring: establishment and validation](https://doi.org/10.3389/fbioe.2025.1563263) | Li, Yu, Qiao, Ren, Xu, Wang, Gu, Yao — Fudan University et al. | Frontiers in Bioengineering and Biotechnology, 2025 | Leadshine 35CM015 stepper mounted on the flange of a RealMan RM65 arm as a rotary end-effector, plus force monitoring | Arm + small rotary tool reproduces a therapist's needle manipulations; mean absolute motion error below 0.2 mm; similarity to manual technique quantified | T04 (T04.2, T04.6) |\n| [FGA-Corn: an integrated system for precision pesticide application in center leaf areas using deep learning vision](https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2025.1571228) | Song, Li, Song, Li — Weifang University / Henan Agricultural University | Frontiers in Plant Science, 2025 | Leadshine HBS57 closed-loop stepper driver (STM32 relay layer) turning a dosing funnel | YOLOv8-based detection triggers targeted dosing instead of blanket spraying — a camera → decision → stepper actuation pipeline | T03 (T03.6) |\n| [A radiation therapy platform to enable upright cone beam computed tomography and future upright treatment on existing photon therapy machines](https://doi.org/10.1002/mp.17523) | Korte et al. — Peter MacCallum Cancer Centre / University of Melbourne | Medical Physics, 2025 | Leadshine EM556S stepper driver (800 microsteps/rev) on a slewing drive rotating a patient chair, 0.006° per step | A stepper + slew drive safely rotates a seated patient with high angular accuracy; design process with clinicians and patients | T04 (T04.4) |\n| [Soft multifunctional bistable fabric mechanism for electronics-free autonomous robots](https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.ads8734) | Yang, Feng, Sun, Wei, Zou, Zhu, Gu — Shanghai Jiao Tong University | Science Advances, 2025 | Leadshine HS2257 stepper in the torque–angle characterisation rig (test equipment only) | Fabric bistable actuator-valves for soft grippers and crawlers running on one constant-pressure air supply | — |\n| [A Robotic Solution for Precision Smoothing and Roughening of Precast Concrete Surfaces: Design and Experimental Validation](https://doi.org/10.3390/s24113336) | Gang, Duan, Wang, Nan, Song — Xi'an University of Architecture and Technology | Sensors, 2024 | All five joints on Leadshine steppers: 86CM85-BZ (X, Y), 86CM45-BZ brake motor (Z) with M860D driver, geared 86 / 57 steppers (R1, R2) | Gantry robot with coverage path planning (B-splines, bio-inspired neural network, A* escape from dead zones) around embedded obstacles | T03 (T03.4), T01 (T01.2, T01.6) |\n| [Development and Validation of a Novel In Vitro Joint Testing System for Reproduction of In Vivo Dynamic Muscle Force](https://doi.org/10.3390/bioengineering10091006) | Yang, Wang, Zheng, Cheng, Zou, Zhao, Tsai — Shanghai Jiao Tong University | Bioengineering, 2023 | Complete Leadshine chain: DMC3C00 PC motion-control card, ACC-XC00 connection board, DMA882S drive, 86CM120 stepper | Cable-loading rig reproduces time-varying knee-muscle forces (error −6.2% to +1.81%) for implant testing and rehabilitation guidance | T04 (T04.3) |\n| [Fully 3D printed flexible, conformal and multi-directional tactile sensor with integrated biomimetic and auxetic structure](https://doi.org/10.1038/s44172-023-00131-x) | Wei, Li, Domingos, Qian, Zhu, Yan, Ren, Wei — University of Manchester / Oxford / Jilin University | Communications Engineering, 2023 | Leadshine 6-axis motion controller running G-code in the custom multi-material 3D printer (fabrication tool) | Normal + shear + temperature tactile sensor printed directly onto the fingertip of an anthropomorphic hand | T04 (T04.5) |\n\n## Reading list by track\n\n### T01 Autonomous Urban Services\nNo directly relevant paper found for Leadshine's hands or joint modules. Closest material:\n- [Precast-concrete surface robot (Sensors 2024)](https://doi.org/10.3390/s24113336) — coverage path planning around obstacles with A* dead-zone escape; the planner idea transfers to cleaning / maintenance coverage (T01.2, T01.6).\n- General background, not Leadshine-specific and does not mention Leadshine: [Dexterous Robotic Hands in the Foundation-Model Era: A Full-Stack Survey (arXiv 2611.06724, Ji Pei, Nov 2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06724) — orientation on data collection, retargeting and evaluation if you put a DH-series hand on a service robot.\n\n### T02 Resilient & Safe City\nNo directly relevant paper found.\n\n### T03 Sustainable City\n- [Salt-marsh carbon-flux monitoring system (Sensors 2026)](https://doi.org/10.3390/s26102966) — full stack for unattended environmental sensing: sensor node, stepper-driven mast, cloud dashboard, data-fusion; a template for T03.2 / T03.7.\n- [FGA-Corn precision application (Front. Plant Sci. 2025)](https://doi.org/10.3389/fpls.2025.1571228) — small detector → decision rule → stepper actuation; the same pattern fits sorting or targeted dosing demos (T03.1, T03.6).\n- [Precast-concrete surface robot (Sensors 2024)](https://doi.org/10.3390/s24113336) — stepper-only 5-axis machine with brake motors for power-off safety; reference for a custom mechanism in construction / infrastructure (T03.4).\n\n### T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation\n- [Robot arm-assisted acupuncture system (Front. Bioeng. Biotechnol. 2025)](https://doi.org/10.3389/fbioe.2025.1563263) — how to bolt a small actuator to an arm flange and validate motion and force against a human therapist; method transfers to any care-procedure demo (T04.2, T04.6).\n- [In vitro joint testing system (Bioengineering 2023)](https://doi.org/10.3390/bioengineering10091006) — force-profile tracking with a Leadshine motion card + stepper; useful for rehab-training force targets (T04.3).\n- [Upright radiotherapy platform (Medical Physics 2025)](https://doi.org/10.1002/mp.17523) — patient-carrying rotation stage; safety margins, load assumptions and accuracy tests for moving a seated person (T04.4).\n- [3D-printed multi-directional tactile sensor (Commun. Eng. 2023)](https://doi.org/10.1038/s44172-023-00131-x) — what normal / shear fingertip sensing enables on an anthropomorphic hand; context for the tactile DH116-M and DH2116 (T04.5).\n- [Dexterous-hand survey (arXiv 2611.06724)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06724) — general background for hand-over and assistive manipulation; does not mention Leadshine.\n\n## White papers, patents and technical reports\n\n- **White papers / technical reports:** none found. The English blog has four selection guides (closed-loop stepper series, integrated vs. separate servo, servo sizing, stepper vs. servo for packaging) — practical, not research.\n- **Patents:** company statements only. The English technology page (dated) lists 24 invention patents, 45 utility-model patents, 89 design patents and 116 software copyrights; elsewhere the site cites about 500 patents. No individual dexterous-hand or joint-module patent was opened and verified in this pass. ⚠️ unverified at patent level.\n- **Standards / certifications:** ISO 9001 (since 2004), CE and TÜV on selected products; no evidence found of Leadshine authoring public technical standards.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Leadshine corporate overview (founder biography, history, engineering laboratory)](https://www.leadshine.com/abouts/about_corporate_overview.html)\n- [Leadshine technology and quality page (R&D share, patent counts, MIT origin)](https://www.leadshine.com/abouts/about_technology_quality.html)\n- [Leadshine English blog list](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvents/Blog.html)\n- [Leadshine store news list](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news)\n- [Leadshine Chinese site (DH116 developer entity)](https://www.leisai.com/)\n- [DH2116 series product page (SDK, OS and language support)](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [DH116S user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH2116 user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH2116_Series_Dexterous_Hand_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS 2 example project description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [Eastmoney F10 management data for 002979 (Li Weiping résumé, board)](https://emweb.securities.eastmoney.com/PC_HSF10/CompanyManagement/PageAjax?code=SZ002979)\n- [Eastmoney F10 company survey for 002979 (legal name, employees)](https://emweb.securities.eastmoney.com/PC_HSF10/CompanySurvey/PageAjax?code=SZ002979)\n- [Crossref record: On the Adaptive Control of Robot Manipulators](https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1177/027836498700600303)\n- [Crossref record: Adaptive manipulator control: A case study](https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1109/9.14411)\n- [Crossref record: Composite adaptive control of robot manipulators](https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1016/0005-1098%2889%2990094-0)\n- [Open Library: Applied nonlinear control](https://openlibrary.org/works/OL4465059W)\n- [arXiv listing search for \"Leadshine\" (no results)](https://arxiv.org/search/?query=Leadshine&searchtype=all)\n- [arXiv 2611.06724 — dexterous-hand survey](https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06724)\n- [GitHub repository listing for \"leadshine\"](https://github.com/search?q=leadshine&type=repositories)\n- [GitHub: openvmp/stepper_driver_em2rs](https://github.com/openvmp/stepper_driver_em2rs)\n- [GitHub: marcoreps/linuxcnc_leadshine_EL8](https://github.com/marcoreps/linuxcnc_leadshine_EL8)\n- [Europe PMC full text: PMC13211138 (salt-marsh carbon flux, Sensors 2026)](https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC13211138)\n- [Europe PMC full text: PMC12860710 (acupuncture twisting parameters, Biomed J)](https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12860710)\n- [Europe PMC full text: PMC12364822 (robot arm-assisted acupuncture)](https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12364822)\n- [Europe PMC full text: PMC12279808 (FGA-Corn)](https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC12279808)\n- [Europe PMC full text: PMC11788243 (upright radiotherapy platform)](https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11788243)\n- [Europe PMC full text: PMC11784860 (bistable fabric mechanism)](https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11784860)\n- [Europe PMC full text: PMC11174809 (precast-concrete robot)](https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC11174809)\n- [Europe PMC full text: PMC10525521 (in vitro joint testing system)](https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC10525521)\n- [Europe PMC full text: PMC10956052 (3D-printed tactile sensor)](https://europepmc.org/article/PMC/PMC10956052)\n","Leadshine-Products":"---\ntype: products\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, products]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. — Product Portfolio\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](Leadshine.md) · [Developer guide](Leadshine-Developer-Guide.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](Leadshine-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md) · [Solutions and projects](Leadshine-Solutions-and-Projects.md)\n\n> **Read this first.** Leadshine (雷赛智能, SZSE 002979) sells **parts, not robots**: dexterous hands, joint actuators, frameless and coreless motors, plus a large industrial motion-control catalogue. For a 48-hour build only the **hands** (SDK + ROS 2 + URDF) are plug-and-code; everything else needs a fieldbus master and mechanical work.\n> The hands and their software are developed by Leadshine's hand unit **Leadtron / 灵巧驱控** (the manuals name \"Shenzhen Lingqiao Drive and Control Technology Co., Ltd.\" and \"Shenzhen Leadtron Technology Co., Ltd.\"); ownership share ⚠️ unverified.\n> Prices are **USD list / sale prices from Leadshine's own web store as captured on 2026-11-14** — they change often.\n\n## Lineup map\n\nTrack relevance: ● strong · ◐ partial · ○ none. Status: ✅ shipping · 🧪 announced / shown · 🔭 planned.\n\n| Product | Category | Status | Launched | Price (if public) | Positioning (one line) | T01 | T02 | T03 | T04 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| [DH116 series](#dh116-series) | Five-finger dexterous hand, 11 DoF | ✅ | DH series announced Mar 2025; DH116 = 2nd-generation volume model | **USD 2,199** (no sensors) · **USD 2,799** (tactile -M) | Robust, strong workhorse hand: 545 g, 30 kg hook load | ● | ◐ | ◐ | ● |\n| [DH116S series](#dh116s-series) | Compact five-finger hand, 11 DoF | ✅ | Manual first issued Dec 2025; presented as newly launched at WAIC, Jul 2026 | **USD 2,299** (CAN FD or RS485, no sensors) | Ultra-thin 359 g hand for small / mid-size humanoids, education, research | ● | ◐ | ◐ | ● |\n| [DH2116 series](#dh2116-series) | High-DoF hand, 21 DoF (16 active) | ✅ | 2025–2026 (successor of the 20-DoF DH2015 shown May 2025) | **USD 12,999** | Flagship research hand with finger abduction; fine manipulation, not heavy loads | ● | ◐ | ◐ | ● |\n| [PJ3 series](#pj3-series) | Planetary integrated joint actuator | ✅ | 2025–2026 (store listings from May 2026) | **USD 339–499** | Low-cost back-drivable joint for legs, small arms, exoskeleton-type joints | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ |\n| [HJ2 series](#hj2-series) | Harmonic integrated joint actuator | ✅ | 2025–2026 (store listings from Apr 2026) | **USD 599–929** | Precise hollow-shaft joint for arms / cobot-style mechanisms | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ |\n| [FM2 series (FM family)](#fm2-series-fm-family) | Frameless torque motor | ✅ | 2025 (FM1 earlier) | **USD 79–239** | Motor kit for people who design their own joints | ○ | ○ | ◐ | ○ |\n| [CD / CDH ring servo drives](#cd--cdh-ring-servo-drives) | Hollow ring-shaped joint drive | ✅ (Chinese site only) | 2025 | not public | Drive board that sits inside a custom joint; EtherCAT + STO | ○ | ○ | ○ | ○ |\n| [RJ1 / RJH joint modules](#rj1--rjh-collaborative-robot-joint-modules) | Cobot joint module (harmonic) | ✅ (Chinese site only) | 2025 | not public | Bigger joints for collaborative arms, up to 762 N·m peak | ○ | ◐ | ○ | ○ |\n| [iW series](#iw-series) | Wheel module / low-voltage integrated wheel drive | 🧪 | Shown Jun–Jul 2026 | not public | Drive wheel for AGV / AMR bases; no product page yet | ◐ | ◐ | ◐ | ○ |\n| [Coreless motors, micro servo drives, encoders](#coreless-motors-micro-servo-drives-and-encoders) | Hand / small-joint actuation components | ✅ | First shown Nov 2023 (SPS) | not public | The motor + drive + encoder inside the hands, sold separately | ○ | ○ | ○ | ◐ |\n| [EL7 / EL8 / EL8S AC servo + ELM motors](#el7--el8--el8s-ac-servo-drives-and-elm-motors) | Mains-voltage servo systems | ✅ | EL7 / EL8 current generation; EL8S shown 2026 | via distributors | Factory-grade servo axes, 50 W–7.5 kW | ○ | ○ | ○ | ○ |\n| [ELD3 / ELD3 MINI / 2ELD2 low-voltage servo](#eld3--eld3-mini--2eld2-low-voltage-servo-drives) | DC servo drives and integrated servo motors | ✅ | current | via distributors | 24–60 V servo for mobile robots and compact machines | ◐ | ○ | ◐ | ◐ |\n| [Stepper and closed-loop stepper drives](#stepper-and-closed-loop-stepper-drives) | Stepper drives | ✅ | current | via distributors | Cheap positioning axes; EtherCAT and EtherNet/IP versions | ○ | ○ | ◐ | ○ |\n| [Integrated motors](#integrated-motors) | Motor + drive + encoder in one | ✅ | current (ISS1 shown 2026) | via distributors | One-cable axis for conveyors, sorters, simple mechanisms | ◐ | ○ | ◐ | ○ |\n| [PLCs, remote I/O, HMIs](#plcs-remote-io-and-hmis) | Controllers | ✅ | current | via distributors | EtherCAT motion PLCs from 6 to 256 axes | ○ | ○ | ○ | ○ |\n\n**How to read the track columns:** hands are rated ● for T01 / T04 because a five-finger hand on a host arm or humanoid is directly usable for service tasks (T01.3, T01.7) and for assistance, hand-over and rehab-mirroring demos (T04.1, T04.3, T04.5). For T02 / T03 they are a supporting end-effector (T02.1 valve / switch / debris handling, T03.1 / T03.6 sorting). Joint modules and motors only matter if a team builds its own mechanism.\n\n## Products\n\n### DH116 series\n\n![DH116 series](assets/leadshine-dh116.jpg)\n\nSecond-generation, volume-produced five-finger hand. Brushless coreless servo motors with worm-gear transmission (self-locking), FOC current loop and hybrid force / position control. The strongest and most widely demonstrated Leadshine hand — the most likely loan unit on site (best guess).\n\n| Spec | Value (source: user manual unless noted) |\n|---|---|\n| Degrees of freedom | **11 total, 6 active** |\n| Active joint ranges | Thumb side swing 0–60° · thumb flexion 0–30° · index / middle / ring / little flexion 0–80° each |\n| Weight | **545 g ± 10 g** |\n| Load | Hook grip **≤ 30 kg** · single finger max **5 kg** (product page) |\n| Lateral (side) grip | **≤ 50 N** (product page: \"exceeds 50 N\") |\n| Open / close time | 0.5 s (manual lists \"≥ 0.5 s\") |\n| Repeatability | ± 0.2 mm |\n| Power | **12–48 V DC @ 2.5 A** |\n| Interfaces | CAN FD (DH116), EtherCAT 100 Mbit (DH116EC), RS485 (DH116RS — store listing) |\n| Connector | 8-pin cable: VDC, GND, EtherCAT RX± / TX±, CANH, CANL |\n| Tactile sensing (-M models) | \"Multimodal\" tactile sensors, **6 as standard**: normal force, tangential force and its direction, proximity; soft-landing behaviour |\n| Lifetime | **1 million** grasp cycles (vendor claim) |\n| Environment | −10 to 40 °C, 85 % RH in the spec table; the usage notes say operate at **0–40 °C**. Not for dusty / oily / wet use; no IP rating stated |\n| Software | Windows 10+, Linux, ROS 2; C++ and Python; x86 and ARM |\n| Warranty | 12 months |\n\n**Variants (what each unlocks)**\n\n| Model | Bus | Tactile sensors | Hand | Store price (sale / list) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| DH116-L1 / DH116-R1 | CAN FD | none | left / right | USD 2,199 / 2,299 |\n| DH116EC-L1 / DH116EC-R1 | EtherCAT | none | left / right | USD 2,199 / 2,299 |\n| DH116RS-L1 / DH116RS-R1 | RS485 | none | left / right | USD 2,199 / 2,299 |\n| DH116-L1-M / DH116-R1-M | CAN FD | multimodal tactile | left / right | USD 2,799 / 2,999 |\n| DH116EC-L1-M / DH116EC-R1-M | EtherCAT | multimodal tactile | left / right | USD 2,799 / 2,999 |\n| DH116P | — | — | — | Enhanced variant named in the manual title and shown at CES 2026; no separate spec sheet found ⚠️ unverified |\n\n- **No EDU / developer edition exists.** Every variant uses the same SDK (\"LHandProLib\"), the same Windows GUI tool (\"LHandPro\") and the same URDF / MJCF. What changes is the bus and whether you get force feedback.\n- **Downloads listed in the manual:** GUI debug tool, SDK, URDF + MJCF, ROS 2 workspace (`lhandpro_ws_116_2026_02_10`), EtherCAT ESI file (`LSLQ_DH116.xml`), protocol spreadsheet (EtherCAT / CAN FD / RS485), STEP model.\n- **Version trap:** hands manufactured **before 10 Feb 2026** use an older SDK, URDF and protocol set (listed separately in the manual). Ask which generation you are holding.\n- **Typical applications:** carrying and handling (hook grip), tool and door-handle grasping, parcel hand-over, tactile pick-and-place, teleoperation targets.\n- Official pages: [DH116 series](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html) · [store listing DH116-R1](https://store.leadshine.com/products/dh116-r1-dexterous-hand) · [user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n\n### DH116S series\n\n![DH116S series](assets/leadshine-dh116s.jpg)\n\nUltra-thin \"small hand\" built for small and mid-size humanoids (the first-pass profile cites 1.1–1.4 m robots). Same 11-DoF / 6-active layout and worm-gear drive as DH116 in a much smaller, lighter body. Positioned by Leadshine for entertainment, display, **education and research**.\n\n| Spec | Value (source: user manual unless noted) |\n|---|---|\n| Degrees of freedom | **11 total, 6 active** |\n| Active joint ranges | Thumb lateral swing 0–91° · thumb flexion 0–59° · index / middle / ring 0–72° · little finger 0–74° |\n| Size | **147.54 × 73.53 × 26.35 mm** |\n| Weight | **359 g ± 10 g** (the first-pass profile quotes \"< 380 g\" from the store) |\n| Load | Hook grip max **15 kg** (manual and store); the product page says \"light-load handling of 10 kg\" — sources differ |\n| Five-finger side grip | **50 N** |\n| Min. open / close time | **0.4 s** |\n| Repeatability | ± 0.2 mm |\n| Power | **12–48 V DC** (−10 % / +10 %) · static 0.1 A · max 2.0 A |\n| Interfaces | CAN FD (DH116S), EtherCAT (DH116SEC), RS485 (DH116SRS) |\n| Connector | 6-pin: VDC ×2, GND ×2, CANH / 485+, CANL / 485− (CAN FD or RS485, choose one) |\n| Tactile options | none · **-S** standard piezoresistive · **-M** multimodal. Palm: normal force 0–25 N at 0.1 N resolution. Five fingers: normal force 0–20 N at 0.01 N resolution, tangential force at 0.01 N with direction 0–360° at 1° |\n| Lifetime | 1 million no-load cycles and 1 million full-load side-grip cycles; design life 3 years (vendor figures) |\n| Environment | Working −10 to 45 °C, 10–90 % RH · storage −10 to 70 °C · **IP30** |\n| Colours | Ceramic White, Obsidian Black, Cloud Pattern Silver, Black Jade Gray |\n| Software | Windows, Linux, ROS 2; C++ and Python; x86 and ARM; GUI tool; SDK and demos described as open-source |\n| Warranty | 12 months |\n\n**Variants**\n\n| Model family | Bus | Sensor suffix | Hand / colour | Store price (sale / list) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| DH116S-L1 / -R1 | CAN FD | none, -S, -M | left / right | USD 2,299 / 2,399 (no-sensor units: DH116S-LG1, -LW1, -RG1, -RW1) |\n| DH116SRS-L1 / -R1 | RS485 | none, -S, -M | left / right | USD 2,299 / 2,399 (no-sensor units: DH116SRS-LG1, -LW1, -RG1, -RW1) |\n| DH116SEC-L1 / -R1 | EtherCAT | none, -S, -M | left / right | not in the store capture |\n\n- In store part numbers the letter before the final \"1\" is the colour (**G** = gray, **W** = white).\n- **Optional accessories named in the manual:** KJS-1509 variable power supply (3–24 V @ 2.5 A), 0.5 m EtherCAT debug cable, **MINI USB-CANFD-I** USB-to-CAN-FD debugger, and **6248XL \"Super Sensing\" data gloves** for data acquisition / teleoperation.\n- **Downloads listed in the manual:** GUI tool, SDK + demo, URDF + MJCF, ROS 2 workspace (`lhandpro_ws_DH116S_2026_1_6`), CAN FD protocol, STEP model.\n- **Typical applications:** end-effector for small humanoids and light arms, gesture / HRI demos, glove teleoperation, education.\n- Official pages: [DH116S series](https://en.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116s-series.html) · [store listing DH116S-RG1](https://store.leadshine.com/products/dh116s-rg1-dexterous-hand) · [user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf) · [ROS 2 example description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n\n### DH2116 series\n\n![DH2116 series](assets/leadshine-dh2116.jpg)\n\nFlagship high-DoF hand: 16 brushless coreless motors plus 5 passive joints, trapezoidal-screw drive. Each finger has side-to-side (abduction) motion, so it can do in-hand and fine manipulation the 11-DoF hands cannot. Heavier, far more expensive and rated for much lower loads.\n\n| Spec | Value (source: user manual unless noted) |\n|---|---|\n| Degrees of freedom | **21 total: 16 active + 5 passive** |\n| Finger kinematics | A finger is driven by 3 motors controlling 4 joints: joint 1 (abduction) −20° to +20°; joints 2–4 flexion 0–90° (motor-commanded range about 0–75°); joint 4 follows joint 3. Motor commands 0–4095 |\n| Size | **218 × 119 × 80.5 mm** |\n| Weight | **850 g ± 10 g** |\n| Max recommended load | **5 kg** |\n| Five-finger grip force | **150 N** max in the manual; **94 N** max whole-hand grip on the product page and store — sources differ, treat 94 N as the safe figure |\n| Min. open / close time | 0.6 s |\n| Repeatability | ± 0.6 mm |\n| Power | **24–60 V DC** · quiescent 0.16 A, rated 1 A, max 5 A (all at 24 V) |\n| Interfaces | CAN 1 Mbps · CAN FD 1 / 5 Mbps · RS485 5 Mbps (no EtherCAT) |\n| Lifetime | 300,000 no-load cycles and 300,000 full-load side-grip cycles; design life 2 years (vendor figures) |\n| Colour | Ink Jade Gray |\n| Software | Windows, Linux, ROS 2 (claimed); C++ and Python; x86 and ARM; GUI tool; SDK and demos |\n| In the box | Hand (DH2116-L1 left or DH2116-R1 right), M9 communication debug cable, **PCAN-USB** USB-to-CAN module |\n\n- **Variants and price:** DH2116-L1, DH2116-R1 — **USD 12,999** sale / 14,999 list in the store.\n- **Downloads listed in the manual:** GUI tool, SDK, STEP, URDF (`DH2116_URDF_Files_2026_3_16`), protocol spreadsheet. The manual lists **no ROS 2 workspace and no MJCF** for DH2116 — confirm with staff before planning a ROS 2 pipeline around it.\n- **Typical applications:** dexterous-manipulation research, imitation-learning targets, fine object handling (T04 assistance, T01 service tasks).\n- Official pages: [DH2116 series](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html) · [store listing DH2116-R1](https://store.leadshine.com/products/dh2116-r1-dexterous-hand) · [user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH2116_Series_Dexterous_Hand_User_Manual.pdf)\n\n**Hand chooser**\n\n| Need | Pick |\n|---|---|\n| Carry or pull something heavy, robust demo | DH116 (30 kg hook grip) |\n| Light host robot or arm with small payload | DH116S (359 g) |\n| Force feedback / gentle grasp | DH116-…-M or DH116S-…-S / -M |\n| Finger abduction, in-hand manipulation | DH2116 |\n| Fastest bring-up on a laptop | CAN FD variant + USB-CAN-FD adapter + LHandPro GUI, then SDK |\n\n### PJ3 series\n\n![PJ3 series](assets/leadshine-pj3.jpg)\n\nPlanetary (\"quasi-direct-drive\" style) integrated joint: frameless brushless motor, planetary gearbox, encoder and drive in one housing. Gears are vacuum-nitrided; the drive uses GaN devices (lower noise and vibration, per vendor) and offers a monitoring port (for example temperature). Marketed for humanoid legs and arms, robot dogs, cobots and automation.\n\n| Model | Outer Ø | Bus | Rated torque | Peak torque | Output speed | Weight | Store price (sale / list) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PJ3-60CAN29 | 60 mm | CAN FD | **9.5 N·m** | 28 N·m | 200 rpm | 530 g | USD 339 / 349 |\n| PJ3-60EC29 | 60 mm | EtherCAT | 9.5 N·m | 28 N·m | 200 rpm | 530 g | USD 369 / 379 |\n| PJ3-75CAN42 | 75 mm | CAN FD | **14 N·m** | 42 N·m | 155 rpm | 800 g | USD 399 / 419 |\n| PJ3-75EC42 | 75 mm | EtherCAT | 14 N·m | 42 N·m | 155 rpm | 800 g | USD 439 / 459 |\n| PJ3-97CAN140 | 97 mm | CAN FD | **44 N·m** | 140 N·m | 130 rpm | 1,200 g | USD 489 / 509 |\n| PJ3-97EC140 | 97 mm | EtherCAT | 44 N·m | 140 N·m | 130 rpm | 1,200 g | USD 499 / 529 |\n\n- Values are from the store listings; supply voltage, encoder resolution and gear ratio are not stated there. (The older PJ2 generation on the Chinese site runs on 24 / 48 V with a 17-bit encoder — see [Legacy](#legacy-and-discontinued).)\n- **Developer access:** no public SDK, ROS 2 driver or protocol document found. EtherCAT versions should behave as CiA 402 drives ⚠️ unverified — ask staff for the object dictionary / CAN FD frame format.\n- **Typical applications:** leg and arm joints of small / mid humanoids, quadrupeds, pan-tilt units, assistive or exoskeleton-type joints (T04.3 / T04.4, custom mechanics only).\n- Official pages: [PJ3 series](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html) · [store collection](https://store.leadshine.com/collections/pj3-planetary-integrated-actuators) · [Chinese PJ module page](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1321.html)\n\n### HJ2 series\n\n![HJ2 series](assets/leadshine-hj2.jpg)\n\nHarmonic-drive integrated joint with hollow shaft: FM2 frameless motor, 101:1 harmonic reducer, **24-bit** encoder and drive in one package; optional holding brake. Vendor claims versus its earlier design: 65 % lighter, 20 % smaller, double torque density, about 30 % cheaper.\n\n| Model (NK = no brake, BK = brake) | Outer Ø × length | Hollow shaft | Rated torque | Peak torque | Peak output speed | Weight | Store price (sale / list) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HJ2-52H101D24-NK-EC | 52 × 56.6 mm | 10 mm | **5 N·m** | 11 N·m | 50 rpm | 0.29 kg | USD 599 / 609 |\n| HJ2-52H101D24-BK-EC | 52 × 74.1 mm | 10 mm | 6.5 N·m | 11 N·m | 59 rpm | 0.38 kg | USD 669 / 679 |\n| HJ2-70F101D24-NK-EC | 70 × 61.3 mm | 12 mm | **9.6 N·m** | 34 N·m | 64 rpm | 0.60 kg | USD 669 / 679 |\n| HJ2-70F101D24-BK-EC | 70 × 82.3 mm | 12 mm | 9.6 N·m | 34 N·m | 49 rpm | 0.80 kg | USD 729 / 749 |\n| HJ2-80F101D24-NK-EC | 80 × 68.2 mm | 17 mm | **30 N·m** | 66 N·m | 48 rpm | 0.96 kg | USD 729 / 749 |\n| HJ2-80F101D24-BK-EC | 80 × 84.2 mm | 17 mm | 30 N·m | 66 N·m | 41 rpm | 1.17 kg | USD 799 / 809 |\n| HJ2-90F101D24-NK-EC | 90 × 71.2 mm | 18 mm | **50 N·m** | 102 N·m | 50 rpm | 1.23 kg | USD 799 / 809 |\n| HJ2-90F101D24-BK-EC | 90 × 88.3 mm | 18 mm | 50 N·m | 102 N·m | 40 rpm | 1.60 kg | USD 859 / 889 |\n| HJ2-110F101D24-NK-EC | 110 × 80.2 mm | 25 mm | **84 N·m** | 194 N·m | 40 rpm | 1.98 kg | USD 859 / 889 |\n| HJ2-110F101D24-BK-EC | 110 × 97.6 mm | 25 mm | 84 N·m | 194 N·m | 36 rpm | 2.60 kg | USD 929 / 949 |\n\n- Common to all: **48 V** supply, gear ratio **101**, backlash ≤ 10 arcsec, 24-bit encoder, bus **EtherCAT / CAN / CAN FD** (Chinese spec table; store units are sold as EtherCAT). Rated torque is quoted at 2000 rpm motor speed. Store listings give near-identical weights (for example 326 g for the 52 mm no-brake unit).\n- **Developer access:** same caveat as PJ3 — no public SDK or ROS 2 driver found.\n- **Typical applications:** arm, wrist and waist joints, cobot-style manipulators, precise pan-tilt / camera gimbals.\n- Official pages: [HJ2 series](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/hj2-series.html) · [store listing HJ2-52](https://store.leadshine.com/products/hj2-52h101d24-nk-ec-harmonic-integrated-actuator) · [Chinese HJ module page](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1321.html)\n\n### FM2 series (FM family)\n\n![FM2 series (FM family)](assets/leadshine-fm2.jpg)\n\nFrameless torque motor kits (stator + rotor, no housing, bearings or encoder) for teams that design their own joints. All listed models are wound for **48 V DC**, 3-phase. FM2 is the current \"ultra-high-density\" generation; FM1 is the earlier one, still on the Chinese site.\n\n**Frame sizes — sources disagree:** English product page: five sizes 43 / 50 / 60 / 70 / 85 mm. Chinese product page: eight sizes 28 / 43 / 50 / 60 / 70 / 85 / 104 / 127 mm. Chinese spec table: 50, 60, 69, 85, 94 and 127 mm stator diameters. Automate 2026 release: six sizes, 50–127 mm. Customisation offered.\n\nModels sold in the web store (store figures):\n\n| Model | Stator Ø | Rated power | Rated / peak torque | Rated / max speed | Weight | Store price |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FM2-04308 | 43 mm | 77 W | 0.20 / 0.82 N·m | 3,700 / 5,500 rpm | 54 g | USD 89 |\n| FM2-05008 | 50 mm | 207 W | 0.36 / 1.26 N·m | 5,500 / 6,500 rpm | 92 g | USD 79 |\n| FM2-05014 | 50 mm | 211 W | 0.60 / 2.50 N·m | 3,360 / 3,970 rpm | 136 g | USD 109 |\n| FM2-06008 | 60 mm | 251 W | 0.48 / 1.43 N·m | 5,000 / 6,500 rpm | 135 g | USD 89 |\n| FM2-06011 | 60 mm | 259 W | 0.65 / 1.95 N·m | 3,800 / 5,000 rpm | 175 g | USD 109 |\n| FM2-07010 | 70 mm | 183 W | 0.70 / 3.00 N·m | 2,500 / 3,110 rpm | 211 g | USD 129 |\n| FM2-07013 | 70 mm | 272 W | 1.0 / 3.5 N·m | 2,600 / 4,600 rpm | 264 g | USD 149 |\n| FM2-07018 | 70 mm | 400 W | 1.40 / 4.90 N·m | 3,000 / 4,000 rpm | 330 g | USD 169 |\n| FM2-08513 | 85 mm | 471 W | 1.80 / 7.00 N·m | 2,500 / 3,000 rpm | 330 g | USD 179 |\n| FM2-08523 | 85 mm | 1,063 W | 2.90 / 10.80 N·m | 2,500 / 3,550 rpm | 500 g | USD 239 |\n\n- **Larger models (Chinese spec table only):** FM2-06026, FM2-09408 / 09413 / 09416 / 09426 (94 mm, up to 4 N·m rated / 10 N·m max) and FM2-12718 / 12725 (127 mm, up to **6.4 N·m rated / 19.2 N·m max**, 1.42 kg).\n- ⚠️ The store and the Chinese spec table give **different numbers for the same model** (for example FM2-08523: store 2.9 / 10.8 N·m and 500 g; Chinese table 2.86 / 7.8 N·m and 0.629 kg). Use the datasheet of the exact part number before sizing anything.\n- Vendor claims for FM2 versus FM1: about 10 % more torque at the same current, 20 % lower loss, lower temperature rise. Shipments of frameless motors exceeded 120,000 units in 2025 (company figure).\n- **Typical applications:** custom joint modules for humanoids and cobots, gimbals, direct-drive stages; needs a drive (CD / CDH or ELD3) and an encoder.\n- Official pages: [FM2 series](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/frameless-motors/fm2-series.html) · [store listing FM2-07013](https://store.leadshine.com/products/fm2-07013-frameless-motor) · [Chinese frameless motor page](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1325.html)\n\n### CD / CDH ring servo drives\n\nRing-shaped (\"hollow\") servo drive boards that mount inside a joint around the hollow shaft. Listed only on the Chinese site under robot core components; not in the English catalogue or web store.\n\n| Series | Models (outer Ø) | Rated / peak current | Supply | Key functions |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CD (cobot joints) | CD-EC4810-65 (Ø65), CD-EC4810-70 (Ø70), CD-EC4820-100 (Ø100), CD-EC4820-115 (Ø115); bore Ø15–20 mm | 10 / 30 A rms (65, 70) · 20 / 60 A rms (100, 115) | 48 V ± 10 % | EtherCAT in / out (CoE, DS402, FoE, **FSoE**), CAN, RS485, USB; **2-channel STO**; 2 absolute-encoder inputs (BiSS-C / RS485); optional 2 × 19-bit integrated encoders; torque-sensor port; brake output; PT1000 motor and gearbox temperature inputs |\n| CDH (humanoid joints) | CDH-EC4805-058 (Ø58), CDH-EC4810-066 (Ø66), CDH-EC4820-099 (Ø99); bore Ø15 mm | 5 / 15 A · 10 / 30 A · 20 / 60 A rms | 48 V ± 10 % | EtherCAT in / out (CoE, DS402, FoE, FSoE), CAN, RS485, USB; optional 2 × 19-bit integrated encoders; torque-sensor port; brake output; motor temperature input; STO only on the Ø58 model |\n\n- Supports dual-encoder full closed loop and open / closed-loop torque control with a torque sensor (vendor description).\n- Official page: [robot ring drives (Chinese)](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1324.html)\n\n### RJ1 / RJH collaborative-robot joint modules\n\nHarmonic joint modules aimed at collaborative arms and automation equipment (motor + harmonic reducer + encoder, RJ1 with drive on EtherCAT). Chinese site only.\n\n| Series | Sizes | Supply / bus | Peak torque range | Rated torque range | Weight range | Encoder |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RJ1 (\"economy\") | 14 / 17 / 20 / 25 / 32 (Ø70–142 mm) | 48 V · EtherCAT | 34 – **411 N·m** | 9.6 – 169 N·m | 1.3 – 6.6 kg | 19–20 bit |\n| RJH (\"high-voltage\") | 20 / 25 / 32 / 40 (Ø90–170 mm) | 220 V · RS485 | 102 – **762 N·m** | 50 – 363 N·m | 2.8 – 11.5 kg | 23 bit |\n\n- Too large and too slow to integrate for a hackathon build; listed for completeness.\n- Official page: [collaborative joint modules (Chinese)](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1320.html)\n\n### iW series\n\nWheel modules for mobile robots. Leadshine listed \"iW Series wheel modules\" among its WAIC 2026 exhibits (July 2026) and described a \"low-voltage integrated wheel solution\" for AGV / AMR at Automate 2026 (June 2026).\n\n- **No product page, datasheet, model list or price found** on the English site, the Chinese site or the web store — every spec is ⚠️ unverified. No picture saved.\n- Related shipping products for mobile bases: iSV2 integrated servo motors (24–60 V), LVM low-voltage servo motors (IP65), LD2 / 2LD2 CANopen drives — see [low-voltage servo](#eld3--eld3-mini--2eld2-low-voltage-servo-drives).\n- Sources: [WAIC 2026 recap](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html) · [Automate 2026 recap](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/automate-2026-concludes-successfully-%7C-leadshine-drives-global-industrial-automation-and-humanoid-robotics-innovation-with-core-motion-control-technologies.html)\n\n### Coreless motors, micro servo drives and encoders\n\n![Coreless motors, micro servo drives and encoders](assets/leadshine-coreless.jpg)\n\nThe small-actuator building blocks Leadshine uses inside its hands and sells to other hand and joint makers.\n\n| Item | What is confirmed |\n|---|---|\n| Coreless (hollow-cup) brushless motors | Chinese site lists three families: **EC** (standard), **ECH** (high-speed), **ECT** (high-torque). No public spec table. The \"coreless micro servo system\" first shown at SPS Nuremberg, Nov 2023, covered motor diameters **10–35 mm** |\n| Micro servo drive ELD3 MINI (Chinese name LD3M-EC) | EtherCAT micro low-voltage drive, described as smaller than a business card; **3.0 kHz** speed-loop bandwidth; direct brake drive; runs rotary, linear, voice-coil, coreless and brushed DC motors; supports full closed loop |\n| Kit encoders | Split magnetic: 17-bit (M17M, M17MP) and 23-bit (M23M). Split optical: 23-bit (B23M, B23MP) and 25-bit (B25M). Integrated optical types for 80 mm+ servo motors |\n\n- English product pages do not exist for the coreless motors; treat detailed specs as \"ask on site\".\n- Official pages: [coreless motors — high-speed type (Chinese)](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1381.html) · [LD3 Mini drive (Chinese)](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1006.html) · [SPS 2023 news](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/Leadshine%20coreless%20micro%20servo%20system%20made%20a%20shocking%20debut%20at%20the%202023%20SPS%20exhibition.html)\n\n### EL7 / EL8 / EL8S AC servo drives and ELM motors\n\n![EL7 / EL8 / EL8S AC servo drives and ELM motors](assets/leadshine-el8.jpg)\n\nMains-powered industrial servo systems. Not hackathon material (mains wiring, tuning software), listed for completeness.\n\n| Series | Confirmed facts |\n|---|---|\n| EL8 | Frequency response up to **3.5 kHz**; EtherCAT, Modbus RTU, pulse + direction, analogue; **STO SIL3** available; drives 400 W–2 kW; matching motors 50 W–2 kW with 23-bit magnetic / optical encoders; second-encoder input; motor auto-recognition |\n| EL8S | Higher-response update shown at Automate 2026; UL-certified with STO (company release) |\n| EL7 | EtherCAT, Modbus RTU / pulse, **Profinet**; 220 VAC range 400 W–2 kW; 400 VAC range **750 W–7.5 kW**; 23-bit encoders incl. battery-free type |\n| ELM1 / ELM2 | Matching AC servo motors |\n| Multi-axis | 2EL6 dual-axis and 4EL7 four-axis drives are listed in the English catalogue |\n\n- Official pages: [EL8 series](https://www.leadshine.com/products/servo/servo-drive/el8-series.html) · [EL7 series](https://www.leadshine.com/products/servo/servo-drive/el7-series.html)\n\n### ELD3 / ELD3 MINI / 2ELD2 low-voltage servo drives\n\n![ELD3 / ELD3 MINI / 2ELD2 low-voltage servo drives](assets/leadshine-eld3.jpg)\n\nDC-bus servo drives and matching low-voltage motors — the part of the industrial range closest to mobile robots.\n\n| Item | Confirmed facts |\n|---|---|\n| ELD3 | EtherCAT, CANopen, Modbus RTU, plus pulse and analogue control; full closed-loop control; **Safe Torque Off**; drives slotless motors, ELVM DC servo motors, brushed motors, hall-sensor motors, voice coils, linear motors |\n| ELD3 MINI | Compact high-power-density version (see coreless section) |\n| 2ELD2 | Dual-axis low-voltage drive with STO (CANopen and RS485 versions on the Chinese site as 2LD2) |\n| 4LD3-EC (Chinese site) | Four axes in one EtherCAT drive; any axis can run a low-voltage servo or a stepper; STO |\n| ELVM / LVM motors | Low-voltage servo motors; LVM: short body, magnetic encoder, **IP65** |\n| iSV2 integrated servo motors | **24–60 V DC**, up to 5,000 rpm, 17-bit absolute encoder; CANopen (iSV2-CAN) or Modbus RTU (iSV2-RS); optional brake |\n| Servo rollers (Chinese site) | Motorised conveyor rollers for cross-belt and narrow-belt sorters |\n\n- **Typical applications:** AGV / AMR traction, conveyors and sorters, compact gantries. CANopen / EtherCAT CiA 402 profiles make them drivable from generic masters.\n- Official pages: [ELD3 series](https://www.leadshine.com/products/servo/servo-drive/eld3-series.html) · [low-voltage servo tree (Chinese)](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1007.html)\n\n### Stepper and closed-loop stepper drives\n\n![Stepper and closed-loop stepper drives](assets/leadshine-stepper-drives.jpg)\n\nLeadshine's original business. Open-loop (DM, EM, DM5 five-phase) and closed-loop (CS, CS-M motors) lines with fieldbus versions.\n\n| Series | Confirmed facts |\n|---|---|\n| EM3E (open loop) / CS3E (closed loop) | EtherCAT; EM3E modes: Profile Position, Profile Velocity, Homing, Cyclic Synchronous Position; works with common EtherCAT masters (Beckhoff, Omron, Trio, Keyence named) |\n| 2CS3EIP / 2EM3EIP | Dual-axis **EtherNet/IP** drives, highlighted for North America at Automate 2026 |\n| 2EM3E / 2CS3E | Dual-axis EtherCAT |\n| EM2RS / CS2RS | Modbus RTU (RS485) |\n| DM, EM, EM1, DM5 | Pulse / direction drives (DM5 = five-phase) |\n| CM, 3S, CS-M | Stepper and closed-loop stepper motors |\n\n- Official page: [stepper drives](https://www.leadshine.com/products/stepper/stepper-drive.html)\n\n### Integrated motors\n\n![Integrated motors](assets/leadshine-integrated-motors.jpg)\n\nMotor, drive and encoder in one body — one power cable plus one bus cable per axis.\n\n| Family | Type | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| iEM / iEM-RS | Integrated stepper | pulse or Modbus RTU |\n| iCS / iCS-RS | Integrated closed-loop stepper | pulse or Modbus RTU |\n| iSV / iSV2-CAN / iSV2-RS | Integrated servo | iSV2: 24–60 V DC, CANopen or Modbus RTU |\n| ISS1 | Cost-optimised integrated stepper system | shown at Automate 2026; standard mounting dimensions |\n\n- Official page: [integrated motors](https://www.leadshine.com/products/integrated-motor.html)\n\n### PLCs, remote I/O and HMIs\n\n![PLCs, remote I/O and HMIs](assets/leadshine-plc.jpg)\n\n| Series | Confirmed facts |\n|---|---|\n| SCnU (small PLC) | Up to 12 pulse axes + 32 EtherCAT axes (Automate 2026 release) |\n| S series | Quad-core up to 1.6 GHz; 32 axes at 2 ms bus cycle, up to 64 axes; E-cam, G-code, interpolation; **OPC UA, MQTT, EtherNet/IP**, web visualisation; expands with up to 32 R3S I/O modules |\n| MC500 (mid-range) | 6 local pulse axes, up to 32 EtherCAT axes; IEC 61131-3 languages (ST, LD, SFC, CFC, FBD, IL); PLCopen motion library |\n| MC family | Up to 64 bus axes (Automate 2026 release) |\n| LC family (line controller) | Up to 256 synchronised axes |\n| R2 / R3 | Remote I/O modules |\n| LT2000 | HMI panels |\n| PC-based (Chinese site) | DMC / EMC motion-control cards, PAC1000 |\n\n- Official pages: [MC500 series](https://www.leadshine.com/products/control/plc/mc500-series.html) · [controls overview](https://www.leadshine.com/products/control.html)\n\n## Accessories and ecosystem\n\n| Item | What it is | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| **LHandPro** GUI tool | Windows host tool for jogging fingers, reading sensors, running preset gestures — first thing to run on a new hand | Download page linked from each hand manual |\n| **LHandProLib** SDK | C++ / Python library with demos for Windows and Linux (x86, ARM); described as open-source | Download page linked from each hand manual |\n| ROS 2 workspaces | `lhandpro_ws` for DH116 and DH116S (state publisher, interfaces, service node, sequence demos) | Zip archives linked from the manuals |\n| URDF + MJCF | Simulation models for DH116 / DH116S; URDF only for DH2116 | Download page linked from the manuals |\n| STEP models, EtherCAT ESI XML, protocol spreadsheets | Mechanical integration and bus bring-up | Linked from the manuals |\n| MINI USB-CANFD-I | USB-to-CAN-FD debugger (optional accessory for DH116S) | DH116S manual |\n| PCAN-USB + M9 cable | USB-to-CAN adapter and debug cable shipped with DH2116 | DH2116 manual |\n| KJS-1509 power supply | Variable 3–24 V @ 2.5 A bench supply (optional) | DH116S manual |\n| 6248XL data gloves | \"Super Sensing\" data gloves for data acquisition / teleoperation (optional; maker not stated) | DH116S manual |\n| Cables, LSP / SPS power supplies, PLF planetary gearboxes, training kit | Industrial accessories | [Accessories](https://www.leadshine.com/products/accessories.html) |\n| Tuning software, manuals, 3D models | Industrial drive tooling (Windows) | [Software downloads](https://www.leadshine.com/download/Software.html) |\n| Web store | Single-unit purchase of hands, PJ3, HJ2, FM2 (46 listings on 2026-11-14) | [store.leadshine.com](https://store.leadshine.com/collections/all) |\n\n**Not offered:** complete robots, arms, mobile bases, on-board computers, cameras, AI models, datasets, a public GitHub organisation.\n\n## Legacy and discontinued\n\n| Item | Status | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| DH2015 20-DoF hand | Superseded by DH2116 | Announced Apr 2025 for Automate 2025: 15 active + 5 passive DoF, 650 g, 15 kg hook payload, 1 million grip cycles, 26 preset gestures, tactile sensors standard |\n| First-generation DH hand | Superseded | DH116 is described as the second-generation volume version; no first-generation spec sheet found |\n| PJ2 planetary modules | Previous generation (still in the Chinese spec table) | PJ2-56 (36:1, 36 N·m peak, 24 V, 0.377 kg), PJ2-88 (25:1, 60 N·m, 48 V, 0.604 kg), PJ2-105 (18:1, 90 N·m, 48 V, 0.83 kg); CAN, 17-bit encoder |\n| FM1 frameless motors | Previous generation, still listed | 50 / 70 / 85 / 115 mm frames, eight models, up to 9.51 N·m rated / 28.5 N·m max (FM1-11550) |\n| EL6, ELD2, older DM / EM drives | Older industrial generations | English site keeps \"Legacy Products\" and \"Discontinued Products\" sections |\n\n## Image credits\n\n| File | Shows | Source URL |\n|---|---|---|\n| assets/leadshine-dh116.jpg | DH116 series banner | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/series/71273b454cd0d9277fd390c901a63dec_1777283240517.jpg |\n| assets/leadshine-dh116s.jpg | DH116S series banner | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/series/bc3dedf482a8ccddd46a6ba58abf65dc_1777283837299.jpg |\n| assets/leadshine-dh2116.jpg | DH2116 series banner | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/series/1ddc05caa952f74c35ce78d77f86fb85_1777283314247.jpg |\n| assets/leadshine-pj3.jpg | PJ3 series banner | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/series/26db8defa9ecd2ef254a0189999d77f7_1777283471176.jpg |\n| assets/leadshine-hj2.jpg | HJ2 series banner | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/series/be491e432e7b6532a52db73e2b23daf3_1777283400701.jpg |\n| assets/leadshine-fm2.jpg | FM2 series banner | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/series/ba95e65fa099c44043e25813c723df26_1777283028919.jpg |\n| assets/leadshine-coreless.jpg | Coreless brushless motors, five sizes | https://www.leisai.com/upload/image/2025/08/20/%E7%A9%BA%E5%BF%83%E6%9D%AF.png |\n| assets/leadshine-el8.jpg | Two EL8 AC servo drives (EtherCAT and pulse versions) | Leadshine official EL8 imagery; not saved in this pass, exact file URL not recorded ⚠️ — series page: https://www.leadshine.com/products/servo/servo-drive/el8-series.html |\n| assets/leadshine-eld3.jpg | ELD3 series banner | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/series/c5f3d645ebc6ae3adc0d3f1e26db6131_1782196861368.png |\n| assets/leadshine-stepper-drives.jpg | Stepper drive series image | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/series/cd810d3a4266a8d4d5027439e270d5f8_1761550720025.png |\n| assets/leadshine-integrated-motors.jpg | Integrated motors category image | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/category/70b9f16bf942fffeebbdc0ff59df5bcc_1744960552818.png |\n| assets/leadshine-plc.jpg | Controls / PLC category image | https://www.leadshine.com/upfiles/category/edddc64a08d189aee98fa968ebf7a572_1708845191928.png |\n\nTen of the eleven pictures saved before this pass were re-identified by file hash against the official site (all except the EL8 picture); `leadshine-coreless.jpg` was added in this pass. No official picture exists yet for the iW series.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Leadshine robot components overview](https://www.leadshine.com/robot/index.html)\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [DH116S series product page](https://en.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116s-series.html)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [HJ2 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/hj2-series.html)\n- [FM2 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/frameless-motors/fm2-series.html)\n- [DH116 / DH116P user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116S user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH2116 user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH2116_Series_Dexterous_Hand_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [Leadshine web store — all products (models and prices)](https://store.leadshine.com/collections/all)\n- [Store: PJ3 planetary integrated actuators](https://store.leadshine.com/collections/pj3-planetary-integrated-actuators)\n- [Leadshine China (雷赛智能) — DH series hand page, with full product menu](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1288.html)\n- [Leadshine China — frameless motors FM2 / FM1 (spec tables)](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1325.html)\n- [Leadshine China — humanoid joint modules PJ / HJ (spec tables)](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1321.html)\n- [Leadshine China — collaborative joint modules RJ1 / RJH](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1320.html)\n- [Leadshine China — robot ring drives CD / CDH](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1324.html)\n- [Leadshine China — coreless motors](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1381.html)\n- [Leadshine China — LD3 Mini micro servo drive](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1006.html)\n- [Leadshine China — LD2 low-voltage servo drives](https://www.leisai.com/product/product_details_1007.html)\n- [EL8 series servo drives](https://www.leadshine.com/products/servo/servo-drive/el8-series.html)\n- [EL7 series servo drives](https://www.leadshine.com/products/servo/servo-drive/el7-series.html)\n- [ELD3 series servo drives](https://www.leadshine.com/products/servo/servo-drive/eld3-series.html)\n- [EM3E series stepper drives](https://www.leadshine.com/products/stepper/stepper-drive/em3e-series.html)\n- [MC500 series PLC](https://www.leadshine.com/products/control/plc/mc500-series.html)\n- [S series PLC](https://www.leadshine.com/products/control/plc/s-series.html)\n- [Automate 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/automate-2026-concludes-successfully-%7C-leadshine-drives-global-industrial-automation-and-humanoid-robotics-innovation-with-core-motion-control-technologies.html)\n- [WAIC 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html)\n- [20-DoF dexterous hand at Automate 2025 (Leadshine news, Apr 2025)](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-debuts-20dof-dexterous-hand-at-automate-2025:-redefining-humanoid-robotics-precision.html)\n- [Coreless micro servo system at SPS 2023 (Leadshine news)](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/Leadshine%20coreless%20micro%20servo%20system%20made%20a%20shocking%20debut%20at%20the%202023%20SPS%20exhibition.html)\n- [Leadshine profile in this knowledge base (first-pass facts: launch dates, CES 2026, DH116P)](Leadshine.md)\n","Leadshine":"---\ntype: company\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nhq: \"Shenzhen, China\"\nfounded: 1997\nwebsite: https://www.leadshine.com\ncategories: [components, dexterous-hand]\non_site_guess: \"DH116 / DH116S / DH2116 dexterous hands plus PJ3 / HJ2 joint modules as demo and loan parts; no complete robot\"\nfit_t01: 3\nfit_t02: 2\nfit_t03: 2\nfit_t04: 3\nidentity_confidence: high\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, company]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\n\n> **One-liner:** Shenzhen-listed motion-control maker (steppers, servos, PLCs) that since 2025 also sells humanoid-robot building blocks: frameless motors, integrated joint actuators and five-finger dexterous hands.\n> **Why it matters at Leukerbad:** Leadshine does not bring a robot — it brings the **parts you bolt onto someone else's robot**. The dexterous hands (ROS 2 + C++/Python SDK, URDF/MJCF) are the only items a team can realistically program within 48 hours; the joint modules matter only if you build custom mechanics.\n\n## Snapshot\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| HQ | Shenzhen, Guangdong, China |\n| Founded | 1997 (company states \"over 27 years\" of experience) |\n| Founders / CEO | Founder and chairman Li Weiping (\"Dr. Li\" in the CES 2026 release) ⚠️ unverified full name/title in this research pass |\n| Size and funding | Public: Shenzhen Stock Exchange **002979**. H1 2026 revenue **CNY 1.247 bn (+39.9% YoY)**, net profit **CNY 194 m (+62.8%)**. Site states ~1,500 employees, 500 patents, 10,000 clients (figure may be dated) |\n| Core business | Stepper / closed-loop stepper / servo drives and motors, integrated motors, motion-control PLCs, remote I/O, HMIs; robot components (frameless motors, joint actuators, dexterous hands, wheel modules) |\n| Website | [leadshine.com](https://www.leadshine.com) · robot components: [leadshine.com/robot](https://www.leadshine.com/robot/index.html) · web store: [store.leadshine.com](https://store.leadshine.com/collections/all) |\n| Docs / developer portal | No dedicated developer portal. [Downloads](https://www.leadshine.com/downloads.html) (manuals, datasheets, 3D models, [software](https://www.leadshine.com/download/Software.html)); hand manuals and ROS 2 notes are PDFs on the store |\n| GitHub | No official GitHub org found. SDK (\"LHandProLib\"), ROS 2 package and URDF/MJCF are distributed as download archives linked from the user manuals |\n\n## Portfolio at a glance\n\nRobot-component lines first (most relevant here), then the industrial automation lines.\n\n| Product | Type | Status | Key specs | Price (if public) | Img key |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| DH116 series (incl. DH116EC EtherCAT, -M tactile variants; DH116P enhanced variant shown at CES 2026) | Five-finger dexterous hand | ✅ | **11 DoF**, 6 actively driven; **545 g** (±10 g); whole-hand load up to **30 kg**, single finger **5 kg**; lateral grip > 50 N; brushless coreless servo + worm gear; EtherCAT / CAN FD / RS485; rated 1 million grasp cycles; optional tactile sensors (6 standard on -M) | Store: **USD 2,199** (no sensors), **USD 2,799** (tactile -M) sale prices | leadshine-dh116 |\n| DH116S series | Compact \"ultra-thin\" five-finger hand | ✅ (launched WAIC, Jul 2026) | **11 DoF**, **6 active**; 147.54 × 73.53 × 26.35 mm; **< 380 g** (store figure); light-load handling 10 kg; EtherCAT / CAN FD / RS485; sized for 1.1–1.4 m robots; optional tactile on fingers and palm | not public (store lists it; price not captured) | leadshine-dh116s |\n| DH2116 series | High-DoF dexterous hand (flagship) | ✅ | **21 DoF: 16 active + 5 passive**; brushless coreless motors + precision screw drive; max whole-hand grip **94 N**; CAN 1 Mbps, CAN FD 1/5 Mbps, RS485 5 Mbps; SDK for Windows / Linux / ROS 2, C++ and Python | not public (store lists it; price not captured) | leadshine-dh2116 |\n| PJ3 series | Planetary integrated joint actuator (motor + gearbox + encoder + drive) | ✅ | Models by outer diameter: **PJ3-60** (9.5 N·m rated), **PJ3-75** (14 N·m rated), **PJ3-97** (44 N·m rated); CAN FD or EtherCAT versions; monitoring port (e.g. temperature); targets robot dogs, small/mid humanoids, exoskeletons | Store sale prices: **USD 329** (60 CAN), **USD 399** (75 CAN), **USD 429** (75 EtherCAT), **USD 479** (97 CAN) | leadshine-pj3 |\n| HJ2 series | Harmonic integrated joint actuator | ✅ | Sizes **52 / 70 / 80 / 90 / 110 mm**; built-in **24-bit** encoder; EtherCAT and CAN FD; uses FM2 frameless motor; torque values not on product page | not public | leadshine-hj2 |\n| FM2 series (FM family) | Frameless torque motor | ✅ | Five standard sizes **43 / 50 / 60 / 70 / 85 mm** on the product page (Automate 2026 release says six sizes, 50–127 mm — sources disagree); 10% higher torque density and 20% lower loss vs previous generation (vendor claim); one FM model cited at 7 N·m peak / 326 g | not public | leadshine-fm2 |\n| iW series | Wheel module / low-voltage integrated wheel drive for AGV/AMR | 🧪 shown at WAIC and Automate 2026; no product page found | not public | not public | leadshine-iw |\n| Coreless motors + micro servo drives, hollow encoders | Hand / small-joint actuation components | ✅ (per investor disclosures; no English product page) | not public | not public | leadshine-coreless |\n| EL7 / EL8 / EL8S AC servo drives + ELM motors | High-voltage AC servo systems | ✅ | EtherCAT (CoE, CiA 402), Modbus RTU / RS485, pulse and analog variants; STO on EL8-EC and UL-listed models; e.g. EL7-EC 750 W–2 kW models | via distributors | leadshine-el8 |\n| ELD3 / ELD3 MINI / 2ELD2 low-voltage servo drives | DC servo drives (single and dual axis) | ✅ | Low-voltage, compact; 2ELD2 dual-axis with STO; fieldbus variants (EtherCAT, CANopen, Modbus RTU exist across the low-voltage range) | via distributors | leadshine-eld3 |\n| DM5 / EM3E / CS3E / 2CS3EIP / 2EM3EIP stepper and closed-loop stepper drives | Stepper drives | ✅ | Pulse/direction plus EtherCAT (EM3E, CS3E) and EtherNet/IP (dual-axis 2CS3EIP, 2EM3EIP) | via distributors | leadshine-stepper-drives |\n| Integrated motors (ISS1 and integrated servo / closed-loop stepper) | Motor + drive + encoder in one unit | ✅ | Standard mounting dimensions; fieldbus options | via distributors | leadshine-integrated-motors |\n| SCnU / Sn / MC / LC motion-control PLCs, remote I/O, HMIs | Controllers | ✅ | SCnU: up to 12 pulse axes + 32 EtherCAT axes; MC: up to 64 bus axes; LC: up to 256 synchronized axes | via distributors | leadshine-plc |\n\n## What they will most likely bring to the hackathon\n\n**Best guess — not confirmed by Leadshine or the organizers.**\n\n- **Most likely: dexterous hands** (DH116, DH116S, DH2116). These are what Leadshine demonstrated live at CES, FAIR plus, WAIC and Automate in 2026, they are sold as single units in the web store, and they are the only Leadshine products with a ready SDK, ROS 2 package and URDF.\n- **Likely as display or loan parts: PJ3 planetary and HJ2 harmonic joint modules and FM2 frameless motors.** Useful only to teams building their own mechanism (gripper, pan-tilt, small arm, exoskeleton joint).\n- **Unlikely: industrial servo / stepper / PLC kits.** They need mains wiring, tuning software and a fieldbus master — poor fit for a 48-hour event.\n- **No complete robot.** Leadshine does not make humanoids, arms or quadrupeds. Expect its hands to be mounted on another vendor's arm or humanoid (Leadshine reports AGIBOT as a customer — AGIBOT gave it a supplier award).\n- Practical check on arrival: which hand variant (CAN FD / RS485 / EtherCAT), left or right, with or without tactile sensors, and what mechanical flange and power supply come with it.\n\n## Developer platform (summary)\n\n- **Hands — SDK:** \"LHandProLib\" SDK with demos, described by Leadshine as open-source; **C++ and Python**; Windows and Linux; x86 and ARM.\n- **Hands — ROS 2:** official example project for DH116S (\"LHandPro\") with packages for a state publisher, interfaces, a service node and C++ / Python sequence demos; RViz2 mirrors the physical hand. Third-party reseller docs state ROS 2 Humble on Ubuntu 22.04 ⚠️ unverified against the official PDF.\n- **Simulation assets:** **URDF and MJCF** files for the hands are linked from the user manuals. No Isaac / Gazebo scenes found.\n- **Low-level buses:** hands speak CAN FD, RS485 and (DH116EC, DH116S) EtherCAT; the CAN FD protocol document is hosted on Feishu, linked from the manual. Control is FOC current loop with hybrid force-position control inside the hand.\n- **GUI tool:** a visual \"upper-computer\" debugging tool (Windows) for jogging fingers and checking sensors — fastest way to verify hardware before coding.\n- **Version trap:** the DH116 manual marks downloads dated before **2026-02-10** as \"old version\"; make sure SDK, firmware and URDF come from the same generation.\n- **Joint modules (PJ3 / HJ2):** CAN FD or EtherCAT. No public SDK, ROS 2 driver or protocol document was found in this pass — plan on needing Leadshine staff on site for the protocol.\n- **Industrial drives:** EtherCAT drives follow **CiA 402 over CoE**, so any generic EtherCAT master (for example IgH or SOEM-based ROS 2 stacks) can in principle drive them; Modbus RTU and CANopen variants exist. Tuning is done with Leadshine's Windows software from the [download centre](https://www.leadshine.com/download/Software.html).\n- **Not offered:** no foundation model, no datasets, no teleoperation / data-collection stack, no on-board compute, no official GitHub org.\n\n## Current projects, deployments and customers\n\n- **2026-01 (CES, Las Vegas, 6–9 Jan):** showed coreless and frameless motors, joint modules and DH116 / DH116P hands; reported booth visits from Tesla Optimus, Boston Dynamics, Amazon and Neura executives and first-day hand pre-orders from North America, Europe and Asia. Visits are not supply contracts. ([PR Newswire Asia](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-achieves-great-success-at-ces-2026-entering-us-and-european-robotics-supply-chain-518892.shtml))\n- **2026-04/05 (FAIR plus, Shenzhen):** claims its components are in the supply chains of **80% of China's leading humanoid makers**; **> 120,000 frameless motors** delivered in 2025; AGIBOT named it an outstanding supplier partner. All company claims. ([Leadshine store blog](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news/leadshine-dexterous-hands-and-joint-modules-shine-at-the-shenzhen-fair-plus-2026))\n- **2026-06 (Automate, Chicago, 22–25 Jun):** full industrial range plus DH116 / DH2116, HJ2 / PJ3 and FM2; low-voltage integrated wheel solution for AGV/AMR. ([Leadshine news](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/automate-2026-concludes-successfully-%7C-leadshine-drives-global-industrial-automation-and-humanoid-robotics-innovation-with-core-motion-control-technologies.html))\n- **2026-07 (WAIC, Shanghai, 17–20 Jul):** launched **DH116S**; showed iW wheel modules; cites a supplier award from AGIBOT, a partner award from \"Xinghai Tu\" (Galaxea), and collaboration with Fourier and Tiangong. ([Leadshine news](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html))\n- **2026-08 (H1 report, via Chinese financial press):** joint modules and high-DoF hands in volume supply with orders from **nearly 100 robot customers**; frameless-motor orders in hand **> 1 million units**. ([Sina Finance](https://cj.sina.com.cn/articles/view/2311077472/89c03e6002002ndni))\n- **Track-relevant deployments:** none found. Leadshine is a component supplier; no public elderly-care, rescue, municipal or sustainability deployments under its own name. CES 2026 talks with exoskeleton, e-bike and medical-equipment prospects were reported but no deals named.\n- **Europe / Switzerland:** no specific office, distributor or project announcement found beyond CE / RoHS / REACH compliance and European pre-orders claimed at CES.\n\n## Roadmap and future direction\n\n- **Strategy (2026):** move from motion-control supplier to \"platform\" supplier of robot core components, focused on two categories — joint modules and dexterous hands; planetary modules prioritised for volume. ([10jqka investor note, 2026-07](https://stock.10jqka.com.cn/20260723/c678395454.shtml))\n- **Capacity:** frameless-motor design capacity to reach the million-unit level in 2026; automated capacity under construction quoted as 2 million units/year (May 2026) and **3 million units/year** (H1 2026 report).\n- **Hand line-up evolution:** DH series launched March 2025 (11 DoF, trial sales from Q2 2025) → DH2116 21-DoF flagship → DH116S compact hand (July 2026). No next model publicly announced.\n- **Overseas:** stated intent to deepen localisation in North America (Automate 2026); \"entering US and European robotics supply chain\" is a headline claim without named contracts.\n- **Finance:** H1 2026 revenue +39.9% YoY; brokerage forecast (not company guidance) of about CNY 2.8 bn revenue for 2026. No external funding round — listed company.\n- **Not public:** dated product roadmap, prices for HJ2 / FM2, any software or AI-model plans.\n\n## Challenge fit\n\n| Track | Fit 0–5 | Why (one line) | Best products | Go deeper |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| T01 Autonomous Urban Services | 3 | A dexterous hand upgrades another vendor's arm or mobile manipulator for handling doors, parcels, tools; Leadshine supplies no mobility or autonomy | DH116 / DH116S, DH2116, iW wheel module (if on site) | [T01 brief](T01-Autonomous-Urban-Services/Leadshine-T01-1-Brief.md) |\n| T02 Resilient & Safe City | 2 | Supporting role: robust high-load hand (30 kg whole-hand) for valve / switch / debris manipulation on a host robot | DH116, PJ3 | [T02 brief](T02-Resilient-and-Safe-City/Leadshine-T02-1-Brief.md) |\n| T03 Sustainable City | 2 | Supporting role: grasping for waste sorting or efficient actuation in a custom mechanism; no domain product | DH116, PJ3, FM2 | [T03 brief](T03-Sustainable-City/Leadshine-T03-1-Brief.md) |\n| T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation | 3 | Human-like hand with tactile option suits assistive fetch / hand-over and hand-rehab mirroring demos; PJ3 is marketed for exoskeletons but has no public SDK | DH2116, DH116S, DH116-M (tactile), PJ3 | [T04 brief](T04-Elderly-Care-and-Rehabilitation/Leadshine-T04-1-Brief.md) |\n\n**Decision hint:** choose Leadshine as an *add-on*, not as your platform. Pair a hand with an arm or humanoid from another vendor (and, for T04, with EMG capture or AR teleoperation from other vendors on site). Budget half a day for mechanical mounting, power and bus bring-up.\n\n## Related docs\n- [Product portfolio](Leadshine-Products.md) · [Developer guide](Leadshine-Developer-Guide.md) · [Roadmap and strategy](Leadshine-Roadmap-and-Strategy.md)\n- [Start here — all companies and tracks](../../00-START-HERE.md)\n\n## Sources\n- [Leadshine official site (home, product categories, company figures)](https://www.leadshine.com)\n- [Leadshine robot components overview](https://www.leadshine.com/robot/index.html)\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [DH116S series product page](https://en.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116s-series.html)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [HJ2 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/hj2-series.html)\n- [FM2 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/frameless-motors/fm2-series.html)\n- [Leadshine web store — all products](https://store.leadshine.com/collections/all)\n- [Store: DH116-R1 dexterous hand (price)](https://store.leadshine.com/products/dh116-r1-dexterous-hand)\n- [Store: PJ3 planetary integrated actuators (prices)](https://store.leadshine.com/collections/pj3-planetary-integrated-actuators)\n- [DH116 / DH116P user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS2 example project description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [DH116S user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH2116 user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH2116_Series_Dexterous_Hand_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [Leadshine at CES 2026 — supply-chain release (PR Newswire Asia)](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-achieves-great-success-at-ces-2026-entering-us-and-european-robotics-supply-chain-518892.shtml)\n- [Leadshine dexterous hands and frameless motors at CES 2026 (PR Newswire Asia)](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-s-dexterous-hands-and-frameless-motors-shine-at-ces-2026-519017.shtml)\n- [FAIR plus 2026 recap (Leadshine store blog)](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news/leadshine-dexterous-hands-and-joint-modules-shine-at-the-shenzhen-fair-plus-2026)\n- [Automate 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/automate-2026-concludes-successfully-%7C-leadshine-drives-global-industrial-automation-and-humanoid-robotics-innovation-with-core-motion-control-technologies.html)\n- [WAIC 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html)\n- [H1 2026 results coverage (Sina Finance)](https://cj.sina.com.cn/articles/view/2311077472/89c03e6002002ndni)\n- [Investor research note, July 2026 (10jqka)](https://stock.10jqka.com.cn/20260723/c678395454.shtml)\n- [DH series launch, March 2025 (Futu news)](https://news.futunn.com/en/post/54351384/china-leadshine-technology-002979-released-the-dh-series-dexterous-hand)\n- [EL7-EC2000Z product page (EtherCAT servo drive)](https://www.leadshine.com/product-detail/EL7-EC2000Z.html)\n","Leadshine-T01-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T01\nchallenge_title: \"Autonomous Urban Services\"\nfit: 3\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, brief]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T01 Autonomous Urban Services — Brief\n\n**Fit: 3/5** — An add-on, not a platform: a Leadshine dexterous hand gives another vendor's arm or mobile manipulator the \"open the door, press the button, hand over the parcel\" skill; Leadshine supplies no mobility, navigation or autonomy.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **DH116** five-finger hand (11 DoF, 6 active, **545 g**, **30 kg** whole-hand load, CAN FD / RS485 / EtherCAT, C++/Python SDK + ROS 2 example). **DH116S** (< 380 g) if the host arm has little payload. |\n| Strongest proof point | No service-robot deployment under Leadshine's name. Closest: its DC servo drives are positioned for **AGV & AMR** on its logistics solution page (undated, no named customers) and a low-voltage integrated wheel solution / **iW** wheel module was shown at Automate (Jun 2026) and WAIC (Jul 2026) — no specs published. |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Hand on a host arm or wheeled dual-arm robot **presses a lift button, opens a lever door handle and hands a parcel to a person** — the \"last 10 metres\" of indoor delivery. |\n| Biggest risk | Integration: flange adapter, power and CAN FD bring-up on a host robot you do not control. Whether hands are on site is a **best guess, not confirmed**. |\n| Pairs well with | Galaxea R1, AGIBOT G1/G2, Galbot G1 (mobile hosts) · Flexiv, ROKAE, Dobot (arm hosts) · Unitree (mobile base / humanoid) · D-Robotics RDK (ARM compute) · XREAL (teleop view) |\n\n## Why\n- Indoor delivery fails at doors, lifts and hand-over; a five-finger hand addresses exactly that gap.\n- Hands are the only Leadshine products with a usable stack: **LHandProLib** SDK (C++/Python, Windows/Linux, x86/ARM), ROS 2 example, URDF/MJCF.\n- DH116 is robust for its size (rated 1 million grasp cycles, 5 kg per finger) and priced at **USD 2,199** (store), so teams can be less timid with it.\n- AGV/AMR drive know-how is real (24–70 VDC ELD3 servo drives, iW wheel module) but not hackathon-programmable.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams needing a robot that navigates or delivers out of the box.\n- Outdoor, wet or snowy work: no IP rating is published for the hands.\n- Building a custom drivetrain in 48 hours: PJ3 / HJ2 / iW have no public SDK or protocol document.\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Products and solutions](Leadshine-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](Leadshine-T01-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T01-Autonomous-Urban-Services/T01-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","Leadshine-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T01\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, products]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T01 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Brief](Leadshine-T01-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](Leadshine-T01-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Read this first:** Leadshine sells components. For T01 it contributes **manipulation at the end of someone else's arm** (hands) and, on paper, **drivetrain parts** (wheel module, low-voltage servo drives, joint actuators). Only the hands come with an SDK a team can use within 48 hours. What is physically on site is a best guess — confirm at the booth.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### DH116 series\n\n![DH116 series](../assets/leadshine-dh116.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the default choice. Sturdy general-purpose hand for the contact tasks that stop delivery and service robots: lift buttons, door handles, parcel and bag hand-over, picking up litter, holding a tool.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Degrees of freedom | **11** (6 actively driven) |\n| Weight | **545 g** (±10 g) |\n| Load | Whole hand up to **30 kg**; single finger up to **5 kg** |\n| Lateral grip | > 50 N |\n| Drive | Brushless coreless motor + worm gear; FOC current loop, hybrid force-position control |\n| Interfaces | EtherCAT (DH116EC), CAN FD, RS485 |\n| Tactile option | -M variants: 6 sensors (fingertips and palm); normal force, tangential force and direction, proximity |\n| Rated life | 1 million grasp cycles |\n| Open/close speed, supply voltage, IP rating, temperature range | Not on the product page — check the user manual / ask on site |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping. Store price **USD 2,199** (no sensors) / **USD 2,799** (tactile -M). An enhanced DH116P variant was shown at CES 2026.\n- **Developer control:** per-finger position, speed and force / current targets and state read-back through the LHandProLib SDK (C++ / Python); tactile read-out on -M variants. A Windows GUI jogs fingers for hardware checks. ⚠️ unverified: the exact API surface was not read in this pass (manual PDF could not be parsed).\n\n### DH116S series\n\n![DH116S series](../assets/leadshine-dh116s.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the light option when the host arm or small humanoid has little spare payload. Same 11-DoF / 6-active layout in a thinner body.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Degrees of freedom | **11** (6 active) |\n| Size | 147.54 × 73.53 × 26.35 mm |\n| Weight | **< 380 g** (store figure) |\n| Load | \"Light-load handling\" up to **10 kg** |\n| Interfaces | EtherCAT, CAN FD, RS485 |\n| Tactile option | Configurable on five fingers and palm |\n| Software | Windows, Linux, ROS 2; x86 and ARM; C++ and Python |\n| IP rating, voltage, speed | Not public on the product page |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ launched at WAIC, July 2026. Price not captured (listed in the store).\n- **Developer control:** same SDK family, plus the official **LHandPro ROS 2 example** (state publisher, interfaces, service node, C++ / Python sequence demos; RViz2 mirrors the real hand). ROS 2 Humble on Ubuntu 22.04 ⚠️ unverified.\n- **Note:** Leadshine's own page positions it for entertainment, display, education and research — not heavy service work.\n\n### DH2116 series\n\n![DH2116 series](../assets/leadshine-dh2116.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** only if the task truly needs in-hand dexterity (operating a spray trigger, turning a key, handling a card). More joints means more bring-up time and less grip force than DH116.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Degrees of freedom | **21** (16 active + 5 passive) |\n| Max whole-hand grip | **94 N** |\n| Drive | Brushless coreless motors + precision screw drive |\n| Interfaces | CAN 1 Mbps, CAN FD 1 / 5 Mbps, RS485 5 Mbps (no EtherCAT listed) |\n| Software | Windows, Linux, ROS 2; x86 and ARM; C++ and Python; SDK and demos |\n| Weight, load, IP rating | Not public on the product page |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping; price not captured.\n\n### PJ3 series\n\n![PJ3 series](../assets/leadshine-pj3.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** a building block for a custom mechanism on a mobile base — tilting cargo bed, parcel-box lid, pan-tilt sensor head. Not a drivetrain you can finish in 48 hours without vendor help.\n\n| Model | Rated torque | Bus options |\n|---|---|---|\n| PJ3-60 | **9.5 N·m** | CAN FD or EtherCAT |\n| PJ3-75 | **14 N·m** | CAN FD or EtherCAT |\n| PJ3-97 | **44 N·m** | CAN FD or EtherCAT |\n\n- Integrates motor, planetary gearbox, encoder and drive; expansion port for monitoring (e.g. temperature rise). Product page lists material handling among suitable tasks.\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping. Store prices **USD 329–479** ⚠️ unverified (from search snippets, not a direct page read).\n- **Developer control:** **no public SDK, ROS 2 driver or protocol document found.** Usable only if Leadshine staff hand over the CAN FD protocol or a library on site.\n\n### iW series\n\n![iW series](../assets/leadshine-iw.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the one Leadshine product aimed squarely at wheeled service robots — an integrated low-voltage wheel drive for AGV / AMR.\n\n- **Status:** 🧪 shown at WAIC (Jul 2026); Automate (Jun 2026) recap mentions a \"low-voltage integrated wheel solution\" for rapid AGV / AMR deployment. **No product page, specs, price or SDK are public.**\n- Treat as a conversation topic with the engineers, not as a build option, unless units and a protocol are on the table.\n\n### ELD3 / ELD3 MINI / 2ELD2 low-voltage servo drives\n\n![ELD3 / ELD3 MINI / 2ELD2 low-voltage servo drives](../assets/leadshine-eld3.jpg)\n\n**Role in T01:** the battery-voltage servo drives Leadshine recommends for AGV / AMR traction. Relevant for product thinking after the hackathon; unlikely to be on site.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Supply | **24–70 VDC** |\n| Power range | **50–750 W** |\n| Fieldbus | EtherCAT, CANopen, Modbus RTU (plus pulse and analog variants) |\n| Safety | Safe Torque Off |\n| Variants | ELD3 MINI (compact), 2ELD2 (dual-axis with STO — one drive for a differential base) |\n\n- **Status / price:** ✅ shipping via distributors; prices not public.\n- **Developer control:** standard drive profiles over the fieldbus (CiA 402 on EtherCAT), so a generic master can command velocity / position. Needs motors, wiring and Windows tuning software — too slow for a 48-hour build.\n\n## What Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. already does in this domain\n\n**Short answer: nothing under its own name in urban service robotics.** No delivery, cleaning, concierge or municipal deployment, pilot or demo was found.\n\n| When | What | Relevance to T01 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Undated (page assets suggest ~2022 — inference) | [Logistics industry solution page](https://www.leadshine.com/solution/logistics-industry.html) recommends DC servo drives for **AGV & AMR**, multi-shuttle vehicles and cross-belt sorters, and EL7 servos for steerable-wheel sorters. No named customers or figures. | Shows drivetrain supply into warehouse mobile robots — the industrial cousin of urban delivery. |\n| Jun 2026 | [Automate 2026](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/automate-2026-concludes-successfully-%7C-leadshine-drives-global-industrial-automation-and-humanoid-robotics-innovation-with-core-motion-control-technologies.html): 2ELD2 and ELD3 MINI low-voltage drives and a low-voltage integrated wheel solution for AGV / AMR. | Component offer for mobile bases. |\n| Jul 2026 | [WAIC 2026](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html): iW wheel modules shown beside PJ and HJ actuators; DH116S launched. | Wheel module exists as hardware; no data. |\n| 2026 (company claims) | Hands and joint modules supplied to \"multiple robotics companies\"; AGIBOT supplier award (see [profile](../Leadshine.md)). | Its parts may already sit inside service-capable robots from other vendors on site — worth asking which. |\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Stated strategy (2026):** become a platform supplier of robot core components, centred on joint modules and dexterous hands, with planetary modules prioritised for volume.\n- **Wheeled-robot components:** the iW wheel module and \"low-voltage integrated wheel solution\" appeared at two 2026 shows without a product page. **Inference:** a formal AGV / AMR wheel-drive product line is being prepared; no date announced.\n- **Hands:** DH116 → DH2116 → DH116S (Jul 2026). No next model, service-robot variant or weather-sealed version announced.\n- **Not public:** any autonomy software, navigation stack, AI model or service-robot reference design. **Inference:** none is planned — Leadshine stays below the software layer.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What it gives you | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| LHandProLib SDK | C++ / Python control of the hands; Windows and Linux; x86 and ARM (so it can run on an ARM edge board) | Download archive linked from the hand user manuals (no GitHub org) |\n| LHandPro ROS 2 example (DH116S) | State publisher, interfaces, service node, sequence demos, RViz2 mirror — a ready ROS 2 node to call from a task planner | PDF description on the store + archive |\n| URDF / MJCF hand models | Attach the hand to a host arm's model for motion planning and simulation | Linked from the user manuals |\n| Windows debugging GUI | Verify wiring, jog fingers, read sensors before writing code | Leadshine downloads |\n| CAN FD protocol document | Raw bus control if the SDK does not fit | Feishu link in the manual |\n\n- **Version trap:** the DH116 manual marks downloads dated before **2026-02-10** as old — keep SDK, firmware and URDF from the same generation.\n- **Not offered:** navigation, perception, grasp-planning models, datasets, teleoperation stack, on-board compute. Bring these from the host robot's vendor or from open-source (Nav2, MoveIt 2, open grasp-pose and VLA models).\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No mobility, no autonomy, no sensors beyond optional tactile.** Leadshine alone cannot produce a T01 demo.\n- **Weather:** no IP rating or operating-temperature range is published for any hand. Assume **indoor only**; Leukerbad in November means snow and wet surfaces.\n- **Integration cost:** mechanical flange, power feed and a CAN FD / RS485 / EtherCAT link to a host arm are on you. Budget about half a day.\n- **Six active motors (DH116 / DH116S):** enough for power, pinch and button-press grasps; not for fine in-hand manipulation.\n- **Drivetrain parts are not self-serve:** PJ3, HJ2 and iW have no public SDK or protocol; industrial drives need tuning tools and wiring.\n- **No T01 reference case:** judges will not see a proven Leadshine deployment behind your project.\n- **Research limits of this note:** web search was unavailable in this pass; findings rest on Leadshine's own pages and the company profile. The hand manuals (PDF) could not be parsed, so API details, supply voltage and speeds are unconfirmed.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Leadshine company profile (this vault) — specs, prices and developer-stack summary](../Leadshine.md)\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [DH116S series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116s-series.html)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [ELD3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/products/servo/servo-drive/eld3-series.html)\n- [Leadshine robot components overview](https://www.leadshine.com/robot/index.html)\n- [Logistics industry solution page](https://www.leadshine.com/solution/logistics-industry.html)\n- [Automate 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/automate-2026-concludes-successfully-%7C-leadshine-drives-global-industrial-automation-and-humanoid-robotics-innovation-with-core-motion-control-technologies.html)\n- [WAIC 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html)\n- [Leadshine web store — DH116-R1 dexterous hand (price)](https://store.leadshine.com/products/dh116-r1-dexterous-hand)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS 2 example project description (PDF; referenced, not parsed in this pass)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n","Leadshine-T01-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T01\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t01, playbook]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T01 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Brief](Leadshine-T01-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](Leadshine-T01-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Ground rules for every concept below**\n- Leadshine is the **end-effector or a sub-mechanism**; the robot, navigation and compute come from another vendor.\n- **Hour 0 gate:** confirm with Leadshine staff which hand variant is available (bus type, left / right, tactile or not), its flange, its power supply and the bus adapter. If no hand can be lent, drop Leadshine for this track.\n- Keep the hand **indoors** — no IP rating is published.\n- Hand API details below (per-finger position / force targets, ROS 2 service node) follow Leadshine's public description; verify against the SDK you are handed.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Hand-over station for hotel and spa deliveries\n\n- **Problem → value:** delivery robots carry items but cannot load themselves or hand things to guests. A fixed arm with a five-finger hand picks mixed items (towel roll, bottle, key-card sleeve, small parcel) and loads a courier robot or hands them to a person — removing the staff step at a hotel or thermal-bath reception.\n- **Hardware:** **DH116** (or DH116S) on a fixed cobot — Dobot CR, ROKAE xMate or Flexiv Rizon. Optional: a Unitree Go2 or other mobile robot with a basket as the courier. One RGB-D camera.\n- **Architecture / stack:** camera → object detection + a small table of pre-set grasp types per item class (power grasp, pinch, tripod) → arm motion via the arm vendor's SDK or MoveIt 2 → hand grasp via LHandProLib (Python) or the LHandPro ROS 2 service node. Compute on a laptop; orders entered through a simple web form.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: mount hand, power up, jog fingers in the Windows GUI, run the SDK demo from Linux.\n  - H6–24: four grasp presets working on four item types; arm pick-and-place to a fixed drop pose.\n  - H24–40: order queue, hand-over to a person (release on pull detected through finger current or tactile, if available), courier robot docking position.\n  - H40–48: reliability runs, fallback video, pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** guest orders \"towel + water\" on a phone → arm picks both with different grasps → loads courier basket → courier leaves → second order is handed directly to a judge.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** flange mismatch (3D-print an adapter plate in H0–6) · release-on-pull unreliable (fall back to a timed release or a button) · no courier robot (hand-over to a person only).\n- **Judging angles:** modest innovation; solid technical depth if grasp selection is perception-driven; clear real-world impact for hotels and clinics short of staff; Leukerbad relevance through the spa / hotel setting.\n\n### Concept 2 — The last 10 metres: lifts, doors and room hand-over\n\n- **Problem → value:** indoor delivery robots normally need lift and door retrofits (network modules, automatic openers). A mobile manipulator with a human-like hand uses the building as it is: presses the lift button, pushes down a lever handle, knocks, hands over the parcel. Value: delivery in old alpine hotels and clinics **without building retrofits**.\n- **Hardware:** **DH116** on a wheeled dual-arm robot (Galaxea R1 family, AGIBOT G1 / G2 or Galbot G1) or on a Unitree G1 humanoid arm. Host robot's cameras and navigation.\n- **Architecture / stack:** host navigation to pre-mapped waypoints → camera detects button panel / handle (fiducial marker as a safe fallback) → arm approach via host SDK → hand: index-finger extension pose for buttons, hook grasp for lever handles, power grasp for the parcel, all via LHandProLib / ROS 2 → simple state machine (py_trees or SMACH) on the host computer or a laptop.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: hardware gate, hand mounted and commanded from the host's computer; pick the exact door and lift panel (or build a mock panel).\n  - H6–24: button press and lever-handle skills as stand-alone routines with marker-based alignment.\n  - H24–40: chain navigate → press → wait → enter → door → hand-over; replace markers with learned detection only if time allows.\n  - H40–48: 10 consecutive runs, record backup video, pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** robot receives \"room 12\" → drives to the mock lift panel, presses the call button → drives to a door, opens it by the handle → offers the parcel; judge takes it and the hand releases.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** door too heavy for the host arm (use a light mock door) · finger alignment on small buttons (use a larger mock panel; compliance from force-position control helps) · no access to the host robot's arm API (fall back to Concept 1).\n- **Judging angles:** strong real-world story (retrofit-free delivery), good technical depth (navigation + manipulation chain), high Leukerbad relevance (older buildings, clinics, many elderly guests who cannot come down to reception).\n\n### Concept 3 — Public-space upkeep with human tools\n\n- **Problem → value:** maintenance robots are usually single-purpose machines. A hand that uses **human tools** — spray bottle, cloth, litter of any shape, dispenser refills — lets one robot disinfect handrails, wipe tables and pick up litter in a spa lobby or station hall.\n- **Hardware:** **DH2116** (16 active DoF, for trigger pulling) or **DH116-M** with tactile sensors, on a mobile manipulator or a cobot on a cart. RGB-D camera. Optional XREAL glasses for a teleoperated first pass and operator view.\n- **Architecture / stack:** camera → surface / litter detection → task planner picks tool and grasp → arm wiping trajectory (force-controlled arm such as Flexiv Rizon or ROKAE xMate makes wiping easy) → hand: hold bottle with three fingers, pull trigger with index finger; pinch or power grasp for litter. Optional: a hosted vision-language model to choose the grasp type from an image. Compute on a laptop.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: hardware gate; test whether the hand can actually pull the spray trigger — decide DH2116 vs DH116 immediately.\n  - H6–24: three skills in isolation: spray, wipe, pick litter.\n  - H24–40: detection-driven sequencing; tactile or current feedback to confirm a grasp.\n  - H40–48: timed runs, video, pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** robot approaches a handrail section → sprays → swaps to cloth and wipes → spots a cup and a crumpled bag on the floor → picks both with different grasps → drops them in a bin.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** trigger force or geometry beyond the hand (use a pump-top bottle pressed with the palm) · liquid near an unsealed hand (use water, spray away from the hand, cover with a glove only if Leadshine agrees) · DH2116 bring-up takes too long (DH116 with litter picking and wiping only).\n- **Judging angles:** highest innovation of the hand concepts (tool use), strong technical depth, hygiene relevance for thermal baths and rehabilitation clinics.\n\n### Concept 4 — PJ3-powered self-unloading cargo module\n\n- **Problem → value:** a quadruped or wheeled base can reach a doorstep over steps and slopes but cannot drop its load without a person. A tilting cargo bed or lidded parcel box driven by one or two **PJ3** joint actuators makes any mobile base a self-unloading courier for steep village streets.\n- **Hardware:** **PJ3-60 or PJ3-75** (9.5 / 14 N·m rated) + printed or laser-cut tray, on a Unitree Go2 / B2 or another vendor's base. Battery or host power rail, CAN FD adapter.\n- **Architecture / stack:** host navigation to drop point → trigger over ROS 2 topic → small node sends position / velocity commands to the PJ3 over CAN FD → tilt, confirm unload (current or a simple switch), return. Compute on the host or a D-Robotics RDK board.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: **hard gate — obtain the PJ3 CAN FD protocol or a library from Leadshine staff and spin one actuator.** No protocol by H6 means abandon this concept.\n  - H6–24: build the tray mechanism, closed-loop tilt with soft limits.\n  - H24–40: mount on the base, integrate with navigation, tune for payload.\n  - H40–48: outdoor-style ramp test, video, pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** parcel loaded → robot walks a ramp or steps to a marked doorstep → bed tilts and parcel slides into a drop box → robot reports delivery and returns.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** no public SDK (highest risk) · supply voltage mismatch with the host rail (ask before designing) · mechanical build eats the schedule (fallback: a hobby servo for the lid and keep PJ3 as a stated upgrade path — which weakens the Leadshine story).\n- **Judging angles:** hardware creativity and alpine relevance are high; software depth is lower; risk is the highest of the four.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| DH116 + Galaxea R1 / AGIBOT G1-G2 / Galbot G1 | Mobile manipulation for lifts, doors, hand-over (Concept 2). Leadshine reports AGIBOT as a customer, so ask whether a mounted example exists. | High — flange, power, bus, host arm API |\n| DH116 or DH116S + Dobot CR / ROKAE xMate / Flexiv Rizon | Fixed hand-over or tool-use station (Concepts 1, 3); force-controlled arms simplify wiping and button pressing | Medium — adapter plate + separate hand bus to a laptop |\n| DH116S + Unitree G1 humanoid | Lighter hand on a humanoid arm for concierge-style demos | High — payload and wrist interface must be checked first |\n| Hand + D-Robotics RDK board | SDK supports ARM, so the hand node can run on the robot instead of a tethered laptop | Low to medium — ⚠️ unverified on that specific board |\n| Hand + XREAL glasses | Operator view / teleoperated fallback when autonomy fails in the demo | Medium — you write the mapping |\n| Hand vs WUJI Hand (20 DoF) | Alternative on site: more dexterity, different stack. Choose Leadshine for load capacity and price, WUJI for fine manipulation | n/a — pick one |\n| PJ3 + Unitree Go2 / B2 | Self-unloading cargo module (Concept 4) | High — depends on protocol access |\n| Hand-equipped robot + SKYLANDX scan | Fast 3D map of the venue for navigation to doors and lifts | Low for the map, host-dependent for use |\n\n## Questions to ask the Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. engineers on site\n\n1. Which hands can teams borrow — DH116, DH116S or DH2116; CAN FD, RS485 or EtherCAT; left or right; with tactile sensors?\n2. What supply voltage and peak current does the hand need, and do you provide the power supply and the USB-to-CAN FD adapter?\n3. What is the mounting flange pattern, and do you have adapter plates for any arm or humanoid at this event?\n4. Which ROS 2 distribution is the LHandPro package built for, and does it cover DH116 and DH2116 or only DH116S?\n5. Can the SDK command fingertip force directly, and what is the control and feedback rate over CAN FD?\n6. Is there a splash or dust rating, and what is the minimum operating temperature? Can the hand wear a glove?\n7. For PJ3 / iW: can you share the CAN FD protocol or a Python library today, and what voltage do they run on?\n8. Is any robot at this event already fitted with Leadshine hands or joint modules?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. for T01 if** you already have access to an arm or mobile manipulator from another vendor, your idea hinges on a contact task (button, handle, hand-over, tool use), and a hand plus adapter is confirmed available in the first hour.\n- **Avoid if** you need mobility or autonomy out of the box, your task is outdoors in snow or wet, or you have nobody on the team comfortable with mechanical mounting and CAN bus bring-up.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Leadshine company profile (this vault) — specs, prices and developer-stack summary](../Leadshine.md)\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [DH116S series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116s-series.html)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [ELD3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/products/servo/servo-drive/eld3-series.html)\n- [Logistics industry solution page](https://www.leadshine.com/solution/logistics-industry.html)\n- [Automate 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://www.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/automate-2026-concludes-successfully-%7C-leadshine-drives-global-industrial-automation-and-humanoid-robotics-innovation-with-core-motion-control-technologies.html)\n- [WAIC 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html)\n","Leadshine-T02-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T02\nchallenge_title: \"Resilient & Safe City\"\nfit: 2\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, brief]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T02 Resilient & Safe City — Brief\n\n**Fit: 2/5** — Supporting role only: Leadshine supplies a strong, programmable hand (and joint modules) for someone else's robot; it has no mobility, sensing, autonomy or safety-domain product of its own.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **DH116 five-finger hand** (11 DoF, 6 active, **545 g**, hook-grip load **≤ 30 kg**, worm-gear self-locking, EtherCAT or CAN FD) mounted on another vendor's arm, humanoid or quadruped. PJ3 joint modules only for custom mechanisms. |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | **None in this domain.** Closest: live hand and joint-module demos at CES (Jan 2026), FAIR plus (Apr/May 2026) and WAIC (Jul 2026) — generic handling, not rescue or inspection. |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | **Remote \"hands in the hazard zone\"**: DH116 on a cobot arm, teleoperated from a safe distance to turn a valve, flip a breaker and pull a lever, with finger current / tactile feedback on the operator screen. |\n| Biggest risk | **Not ruggedised**: manual gives 0–40 °C operation and warns against dust, humidity and oil; no IP rating published. Plus half a day for flange, power (12–48 V) and bus bring-up. |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | Flexiv / ROKAE / Dobot (arm), Unitree / AGIBOT (mobile base or humanoid), XREAL (operator view), OriginFlow (EMG hand control), D-Robotics (edge compute), SKYLANDX (3D map of the scene). |\n\n## Why\n- T02 tasks often end in a **manipulation step built for human hands** (valves, switches, door handles, tools) — a five-finger hand covers these without custom grippers.\n- **Worm-gear drive is self-locking**: the hand keeps its grip without drawing holding current — useful for carrying or holding a load during a response task.\n- **Developer access is real**: LHandProLib SDK (C++ / Python), ROS 2 Humble example workspace, URDF / MJCF, Windows jog tool.\n- Tactile variant (-M) reports normal force, tangential force and proximity — usable as contact feedback for a remote operator.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams that need a **complete platform** (patrol, mapping, search, autonomy) — Leadshine has none.\n- Outdoor snow, wet or dusty demos with the hand exposed.\n- Anything depending on PJ3 / HJ2 joint modules without Leadshine staff support — no public SDK or protocol found.\n\n## Sources\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html) · [DH116 user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf) · [DH116S ROS 2 example note (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf) · [FAIR plus 2026 recap](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news/leadshine-dexterous-hands-and-joint-modules-shine-at-the-shenzhen-fair-plus-2026) · [WAIC 2026 recap](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html) · [company profile](../Leadshine.md)\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Products and solutions](Leadshine-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](Leadshine-T02-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T02-Resilient-and-Safe-City/T02-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","Leadshine-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T02\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, products]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T02 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Brief](Leadshine-T02-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](Leadshine-T02-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Bottom line:** Leadshine is a component supplier. For T02 it contributes the **manipulation end** of a response or inspection robot (a hand) and, for teams building mechanics, actuators. Everything else — mobility, sensing, mapping, autonomy, ruggedisation — must come from another vendor.\n\n> Research note: general web search was unavailable in this pass; findings come from Leadshine's official product pages, store PDFs and news pages plus the company profile. Absence of T02 deployments means \"none found on official pages\", not a proven negative.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### DH116 series\n![DH116 series](../assets/leadshine-dh116.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** human-style end-effector for a host arm, humanoid or mobile manipulator, to operate things built for people — valves, breakers, levers, door handles, hand tools — in a zone a responder should not enter. The strongest and best-documented Leadshine hand.\n\n| Spec | Value | Why it matters for T02 |\n|---|---|---|\n| DoF | **11 total, 6 active** (thumb swing 0–60°, thumb flexion 0–30°, four fingers 0–80° each) | Enough for power, hook, pinch and lateral grasps; no finger abduction |\n| Weight | **545 g ± 10 g** | Fits small cobot and humanoid wrist payloads |\n| Hook-grip load | **≤ 30 kg** whole hand; 5 kg per finger (product page) | Carry or drag a bag, hose or handle |\n| Lateral grip | about **50 N** (page: \"> 50 N\"; manual: \"≤ 50 N\") | Key-style grasps, switches |\n| Drive | Brushless coreless servo + worm gear, **self-locking** | Holds a grip without holding current; do not back-drive by force |\n| Open / close time | about **0.5 s** (manual lists \"≥ 0.5 s\") | Fast enough for live teleoperation |\n| Repeatability | **± 0.2 mm** | Preset grasps are reproducible |\n| Power | **12–48 V, 2.5 A** | Runs off a robot battery rail or a bench supply |\n| Environment | Manual table: −10 to 40 °C, 85% RH; precautions section: **0–40 °C**, not for dusty, humid, oily or corrosive settings | **Indoor demo only** — no IP rating published |\n| Bus | **CAN FD** (DH116-L1 / R1) or **EtherCAT** (DH116EC-L1 / R1) | Pick the variant your host computer can talk to |\n| Tactile (-M variants) | 6 sensors; normal force, tangential force and direction, proximity | Contact feedback for a remote operator; soft-landing |\n| Life / warranty | 1 million grasp cycles (vendor rating); 12 months | — |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping. **Price:** store sale prices **USD 2,199** (no sensors) / **USD 2,799** (tactile -M) per the company profile. Availability on site: not confirmed.\n- **Developer interface:** LHandProLib SDK (C++ / Python; Windows 10+, Linux), ROS 2 example workspace, URDF + MJCF, EtherCAT XML (ESI) file, protocol spreadsheets for EtherCAT / CAN FD / RS485, STEP model, Windows jog tool (LHandPro). You command the six active joints (position, with force-position hybrid control inside the hand) and read joint state and tactile data. Exact API calls and feedback fields: check the SDK archive — ⚠️ unverified here.\n\n### DH116S series\n![DH116S series](../assets/leadshine-dh116s.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** lighter alternative when the host robot's wrist payload is tight (small humanoid or light arm). Lower load than DH116.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| DoF | 11 total, 6 active |\n| Size / weight | 147.54 × 73.53 × 26.35 mm; **< 380 g** (store figure, per profile) |\n| Load | light-load handling, **10 kg** class (per profile) |\n| Bus | EtherCAT / CAN FD / RS485 |\n| ROS 2 | Official example project: **Ubuntu 22.04 + ROS 2 Humble**, connects over an EtherCAT network interface |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ (launched WAIC, July 2026). **Price:** not public.\n- **Developer interface:** same LHandProLib stack as DH116; this is the hand the official ROS 2 walkthrough is written for.\n\n### DH2116 series\n![DH2116 series](../assets/leadshine-dh2116.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** only if the task needs fine in-hand dexterity (small connectors, tool re-grasp). Weaker and more complex — not the first choice for response tasks.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| DoF | **21: 16 active + 5 passive** |\n| Drive | Brushless coreless motors + screw drive |\n| Max whole-hand grip | **94 N** |\n| Bus | CAN 1 Mbps, CAN FD 1 / 5 Mbps, RS485 5 Mbps (no EtherCAT listed) |\n| Software | Windows / Linux / ROS 2, x86 and ARM, C++ / Python, debug GUI |\n| Weight, voltage, IP | not on the product page |\n\n- **Status:** ✅. **Price:** not public.\n\n### PJ3 series\n![PJ3 series](../assets/leadshine-pj3.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** actuator for a team-built mechanism — sensor mast pan-tilt, door-opener, small winch, tracked or legged prototype joint. Leadshine markets PJ3 for robot dogs, small and mid humanoids and exoskeletons.\n\n| Model | Rated torque | Bus | Store price (per profile, from search snippets ⚠️ unverified) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| PJ3-60 | **9.5 N·m** | CAN FD | USD 329 |\n| PJ3-75 | **14 N·m** | CAN FD / EtherCAT | USD 399 / 429 |\n| PJ3-97 | **44 N·m** | CAN FD | USD 479 |\n\n- Speed, weight, voltage, gear ratio, IP rating: **not on the product page**.\n- **Status:** ✅. **Developer interface:** CAN FD or EtherCAT, monitoring port (temperature rise). **No public SDK, ROS 2 driver or protocol document found** — usable in 48 hours only with Leadshine engineers handing over the protocol.\n\n### HJ2 series\n![HJ2 series](../assets/leadshine-hj2.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** precision joint for a custom arm or inspection gimbal. Sizes 52 / 70 / 80 / 90 / 110 mm, 24-bit encoder, EtherCAT and CAN FD. Torque, weight and IP: not public. Same developer-access gap as PJ3. Status ✅, price not public.\n\n### iW series\n![iW series](../assets/leadshine-iw.jpg)\n\n**Role in T02:** drive wheels for a custom patrol or inspection cart. 🧪 Shown at WAIC and Automate 2026 for AGV / AMR; **no specs, product page or developer information public**. Treat as unavailable unless it is physically on the Leadshine table.\n\n**Not ranked (poor 48-hour fit):** FM2 frameless motors, coreless motors, EL / ELD servo drives, stepper drives, integrated motors, PLCs — these need mechanical design, mains or drive wiring and tuning; no T02-specific advantage.\n\n## What Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing found under its own name.** No public emergency-response, inspection, security, hazard-monitoring or teleoperation deployments, pilots or application pages.\n\n| Date | What | Relevance to T02 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2026-01 | CES: hands, joint modules, motors shown; booth visits (not contracts) from large robot makers | Generic component showcase ([release](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-achieves-great-success-at-ces-2026-entering-us-and-european-robotics-supply-chain-518892.shtml), via profile) |\n| 2026-04/05 | FAIR plus Shenzhen: hand demo zone; PJ3 positioned for robot dogs, small / mid humanoids, exoskeletons, e-scooters; iW for AGVs and wheeled robots | Indirect: quadrupeds and wheeled robots are the usual inspection / patrol platforms, but no such customer or use case is named ([blog](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news/leadshine-dexterous-hands-and-joint-modules-shine-at-the-shenzhen-fair-plus-2026)) |\n| 2026-07 | WAIC: DH116S launch; iW wheel modules; work with Fourier and Tiangong on humanoid components | No safety or inspection scenario mentioned ([news](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html)) |\n\n- Its parts may sit inside other makers' quadrupeds or humanoids used for patrol or inspection, but no specific robot or deployment is publicly attributed. Do not claim one.\n- Core industrial business (servo / stepper / PLC) keeps factory machines running; that is not city infrastructure resilience in the T02 sense.\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Stated direction (company):** become a platform supplier of robot core parts — joint modules and dexterous hands — with frameless-motor capacity scaling to the million-unit level and 2 million units/year automated capacity under construction.\n- **Stated (WAIC 2026):** continued humanoid-component development with Fourier and Tiangong.\n- **Nothing announced for T02 specifically:** no ruggedised or IP-rated hand, no teleoperation kit, no inspection or rescue program.\n- **Inference (not a company statement):** as PJ3 goes into robot dogs and iW into wheeled robots, Leadshine parts will increasingly end up in patrol and inspection platforms built by its customers — as an invisible supplier, not as a solution owner.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What you get | T02 use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **LHandProLib SDK** | C++ / Python library (`libLHandProLib.so` on Linux), demos | Direct joint control from your teleop or autonomy node |\n| **ROS 2 example workspace** (`lhandpro_ws`) | Packages: `lhandpro_description` (URDF, meshes, RViz launch), `lhandpro_interfaces` (service definitions), `lhandpro_service` (control node), `sequence_demo_cpp`, `sequence_demo_py`; state publisher feeds `/joint_states` | Start from `sequence_demo_py` and replace the fixed sequence with operator commands |\n| **URDF + MJCF** | Hand models for RViz and MuJoCo | Rehearse a valve or switch grasp in simulation; live digital twin of the hand for the operator |\n| **LHandPro GUI (Windows)** | Jog fingers, check sensors | Hardware check in the first hour |\n| **Protocol sheets / ESI XML** | EtherCAT, CAN FD, RS485 maps | Fallback if the SDK does not fit your compute board |\n\n- Setup facts from the official ROS 2 note: Ubuntu 22.04, ROS 2 Humble, `colcon build`, copy the `.so` to `/usr/local/lib`, grant the service node raw-socket capability, set the **EtherCAT network interface name in code and rebuild**.\n- Distribution: download archives linked from the PDF manuals (some on Feishu) — **get them on a USB stick from Leadshine staff**; venue internet may not reach them quickly. Use resources dated on or after **2026-02-10** for current DH116 units.\n- **Not available from Leadshine:** teleoperation / retargeting software, perception, policies or foundation models, datasets, simulation scenes. Bring your own (for example open-source hand tracking and retargeting tools) — not Leadshine products.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No platform:** no mobility, sensors, compute, mapping or autonomy. Leadshine alone cannot produce a T02 demo.\n- **No domain track record:** zero public safety, rescue or inspection references.\n- **Environment:** 0–40 °C, no dust / humidity / oil, no IP rating — unsuitable for outdoor November conditions at 1,400 m without protection.\n- **Self-locking fingers:** good for holding, but a collision cannot be absorbed by back-driving; plan approach paths and use force limits.\n- **6 active joints** on DH116 / DH116S: no independent fingertip dexterity; tool use is limited to grasps you pre-shape.\n- **Integration cost:** flange adapter to the host wrist, 12–48 V supply, EtherCAT or CAN FD adapter — budget half a day.\n- **Joint modules:** specs incomplete on public pages and no public software — high risk for 48 hours.\n- **No teleoperation stack and no hand-arm coordination** — you write the mapping from operator input to six joint targets.\n\n## Sources\n- [Leadshine company profile (internal, general research)](../Leadshine.md)\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [DH116 / DH116P user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS2 example project description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [FAIR plus 2026 recap (Leadshine store blog)](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news/leadshine-dexterous-hands-and-joint-modules-shine-at-the-shenzhen-fair-plus-2026)\n- [WAIC 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html)\n- [Leadshine at CES 2026 (PR Newswire Asia; cited via the company profile)](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-achieves-great-success-at-ces-2026-entering-us-and-european-robotics-supply-chain-518892.shtml)\n","Leadshine-T02-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T02\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t02, playbook]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T02 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Brief](Leadshine-T02-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](Leadshine-T02-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Read this first:** Leadshine is an **add-on** for T02 (fit 2/5). Every concept below needs a host robot or arm from another vendor. Secure that host **before** committing. What Leadshine brings on site is not confirmed — check the table for hand variant (CAN FD or EtherCAT, left or right, tactile or not), flange and power supply.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Remote hands for the hazard zone (fixed station)\n- **Problem → value:** after a gas alarm, flood or fire in a technical room (pump room of a thermal bath, cable-car machine room, tunnel service niche) someone must close a valve or trip a breaker. A teleoperated hand does it; the responder stays outside.\n- **Hardware:** Leadshine **DH116** (EtherCAT or CAN FD; tactile -M if available) on a cobot arm from Flexiv, ROKAE or Dobot; webcam or depth camera at the scene; laptop. Mock \"infrastructure panel\": ball valve, rocker breaker, lever, door handle.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: scene camera stream to operator; optional hand `/joint_states` twin in RViz2.\n  - Planning: operator-in-the-loop. Arm moved by gamepad or pre-taught waypoints (host arm's SDK); hand driven by **grasp presets** (hook, power, lateral pinch) first, then continuous control from webcam hand tracking mapped to the six active joints.\n  - Control: `lhandpro_service` (ROS 2 Humble, Ubuntu 22.04) or LHandProLib Python directly. Tactile / current values shown as a contact bar on the operator UI.\n  - Compute: one laptop; operator UI on a second machine over Wi-Fi to show real separation.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: get SDK archive from Leadshine staff; jog the hand in the Windows GUI; run `sequence_demo_py`; mount on arm (flange adapter).\n  - H6–24: grasp presets as ROS 2 services; arm waypoints for each panel element; first full valve turn by teleop.\n  - H24–40: operator UI (video + contact bar + preset buttons); hand-tracking input; link-loss behaviour (hand holds grip — self-locking — arm stops).\n  - H40–48: rehearse, time the run, record a backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** 0:00 alarm scenario on screen → 0:30 operator at a separate desk approaches panel → 1:00 closes valve (quarter turn) → 1:40 flips breaker → 2:10 pulls lever / opens door handle, contact bar visible → 2:40 unplug network: hand keeps grip, system reports safe state.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** flange or bus adapter missing → clamp the hand on a fixed stand and bring the panel to it. Hand tracking jittery → stay on preset buttons. Wrist torque of the arm too low for the valve → use a lever-handle valve.\n- **Judging angles:** impact (keeps people out of danger), Leukerbad relevance (spa and cable-car technical rooms, road closures delaying specialists), technical depth modest, innovation modest.\n\n### Concept 2 — Rehearse-then-execute with a hand digital twin\n- **Problem → value:** in an incident you get one attempt. The operator first rehearses the manipulation in simulation on a twin of the panel, then the validated sequence is replayed on the real hand with contact checks.\n- **Hardware:** as Concept 1. Optional: **SKYLANDX** scan of the room for the twin backdrop; **XREAL** glasses as the operator display.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: fiducial markers on the panel for pose; tactile / proximity (-M) for contact confirmation.\n  - Planning: Leadshine **MJCF** hand model in MuJoCo plus simple panel geometry; operator records a grasp sequence in sim; sequence exported as joint targets.\n  - Control: replay through `lhandpro_service`; abort if expected contact does not appear or current exceeds a threshold.\n  - Compute: laptop (sim + ROS 2).\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: hardware bring-up as Concept 1; load MJCF in MuJoCo and confirm joint order matches the real hand.\n  - H6–24: sim panel, recording tool, sim-to-real replay for one element.\n  - H24–40: contact-check logic, marker-based pose correction, second and third element.\n  - H40–48: polish split-screen (sim left, real right), backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** 0:00 scenario → 0:30 rehearse valve closing in sim → 1:15 \"execute\" — real hand repeats it, twin mirrors live → 2:00 deliberately shift the panel: contact check fails, system aborts and re-plans → 2:40 summary.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** MJCF and firmware generation mismatch (use resources dated ≥ 2026-02-10) → fall back to RViz2 twin with URDF and recorded real sequences. Sim contact unrealistic → use sim for kinematic rehearsal only.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation and technical depth higher (digital twin, verified execution); impact credible for infrastructure operators.\n\n### Concept 3 — Mobile first responder that can use its hand\n- **Problem → value:** a robot enters the building, maps it, finds the shut-off and operates it — \"see and act\" instead of inspection only.\n- **Hardware:** DH116 or lighter **DH116S** on a mobile manipulator or humanoid from another vendor (Galaxea, Galbot, AGIBOT, Unitree, Spirit AI, UniX AI — whichever allows an end-effector swap); SKYLANDX MetaCam for the 3D map; D-Robotics board if on-board compute for the hand is needed.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: host robot's cameras; object detector for valve / breaker; 3D scan for situational awareness.\n  - Planning: host robot's navigation and arm stack; the hand is one extra ROS 2 node called at the end of the approach.\n  - Control: `lhandpro_service` on the host computer or a companion board; power from the robot's rail (hand accepts 12–48 V, 2.5 A).\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm with **both** vendors that the wrist flange, power and data connection are feasible. If not confirmed by H6, drop to Concept 1.\n  - H6–24: mount, power, hand node running on the robot; teleoperated drive-and-grasp.\n  - H24–40: detection-triggered grasp presets; map overlay; one full mission.\n  - H40–48: reliability runs, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** 0:00 map of the \"building\" → 0:30 robot drives to the panel → 1:20 hand closes the valve → 2:10 robot carries a 5 kg bag out using hook grip → 2:40 map with marked actions.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** mechanical / electrical integration with an unfamiliar host is the main risk; humanoid arms may lack wrist torque. Fallback: mobile base carries a fixed mini-station from Concept 1.\n- **Judging angles:** strongest real-world story and multi-vendor integration; Leukerbad relevance (first look after avalanche or rockfall cuts access). Leadshine's part is small — be clear about who contributed what.\n\n### Concept 4 — Custom PJ3-driven rescue mechanism (only with Leadshine staff support)\n- **Problem → value:** a purpose-built actuator unit — for example a pan-tilt sensor mast or a door / hatch opener — where a standard robot is overkill.\n- **Hardware:** PJ3-60 / 75 / 97 joint modules (9.5 / 14 / 44 N·m rated), 3D-printed or aluminium brackets, CAN FD adapter, D-Robotics board; possibly coprod modular hardware.\n- **Architecture / stack:** sensor (camera or thermal, own supply) → simple tracking logic → position / torque commands over CAN FD. **Protocol is not public** — everything depends on Leadshine handing over documentation or sample code.\n- **48-hour plan:** H0–6 obtain protocol and spin one module, or stop. H6–24 mechanics. H24–40 closed-loop behaviour. H40–48 demo hardening.\n- **3-minute demo script:** mechanism tracks a simulated hazard (heat source or moving person), then performs one forceful action (opens a weighted hatch).\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** no protocol by H6 → abandon. Missing specs (voltage, speed, IP) → ask before designing.\n- **Judging angles:** technical depth; weakest storytelling unless tied to a concrete Leukerbad hazard.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| DH116 + Flexiv Rizon / ROKAE xMate arm | Force-sensitive arm + strong hand for valves and levers (Concepts 1–2) | **Medium** — flange adapter, separate hand control node |\n| DH116 + Dobot cobot | Same, simpler arm | **Medium** |\n| DH116 / DH116S + wheeled dual-arm robot (Galaxea, Galbot, AGIBOT G1, UniX AI) | Mobile \"see and act\" responder (Concept 3) | **High** — wrist swap, power, both vendors must agree |\n| Hand + XREAL glasses | Operator sees video and hand twin hands-free | **Low–medium** — display only is easy; hand-tracking input is more work |\n| Hand + OriginFlow EMG | Operator's muscle signals drive grasp presets | **Medium–high** — depends on OriginFlow's output API (not verified) |\n| Hand + SKYLANDX MetaCam | 3D scan of the incident room as twin backdrop | **Low** — offline scan, no real-time coupling needed |\n| Hand or PJ3 + D-Robotics RDK board | On-robot compute for the hand node | **Medium** — confirm LHandProLib ARM build and bus adapter |\n\n## Questions to ask the Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. engineers on site\n1. Which hands are available to loan — DH116, DH116S, DH2116; CAN FD or EtherCAT; left or right; tactile (-M) or not — and how many?\n2. Can we get the **current SDK, ROS 2 workspace, URDF / MJCF and protocol sheets on a USB stick** now (matching the firmware of the loan units)?\n3. What flange / mounting pattern does the hand have, and do you have adapter plates for Flexiv, ROKAE, Dobot, Unitree or AGIBOT wrists?\n4. For CAN FD units: which USB-CAN FD adapter does the SDK support on Linux? For EtherCAT units: any NIC restrictions?\n5. Which feedback can we read per finger and at what rate — position, current, force, tactile, proximity? Can we set a grip-force or current limit?\n6. What happens on communication loss or power loss — does the hand hold position (self-locking), and how do we release it safely?\n7. Is there an existing teleoperation or hand-retargeting demo (glove, camera) we can reuse?\n8. For PJ3 / HJ2: is there a protocol document, sample code or ROS 2 driver, and what are supply voltage, peak torque and IP rating?\n\n## Decision rule\n- **Pick Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. for T02 if** you already have a host arm or mobile manipulator secured and your concept's key moment is a robot operating human-made controls (valve, breaker, handle) by remote control or after an autonomous approach — indoors.\n- **Avoid if** you need a self-contained platform for patrol, mapping, search or outdoor hazard monitoring, cannot spare half a day for mounting and bus bring-up, or would depend on the joint modules without confirmed protocol support.\n\n## Sources\n- [Leadshine company profile (internal, general research)](../Leadshine.md)\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [DH116 / DH116P user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS2 example project description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [FAIR plus 2026 recap (Leadshine store blog)](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news/leadshine-dexterous-hands-and-joint-modules-shine-at-the-shenzhen-fair-plus-2026)\n- [WAIC 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html)\n","Leadshine-T03-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T03\nchallenge_title: \"Sustainable City\"\nfit: 2\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, brief]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T03 Sustainable City — Brief\n\n**Fit: 2/5** — Supporting role only: Leadshine sells parts (hands, joint modules, motors), not a robot or a sustainability product; here it is the *gripper* in someone else's sorting or repair cell.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **DH116** five-finger hand (11 DoF, **545 g**, whole-hand load up to **30 kg**, optional tactile sensing) on another vendor's arm |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | **None in this domain.** Closest: undated, unnamed logistics case studies on the Leadshine site (servo drives in AGV/AMR and cross-belt sorters; \"low power consumption\" claimed, no figures) |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | Bench-mounted DH116 \"squeeze-to-sort\": camera plus touch classifies PET / can / glass / carton and lights the right bin; add a host arm if integration goes well |\n| Biggest risk | Needs a host arm, flange, power and bus bring-up — and a two-finger gripper sorts simple items just as well |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | Flexiv, Dobot or ROKAE (arm) · D-Robotics (edge compute) · XREAL or OriginFlow (teleoperation) |\n\n## Why\n- **No domain product or deployment**: nothing public on waste, recycling, energy, EV charging or environmental monitoring.\n- **Hands are the only quickly programmable item**: LHandProLib SDK (C++ / Python), ROS 2 example, URDF / MJCF.\n- **Real value for irregular waste**: the tactile option reports normal and tangential force plus proximity.\n- **Efficiency story is thin**: FM2 motor claims 20% lower loss (vendor claim); PJ3 / HJ2 joint modules have no public SDK or protocol.\n- **Accessible price**: DH116 store price **USD 2,199** (USD 2,799 with tactile sensors).\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams wanting mobility, perception and autonomy in one platform.\n- Monitoring, building-energy or EV-charging concepts.\n- Wet, dirty or outdoor handling — no IP rating is published for the hands.\n\n## Sources\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html) · [Logistics solution page](https://www.leadshine.com/solution/logistics-industry.html) · [Store: DH116 price](https://store.leadshine.com/products/dh116-r1-dexterous-hand) (via profile) · [Company profile](../Leadshine.md)\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Products and solutions](Leadshine-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](Leadshine-T03-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T03-Sustainable-City/T03-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","Leadshine-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T03\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, products]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T03 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Brief](Leadshine-T03-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](Leadshine-T03-3-Playbook.md)\n\n> **Read this first:** Leadshine is a component supplier. Nothing below is a sustainability product; the ranking reflects how quickly each part can be put to work in a T03 build (waste sorting, repair / disassembly, efficient custom mechanisms). What Leadshine actually brings on site is **not confirmed**.\n> **Research note:** track-specific web search was unavailable in this pass (search quota exhausted). Findings rely on official Leadshine pages fetched directly on 2026-11-14 plus the company profile. Absence of evidence below means \"not found on official pages\", not \"proven absent\".\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### DH116 series\n\n![DH116 series](../assets/leadshine-dh116.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** end-effector for a waste-sorting, litter-handling or reuse cell on another vendor's arm. Handles irregular objects (bottles, cans, cartons, bags) that defeat a parallel gripper; the tactile variant adds a material \"squeeze check\".\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Degrees of freedom | **11** (6 actively driven) |\n| Weight | **545 g** (±10 g) |\n| Load | Whole hand up to **30 kg**; single finger **5 kg** |\n| Lateral grip | **> 50 N** |\n| Drive | Brushless coreless motor + worm gear; FOC current loop, hybrid force-position control |\n| Tactile (‑M variant) | 6 sensors standard (fingertips / palm options): normal force, tangential force and its direction; proximity sensing, \"soft landing\" |\n| Rated life | **1 million** grasp cycles (vendor figure) |\n| Interfaces | EtherCAT (DH116EC), CAN FD, RS485 |\n| Supply voltage, power draw, IP rating, operating temperature | **Not on the product page** — check the manual or ask on site |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping. **Price:** store **USD 2,199** (no sensors) / **USD 2,799** (tactile ‑M), sale prices per the company profile.\n- **Developer interface:** LHandProLib SDK (C++ / Python, Windows / Linux, x86 / ARM); ROS 2 example package; URDF / MJCF; Windows GUI for jogging fingers. You command finger positions / grasp sequences and read back joint state; exact tactile-data and force-setpoint API ⚠️ unverified — confirm on site.\n- **T03 caveat:** no IP rating published — treat it as a **dry, clean-object** hand. Wet, sticky or sharp waste is a risk to a USD 2k device.\n\n### DH116S series\n\n![DH116S series](../assets/leadshine-dh116s.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** lighter alternative when the host arm has low payload (small cobot, desktop arm).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Degrees of freedom | **11**, **6 active** |\n| Size | 147.54 × 73.53 × 26.35 mm |\n| Weight | **< 380 g** (store figure) |\n| Load | Light-load handling, **10 kg** |\n| Interfaces | EtherCAT / CAN FD / RS485 |\n| Tactile | Optional, fingers and palm |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ (launched WAIC, July 2026). **Price:** not public.\n- **Developer interface:** same SDK family; the official ROS 2 example project (\"LHandPro\": state publisher, interfaces, service node, C++ / Python sequence demos, RViz2 mirror) was written for this model.\n\n### DH2116 series\n\n![DH2116 series](../assets/leadshine-dh2116.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** fine manipulation for circular-economy demos (opening a battery cover, unplugging a connector, handling small parts), typically under teleoperation.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Degrees of freedom | **21** (16 active + 5 passive) |\n| Max whole-hand grip | **94 N** |\n| Drive | Brushless coreless motors + precision screw drive |\n| Interfaces | CAN 1 Mbps, CAN FD 1/5 Mbps, RS485 5 Mbps |\n\n- **Status:** ✅. **Price:** not public.\n- **Developer interface:** SDK for Windows / Linux / ROS 2, C++ and Python. More joints means more retargeting work — only choose it if the task truly needs in-hand dexterity.\n\n### PJ3 series\n\n![PJ3 series](../assets/leadshine-pj3.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** actuator for a custom mechanism (bin-lid opener, sorting flap, sun-tracking panel mount, valve or cover drive). Only viable if Leadshine staff provide the control protocol.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Models (outer diameter) | **PJ3-60** (9.5 N·m rated), **PJ3-75** (14 N·m rated), **PJ3-97** (44 N·m rated) |\n| Contents | Motor + planetary gearbox + encoder + drive in one unit |\n| Bus | CAN FD or EtherCAT versions |\n| Monitoring | Expansion port for real-time parameters such as temperature rise |\n| Listed applications | Collaborative robots, industrial automation, machine tools, humanoids |\n| Voltage, speed, weight, efficiency, IP rating | **Not on the product page** |\n\n- **Status:** ✅. **Price:** store sale prices **USD 329–479** depending on size and bus ⚠️ unverified (from search snippets in the profile pass; store page could not be re-fetched today).\n- **Developer interface:** **no public SDK, ROS 2 driver or protocol document found.** Budget for a protocol hand-over from Leadshine engineers, or skip.\n\n### FM2 series\n\n![FM2 series](../assets/leadshine-fm2.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** talking point for an energy-efficiency narrative; not a 48-hour building block (a frameless motor needs housing, bearings, encoder and drive).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Efficiency claim | **20% lower loss** and 10% higher torque density vs previous generation (vendor claim) |\n| Sizes | 43 / 50 / 60 / 70 / 85 mm on the product page (a 2026 trade-show release says six sizes, 50–127 mm — sources disagree) |\n\n- **Status:** ✅. **Price:** not public. **Developer interface:** none (bare motor).\n\n### Integrated motors (ISS1 and integrated servo / closed-loop stepper)\n\n![Integrated motors (ISS1 and integrated servo / closed-loop stepper)](../assets/leadshine-integrated-motors.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** simplest Leadshine actuator for a non-robot mechanism (conveyor, diverter, tracker) *if* any are on site — which is unlikely.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Families on the official page | Integrated stepper (iEM, iEM-RS), integrated closed-loop stepper (iCS, iCS-RS), integrated servo (iSV, iSV2-CAN, iSV2-RS) |\n| Fieldbus | Modbus RTU (all three families); CANopen (integrated servo) |\n| Voltage / power ranges | Not on the category page |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ via distributors. **Developer interface:** Modbus RTU from any laptop or SBC with an RS485 adapter is realistic in 48 hours; tuning uses Leadshine's Windows software.\n\n### iW series\n\n![iW series](../assets/leadshine-iw.jpg)\n\n**Role in T03:** low-voltage integrated wheel drive for a small AMR (resource-efficient logistics). 🧪 shown at WAIC and Automate 2026; **no product page, specs or price public**. Do not plan around it.\n\n## What Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing found under a sustainability label.** No public deployments, pilots or partnerships in waste sorting, recycling, energy management, EV charging, environmental monitoring or urban farming.\n\nAdjacent, loosely relevant facts:\n\n| When | What | Relevance to T03 | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Undated (site content) | Logistics solution page lists four unnamed case studies: DC servo drive in a multi-shuttle vehicle, in AGV / AMR, in a cross-belt sorter, and EL7 servo drive in a steerable-wheel sorter | Sorting and intralogistics machinery; the cross-belt sorter case claims \"low power consumption\" — **no figures, no customer names** | [Logistics solution page](https://www.leadshine.com/solution/logistics-industry.html) |\n| Undated (site content) | Electronics and semiconductor solution page mentions LED and lithium-battery test equipment among motion-control applications | Shows presence in clean-tech manufacturing equipment, as a parts supplier only | [Solutions overview](https://www.leadshine.com/solutions.html) |\n| 2026-01 (CES) | Company release mentions talks with e-bike, exoskeleton and medical-equipment prospects; no deals named | Weak hint at light-electric-mobility interest | Via [company profile](../Leadshine.md) |\n| 2026-06 / 2026-07 (Automate, WAIC) | Low-voltage integrated wheel solution (iW) for AGV / AMR shown | Battery-powered mobile platforms | Via [company profile](../Leadshine.md) |\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Stated direction (company):** become a platform supplier of robot core components, concentrating on **joint modules and dexterous hands**; frameless-motor capacity to the million-unit level. Nothing sustainability-specific.\n- **Inference:** the FM2 \"lower loss\" and PJ3 GaN-drive messaging suggests efficiency is a selling point of the hardware generation, but there is **no announced energy, recycling or green-city programme**.\n- **Not public:** any ESG-linked product roadmap, any software or AI plans, any European pilot.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What it gives you | Access |\n|---|---|---|\n| LHandProLib SDK | Finger position / grasp control and state read-back from C++ or Python | Download archive linked from the hand user manuals (no official GitHub org) |\n| LHandPro ROS 2 example | State publisher, service node, sequence demos, RViz2 mirror | Download archive; ROS 2 Humble / Ubuntu 22.04 ⚠️ unverified |\n| URDF / MJCF hand models | Grasp rehearsal in MuJoCo or RViz before hardware time | Linked from the manuals |\n| Windows debugging GUI | Verify the hand and sensors before writing code | Leadshine download |\n| CAN FD protocol document | Bare-metal control without the SDK | Hosted on Feishu, linked from the manual |\n\n**Leadshine offers no models, datasets, perception or task software.** For the sorting side you must bring your own: an open-vocabulary detector or VLM for item classification, and public litter / waste image sets (for example TACO or TrashNet — third-party, not checked in this pass) if you fine-tune.\n\n**Version trap:** downloads dated before **2026-02-10** are marked \"old version\" in the DH116 manual — keep SDK, firmware and URDF from the same generation.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No robot, no arm, no mobility, no perception, no compute** — every T03 concept needs a host platform from another vendor.\n- **No domain proof**: zero public sustainability deployments; do not claim otherwise in a pitch.\n- **Environmental robustness unknown**: no IP rating, temperature range or chemical resistance published for the hands — poor match for wet, dirty, outdoor or sub-zero waste handling.\n- **Over-specification risk**: a five-finger hand is slower and costlier than a suction cup or parallel gripper for plain pick-and-place sorting.\n- **Joint modules are closed for a hackathon**: PJ3 / HJ2 have no public SDK or protocol.\n- **Energy data missing**: no published power draw for hands or joint modules, so any \"low-energy\" claim must come from your own measurement.\n- **Mounting**: flange pattern, cable and power supply for a given host arm are not documented publicly — confirm before committing.\n\n## Sources\n\nFetched in this pass (2026-11-14):\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [Leadshine solutions overview](https://www.leadshine.com/solutions.html)\n- [Leadshine logistics industry solution page](https://www.leadshine.com/solution/logistics-industry.html)\n- [Leadshine integrated motors category page](https://www.leadshine.com/products/integrated-motor.html)\n\nCarried over from the company profile (not re-fetched in this pass):\n- [Leadshine company profile (internal)](../Leadshine.md)\n- [DH116S series product page](https://en.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116s-series.html)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [FM2 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/frameless-motors/fm2-series.html)\n- [Store: DH116-R1 dexterous hand (price)](https://store.leadshine.com/products/dh116-r1-dexterous-hand)\n- [Store: PJ3 planetary integrated actuators](https://store.leadshine.com/collections/pj3-planetary-integrated-actuators)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS2 example project description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [DH116 / DH116P user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [WAIC 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html)\n- [Leadshine at CES 2026 release (PR Newswire Asia)](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-achieves-great-success-at-ces-2026-entering-us-and-european-robotics-supply-chain-518892.shtml)\n","Leadshine-T03-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T03\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t03, playbook]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T03 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Brief](Leadshine-T03-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](Leadshine-T03-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n> **Framing:** Leadshine is a **2/5 supporting-role** vendor for T03. Every concept below uses a Leadshine part as one component; three of four need a host arm from another vendor. Which parts are on site is unconfirmed — check the Leadshine table before committing. All concepts assume **dry, clean objects** (no IP rating is published for the hands).\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — \"Squeeze-to-sort\" material identifier (hand only, no arm)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Mis-sorted recyclables contaminate whole batches. A bench-mounted hand that takes an item from a person, squeezes it gently and classifies the material (rigid glass / deformable PET / crushable aluminium / carton) gives a low-cost sorting assistant for hotel back-of-house or a public recycling point.\n- **Hardware:** DH116 with tactile sensors (‑M) or DH116S, clamped to a stand; laptop; USB camera; three or four labelled bins with LED strips. Optional: D-Robotics RDK X5 to run it laptop-free.\n- **Architecture / stack:** camera frame → small image classifier or VLM for a first guess → hand closes under a force limit via LHandProLib (Python) → record finger position vs force / motor-current curve → simple classifier (stiffness and spring-back features, fused with vision) → light the correct bin. Compute on the laptop. Rehearse grasp poses in MuJoCo with the MJCF.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** power and bus bring-up with the Windows GUI; confirm which signals the SDK exposes (position, current, tactile).\n  - **H6–24:** scripted squeeze routine; log 20–30 squeezes per material; train the classifier.\n  - **H24–40:** add camera fusion, bin LEDs, a tally screen (items sorted, estimated contamination avoided).\n  - **H40–48:** robustness pass with unseen items; rehearse the pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** hand a judge four items → the hand squeezes each → correct bin lights up → show the force curve live → deliberately hand a look-alike (clear PET vs clear glass) that vision alone gets wrong and touch gets right.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** tactile data not exposed in the SDK → use motor current and position only. Hand unavailable with sensors → same fallback. Crushing risk → conservative force limit, test on empties.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation (touch as a sorting signal) · technical depth (sensor fusion, measured confusion matrix) · impact (contamination is the real cost in recycling) · Leukerbad (hotels and spa facilities generate mixed guest waste; Switzerland separates PET, glass and aluminium).\n\n### Concept 2 — Dexterous recycling cell (hand on a host arm)\n\n- **Problem → value:** Mixed tourist waste has irregular shapes that defeat suction cups and parallel grippers. A hand-equipped arm sorts a tray of mixed items into fractions without item-specific tooling.\n- **Hardware:** DH116 (or DH116S on a low-payload arm) + a host arm — Flexiv Rizon, Dobot CR / Nova or ROKAE xMate — + RGB-D camera. Optional D-Robotics RDK for edge inference.\n- **Architecture / stack:** RGB-D → open-vocabulary detector or VLM labels item and material → grasp-type lookup per class (power grasp for bottles, pinch for cans / caps, lateral for cartons) → arm vendor SDK or MoveIt for the reach → LHandPro ROS 2 service node for the grasp sequence → place in bin. Compute on a laptop with GPU; both devices bridged in ROS 2.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** secure arm + hand; solve flange, cable and power; move each separately.\n  - **H6–24:** hand-eye calibration; three grasp primitives; pick one item class end to end.\n  - **H24–40:** add classes and bins; add tactile grasp-confirmation and re-grasp; count throughput.\n  - **H40–48:** tune for reliability over speed; freeze the code; record a backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** tip a tray of 6–8 mixed items → robot sorts them → a judge adds an unseen object → show the detector label and chosen grasp on screen → close with picks per minute and success rate.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** no mechanical adapter → 3D-print or bolt a plate (ask organizers for a workshop early). Integration overruns → fall back to Concept 1 on the bench with the arm doing a fixed hand-over motion. Judges ask \"why not a gripper?\" → have the irregular-object cases ready.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation (moderate) · technical depth (multi-vendor ROS 2 integration, grasp selection) · impact (tool-free sorting of mixed streams) · Leukerbad (resort waste peaks with seasonal tourism).\n\n### Concept 3 — Teleoperated e-waste disassembly with data capture\n\n- **Problem → value:** Reuse and repair are blocked by manual disassembly cost. Teleoperating a dexterous hand to open a small device (remove a battery cover, pull a battery, unplug a connector) shows a path to remote or learned disassembly and produces demonstration data.\n- **Hardware:** DH2116 (21 DoF) or DH116 + host arm (Flexiv Rizon preferred for force control) + XREAL glasses for hand tracking, or OriginFlow EMG capture as the operator interface.\n- **Architecture / stack:** operator hand pose (XREAL spatial SDK or EMG) → retargeting to hand joints (use the URDF) → LHandProLib streaming commands; arm follows wrist pose through the arm SDK. Log synchronized video + joint data per episode. Stretch goal: replay or a small imitation policy for one sub-step.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** hand bring-up; confirm command rate over CAN FD; get a tracking stream from the XR or EMG device.\n  - **H6–24:** retargeting for thumb-index pinch and power grasp; arm teleoperation with safety limits.\n  - **H24–40:** practise one disassembly task on a dummy device; record 20+ episodes.\n  - **H40–48:** replay the best episode autonomously; prepare the dataset summary slide.\n- **3-minute demo script:** operator with glasses removes a battery from a device live → show the recorded episode replayed by the robot → show the dataset (episodes, duration) and what it would take to automate.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** retargeting 16 active joints is slow to tune → drop to DH116 (6 active). Latency too high → scripted keyframes with operator trigger. Never use real lithium cells that could be punctured — use dummy packs.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation (high) · technical depth (teleoperation, retargeting, data pipeline) · impact (circular economy) · Leukerbad (weak — frame as remote repair for places far from service centres).\n\n### Concept 4 — Self-metering efficient actuator for a resource-saving mechanism\n\n- **Problem → value:** Many resource savings are one moving part away: a cover that closes over warm water, a flap that diverts a waste stream, a panel that follows the sun. Build one mechanism with an integrated joint and report the energy it uses versus the energy or water it saves.\n- **Hardware:** PJ3 joint module (PJ3-60 at 9.5 N·m or PJ3-75 at 14 N·m rated) or a Leadshine integrated servo with Modbus RTU / CANopen, if available; D-Robotics RDK board as controller; inline power meter; temperature or light sensor; 3D-printed or plywood mechanics.\n- **Architecture / stack:** sensor reading → rule or small model decides when to move → CAN FD (PJ3) or Modbus RTU (integrated motor) command from the RDK → log position, temperature-rise port and measured power → dashboard of energy in vs savings out.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - **H0–6:** get the PJ3 protocol document and a working \"move to angle\" example **from Leadshine staff**. No protocol by H6 → abandon this concept.\n  - **H6–24:** build the mechanism; closed-loop moves; power logging.\n  - **H24–40:** sensing and decision logic; measure a before / after scenario on a scale model.\n  - **H40–48:** dashboard, repeatability runs, pitch.\n- **3-minute demo script:** scale model (for example a warm-water basin with an automatic insulating cover) → trigger closes the cover → live graph of heat loss with and without → show actuator energy per cycle next to the saving.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** closed protocol is the main risk → swap in any hobby servo or another vendor's motor and keep the measurement story (Leadshine then drops out of the build). Mechanical build time is easy to underestimate.\n- **Judging angles:** innovation (low–moderate) · technical depth (measured energy balance) · impact (quantified saving) · Leukerbad (**strong** — thermal baths and heated pools at 1,400 m lose heat; any measured saving speaks to local operators).\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| DH116 + Flexiv Rizon | Force-controlled reach + multi-finger grasp for mixed waste or disassembly | **Medium–high**: custom flange, two SDKs, ROS 2 bridge |\n| DH116S + Dobot CR / Nova or ROKAE xMate | Lighter hand on a common cobot for a sorting cell | **Medium**: flange and payload check; cobot SDKs are mature |\n| DH116 / DH2116 + XREAL glasses | Hand-tracking teleoperation for disassembly demos | **Medium**: retargeting code is on you |\n| DH2116 + OriginFlow EMG capture | Muscle-signal teleoperation and demonstration data | **High**: two young stacks, calibration time |\n| Any hand + D-Robotics RDK X5 | Low-power edge inference and hand control without a laptop (SDK lists ARM support) | **Low–medium**: CAN FD / RS485 adapter and driver on ARM |\n| Hand on AGIBOT / Galaxea / Galbot mobile manipulator | Mobile litter or sorting robot | **High**: replacing a stock end-effector in 48 hours is unrealistic unless the vendor already supports it ⚠️ unverified |\n| PJ3 + coprod modular hardware | Custom mechanism from modular parts | **Unknown**: depends on PJ3 protocol access and coprod interfaces |\n\n## Questions to ask the Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. engineers on site\n\n1. Which hands are available to teams (DH116, DH116S, DH2116; left / right; with or without tactile sensors), how many, and with which bus (CAN FD, RS485, EtherCAT)?\n2. What supply voltage and peak current does the hand need, and do you provide the power supply and a USB-to-CAN FD adapter?\n3. Does the Python SDK expose **raw tactile readings and motor current** at a usable rate, and can I set a grasp-force limit per finger?\n4. Do you have a mounting adapter for the Flexiv, Dobot or ROKAE flange, or the flange drawing so we can print one?\n5. Can you hand over the latest SDK, ROS 2 package and URDF / MJCF on a USB stick (post-2026-02-10 generation), and which ROS 2 distribution is tested?\n6. Is there an IP rating or cleaning guidance for the hand — can it touch damp or dirty items?\n7. For PJ3 / HJ2: is there a CAN FD protocol document and a minimal example we may use this weekend, and what is the idle and typical power draw?\n8. Are any integrated servo motors, low-voltage drives or iW wheel modules on site?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. for T03 if** your idea hinges on grasping or feeling irregular objects (sorting, disassembly, reuse), you have already secured a host arm, and a Leadshine engineer has confirmed hand availability, power, bus adapter and mounting within the first hours.\n- **Avoid if** you need a complete robot, outdoor or wet operation, environmental sensing, or a ready autonomy stack — or if a suction cup or two-finger gripper would do the job.\n\n## Sources\n\nFetched in this pass (2026-11-14):\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [Leadshine solutions overview](https://www.leadshine.com/solutions.html)\n- [Leadshine logistics industry solution page](https://www.leadshine.com/solution/logistics-industry.html)\n- [Leadshine integrated motors category page](https://www.leadshine.com/products/integrated-motor.html)\n\nCarried over from the company profile (not re-fetched in this pass):\n- [Leadshine company profile (internal)](../Leadshine.md)\n- [DH116S series product page](https://en.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116s-series.html)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS2 example project description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [DH116 / DH116P user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [Store: DH116-R1 dexterous hand (price)](https://store.leadshine.com/products/dh116-r1-dexterous-hand)\n\nNote: track-specific web search was unavailable in this pass (search quota exhausted); concepts are the author's proposals, not Leadshine reference designs.\n","Leadshine-T04-1-Brief":"---\ntype: challenge-brief\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T04\nchallenge_title: \"Elderly Care & Rehabilitation\"\nfit: 3\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, brief]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T04 Elderly Care & Rehabilitation — Brief\n\n**Fit: 3/5** — A good *add-on* (human-like five-finger hand with SDK and optional touch sensing), not a platform: Leadshine has no robot, no care software and no care deployments.\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| Best hardware / platform for this track | **DH116S** compact hand (**359 g**, 11 DoF / 6 active, official ROS 2 Humble example) or **DH116-M** (tactile, **545 g**) on another vendor's arm; **DH2116** (21 DoF / 16 active) for finger-level therapy mirroring |\n| Strongest proof point (real deployment or demo + date) | **None in care.** Closest: CES, 6–9 Jan 2026 — exoskeleton and medical-equipment makers \"discussed cooperation\" on its motors and joint modules; no deal named. Hands demoed live at CES, WAIC (Jul 2026), Automate (Jun 2026) |\n| Fastest credible 48-hour demo | **Hand-rehab mirror coach:** webcam hand tracking → the robot hand shows and mirrors finger exercises, counts repetitions, scores range of motion. Needs no arm and no mobile base |\n| Biggest risk | Hardware on site is unconfirmed; SDK comes as download archives (no GitHub); bus bring-up (EtherCAT / CAN FD / RS485) and mounting can eat half a day; not a medical device |\n| Pairs well with (other vendors on site) | OriginFlow (EMG → hand control), XREAL (AR-guided therapy), Flexiv / ROKAE (force-controlled arm to carry the hand), Galaxea / AGIBOT / Unitree (host robots) |\n\n## Why\n- A five-finger hand is the most human-readable tool for care tasks: hand-overs, cups, pill boxes, door handles.\n- Developer stack exists for the hands only: **LHandProLib** (C++ / Python, Windows / Linux, x86 / ARM), ROS 2 example, URDF / MJCF.\n- DH116-M adds fingertip tactile sensors — useful for \"gentle grip\" and release-on-pull hand-overs.\n- Cheap for its class: DH116 **USD 2,199**, tactile **USD 2,799** (store prices).\n- PJ3 joint modules (USD 329–479) target exoskeleton-class joints, but no public SDK or protocol was found.\n\n## Not a fit for\n- Teams that want a ready robot, autonomy stack, or any mobility — Leadshine supplies none.\n- Wearable exoskeleton or patient-contact force demos in 48 hours (no public joint-module SDK, no safety certification for body contact).\n- Industrial servo / stepper / PLC builds — too much wiring and tuning for this event.\n\n## Sources\n- [Leadshine company profile](../Leadshine.md)\n- [DH116S user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf) · [DH116 user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf) · [ROS 2 example (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [CES 2026 release (PR Newswire Asia)](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-achieves-great-success-at-ces-2026-entering-us-and-european-robotics-supply-chain-518892.shtml)\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Products and solutions](Leadshine-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions.md) · [Playbook](Leadshine-T04-3-Playbook.md) · [Track overview](../../../Challenges/T04-Elderly-Care-and-Rehabilitation/T04-0-Track-Overview.md)\n","Leadshine-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions":"---\ntype: challenge-products\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T04\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, products]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T04 — Products and Solutions\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Brief](Leadshine-T04-1-Brief.md) · [Playbook](Leadshine-T04-3-Playbook.md)\n\n**Bottom line:** for care and rehab, Leadshine = **a robot hand you add to something else**. Only the dexterous hands have a usable developer stack. Joint modules are marketed for exoskeletons but have no public SDK. What is physically on site is **not confirmed** — check at the booth first.\n\n**Research note:** web search was unavailable in this pass; everything below comes from Leadshine's product pages, user manuals (PDF), official show recaps and the company profile. Store pages were rate-limited, so prices are taken from the profile.\n\n## Relevant products (ranked for this track)\n\n### DH116S series\n\n![DH116S series](../assets/leadshine-dh116s.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** the lightest hand and the one with the clearest ROS 2 example — best choice for a desk-mounted therapy / mirror demo or as an end-effector on a small arm.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| DoF | **11 total, 6 active** (thumb swing 0–91°, thumb flex 0–59°, index / middle / ring 0–72°, little 0–74°) |\n| Size / weight | 147.54 × 73.53 × 26.35 mm · **359 g ± 10 g** (manual; store says < 380 g) |\n| Speed | Minimum open / close time **0.4 s** |\n| Force / load | Side grip **50 N**; hook-grip load 15 kg in the parameter table (overview text says 10 kg) |\n| Repeatability | ± 0.2 mm |\n| Power | **12–48 V**, 0.1 A idle, **2.0 A max** |\n| Tactile options | none · **-S** piezoresistive · **-M** multimodal. Fingers: normal force 0–20 N (0.01 N resolution) + tangential direction 0–360° (1°); palm: normal force 0–25 N (0.1 N) |\n| Bus variants | DH116S (CAN FD) · DH116SEC (EtherCAT) · DH116SRS (RS485); left and right |\n| Environment | **IP30**, −10 to 45 °C; surface can be wiped with alcohol |\n| Life | 1 million open / close cycles; 12-month warranty |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping (launched at WAIC, July 2026). **Price:** not public / not captured.\n- **Developer control:** LHandProLib SDK (C++ / Python, Windows / Linux, x86 / ARM); official ROS 2 workspace `lhandpro_ws` (state publisher → `/joint_states`, service node, C++ and Python sequence demos, RViz2 mirror); URDF + MJCF; CAN FD protocol document; Windows GUI for jogging.\n- **Vendor's stated use:** entertainment, display, education, research — **not** medical or care.\n- **Optional accessories listed in the manual:** USB-CAN FD debugger, EtherCAT debug cable, variable power supply, and a \"6248XL\" data glove for data acquisition (on-site availability unknown).\n\n### DH116 series\n\n![DH116 series](../assets/leadshine-dh116.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** the sturdier hand; the **-M tactile variant** is the pick for gentle hand-overs (detect contact, slip and a person pulling on the object).\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| DoF | **11 total, 6 active** (thumb swing 0–60°, thumb flex 0–30°, four fingers 0–80°) |\n| Weight | **545 g ± 10 g** |\n| Speed | Open / close time ≥ 0.5 s |\n| Force / load | Lateral grip up to **50 N**; hook-grip load up to **30 kg**; single finger 5 kg |\n| Sensing (-M) | Multimodal tactile: normal force, tangential force, **proximity sensing** (\"soft landing\"); 6 sensors standard on -M |\n| Control | Brushless coreless servo, FOC current loop, hybrid force-position control inside the hand |\n| Power | **12–48 V @ 2.5 A** |\n| Bus variants | DH116 (CAN FD) · DH116EC (EtherCAT); RS485 also supported per product page |\n| Environment | −10 to 40 °C / 85 % RH (manual precautions say operate at 0–40 °C) |\n| Life | 1 million grasp cycles; 12-month warranty |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping. **Price:** **USD 2,199** (no sensors) / **USD 2,799** (-M tactile), store sale prices per company profile.\n- **Developer control:** same LHandProLib SDK, ROS 2 workspace (`lhandpro_ws_116`, dated 2026-02-10), URDF / MJCF, EtherCAT + CAN FD + RS485 protocol sheet, EtherCAT XML device file.\n- **Version trap:** resources for units built before **2026-02-10** are listed separately as \"old version\" — match SDK, URDF and firmware generation.\n\n### DH2116 series\n\n![DH2116 series](../assets/leadshine-dh2116.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** finger-level realism — each finger has 3 motors driving 4 joints including sideways spread (± 20°). Best for sign / gesture mirroring, fine pinch tasks, or an EMG \"prosthesis trainer\" demo. Heavier and more fragile than the DH116 family.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| DoF | **21 total: 16 active + 5 passive** |\n| Size / weight | 218 × 119 × 80.5 mm · **850 g ± 10 g** |\n| Speed | Minimum open / close time 0.6 s |\n| Force / load | Recommended load **≤ 5 kg**; five-finger grip max **150 N** in the manual (product page says 94 N — sources disagree) |\n| Repeatability | ± 0.6 mm |\n| Power | **24–60 V**; 1 A rated, **5 A max** at 24 V |\n| Bus | CAN, CAN FD, RS485 (no EtherCAT). Ships with M9 debug cable + PCAN-USB adapter |\n| Command model | Motor position 0–4095 per motor; manual gives the motor → joint-angle mapping |\n| Tactile | Not mentioned in the manual or product page |\n| Life | 300,000 cycles; design life 2 years |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping. **Price:** not public / not captured.\n- **Developer control:** LHandProLib SDK, URDF, protocol sheet, Windows GUI. The product page claims ROS 2 support, but the manual lists **no ROS 2 workspace download** ⚠️ unverified — ask on site.\n\n### PJ3 series\n\n![PJ3 series](../assets/leadshine-pj3.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** the only Leadshine part positioned for wearable / assistive joints — Leadshine's own FAIR plus recap lists **exoskeletons** among PJ3 targets. Realistic hackathon use: a bench-top single-joint exerciser, not something worn by a person.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Type | Planetary gearbox + motor + encoder + drive in one unit |\n| Models (rated torque) | PJ3-60 **9.5 N·m** · PJ3-75 **14 N·m** · PJ3-97 **44 N·m** (per company profile) |\n| Bus | CAN FD or EtherCAT versions |\n| Extras | Monitoring port (e.g. temperature) |\n| Peak torque, speed, weight, backdrivability, torque-control mode | **not on the product page** — ask for the datasheet |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping. **Price:** **USD 329–479** on the store (per company profile, from search snippets ⚠️ unverified).\n- **Developer control:** **no public SDK, ROS 2 driver or protocol document found.** Usable only if Leadshine engineers hand over the protocol and a USB-CAN adapter.\n\n### HJ2 series\n\n![HJ2 series](../assets/leadshine-hj2.jpg)\n\n**Role in T04:** harmonic joint for precise, low-backlash arm-type joints. Marginal for a 48-hour care build.\n\n| Spec | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Sizes | 52 / 70 / 80 / 90 / 110 mm |\n| Encoder | Built-in **24-bit** |\n| Bus | EtherCAT and CAN FD |\n| Torque / weight | not on product page |\n\n- **Status:** ✅ shipping. **Price:** not public. **Developer control:** same gap as PJ3 — no public SDK or protocol.\n\n**Not ranked (little T04 value in 48 h):** FM2 frameless motors, coreless motors, iW wheel modules, industrial servo / stepper drives and PLCs — component-level parts that need custom mechanics, wiring and tuning.\n\n## What Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. already does in this domain\n\n**Nothing public under its own name.** No elderly-care, rehabilitation, prosthetics or medical deployment, pilot or partnership was found.\n\n| Date | What | Relevance | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-01 (CES, 6–9 Jan) | Company says makers of **exoskeletons, e-assist bicycles and high-end medical equipment** approached it to \"discuss cooperation\" on frameless motors and joint modules. No customer or contract named | Interest only — not a deployment | [PR Newswire Asia](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-achieves-great-success-at-ces-2026-entering-us-and-european-robotics-supply-chain-518892.shtml) |\n| 2026-04/05 (FAIR plus, Shenzhen) | PJ3 planetary joints described as covering robot dogs, small / mid humanoids, **exoskeletons** and electric scooters; hand demo zone showed \"anthropomorphic\" grasping (no task detail) | Positioning statement | [Leadshine store blog](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news/leadshine-dexterous-hands-and-joint-modules-shine-at-the-shenzhen-fair-plus-2026) |\n| 2026-07 (WAIC, Shanghai) | DH116S launched for small humanoids; cited supplier ties with AGIBOT, Galaxea ⚠️ unverified, Fourier and Tiangong (per company profile). Fourier is known for rehabilitation robotics, but no care-related joint project is stated | Indirect at best | [Leadshine news](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html) |\n\n- Its hands and joints sit inside other companies' humanoids (company claim: supply chains of 80 % of leading Chinese humanoid makers). Any care use happens through those customers, not Leadshine.\n\n## What they are building next in this domain\n\n- **Announced strategy (2026):** become a \"platform\" supplier of robot core components, focused on joint modules and dexterous hands, with planetary modules prioritised for volume (per company profile).\n- **No care- or medical-specific roadmap is public.** No medical certification, prosthetic hand, exoskeleton kit or care software has been announced.\n- **Inference (not announced):** exoskeleton and medical-equipment interest reported at CES plus the PJ3 \"exoskeleton\" positioning suggest Leadshine wants wearable-robot makers as component customers. Expect parts, not finished assistive products.\n\n## Software, models and data that help for this track\n\n| Asset | What it gives you | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **LHandProLib SDK** | C++ / Python API for finger position / motion and sensor read-out on all DH hands | Download page linked from each manual; no GitHub. Windows and Linux, x86 and ARM |\n| **ROS 2 workspace `lhandpro_ws`** (DH116S, DH116) | `lhandpro_description` (URDF + meshes, RViz2 launch), `lhandpro_interfaces`, `lhandpro_service` (service node wrapping the API), `sequence_demo_cpp` / `sequence_demo_py` | Official doc states **Ubuntu 22.04 + ROS 2 Humble**. Example drives the hand over **EtherCAT** (needs raw-socket permission and the right network interface set in code, then rebuild) |\n| **URDF + MJCF** | Simulation and retargeting before you get hardware time | DH116, DH116S, DH2116 (DH2116: URDF listed) |\n| **LHandPro GUI (Windows)** | Jog fingers, check sensors — fastest hardware sanity check | Bring a Windows laptop or VM |\n| **Protocol sheets** | CAN FD / EtherCAT / RS485 register maps | Fallback if the SDK fails on your OS |\n| **Joint-coupling tables** | Passive-vs-driven joint angle relations (DH2116 gives 5th-order polynomials) | Needed for accurate camera-to-hand retargeting |\n| Data glove (\"6248XL\") | Listed as an optional data-acquisition accessory | Availability on site unknown; glove SDK not seen |\n\n- **Not offered:** no AI models, no datasets, no teleoperation stack, no speech / perception, no on-board compute. Bring your own hand tracking (camera-based), retargeting and task logic.\n\n## Gaps and limits for this track\n\n- **No robot.** A hand alone cannot fetch, carry or move — it needs another vendor's arm or mobile base, plus a flange adapter.\n- **Not a medical device.** No medical or body-contact safety certification is stated; vendor lists entertainment, display, education and research as uses.\n- **Do not grasp people.** DH116 / DH116S use worm-gear drives, and the manual warns against forcing the fingers under the \"self-locking function\" — a closed grip may not be openable by the person (inference from the manual). Side grip reaches 50 N.\n- **IP30 only** (DH116S): no water, steam or spa environments; dry objects only.\n- **Tactile depends on the variant.** Only -S / -M models sense force; a plain unit gives position and current only.\n- **Bring-up overhead:** 12–48 V supply (24–60 V for DH2116), bus adapter (USB-CAN FD, PCAN-USB or an EtherCAT-capable NIC), SDK from download archives, old / new resource generations.\n- **Joint modules:** no public SDK, torque-control details or safety functions — unsuitable for anything worn by a person in 48 hours.\n- **No Swiss / European support** channel identified; rely on engineers at the booth.\n\n## Sources\n\nFetched in this pass:\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [DH116S series product page](https://en.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116s-series.html)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [DH116S user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116 / DH116P user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH2116 user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH2116_Series_Dexterous_Hand_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS2 example project description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [Leadshine at CES 2026 — supply-chain release (PR Newswire Asia)](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-achieves-great-success-at-ces-2026-entering-us-and-european-robotics-supply-chain-518892.shtml)\n- [FAIR plus 2026 recap (Leadshine store blog)](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news/leadshine-dexterous-hands-and-joint-modules-shine-at-the-shenzhen-fair-plus-2026)\n\nTaken from the company profile (not re-fetched):\n- [Leadshine company profile](../Leadshine.md)\n- [WAIC 2026 recap (Leadshine news)](https://en.leadshine.com/newsEvent/details/leadshine-successfully-concludes-waic-2026,-showcasing-next-generation-robotics-solutions.html)\n- [HJ2 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/hj2-series.html)\n- [Store: DH116-R1 dexterous hand (price)](https://store.leadshine.com/products/dh116-r1-dexterous-hand)\n- [Store: PJ3 planetary integrated actuators (prices)](https://store.leadshine.com/collections/pj3-planetary-integrated-actuators)\n","Leadshine-T04-3-Playbook":"---\ntype: challenge-playbook\ncompany: \"Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd.\"\nslug: Leadshine\nchallenge: T04\nupdated: 2026-11-14\ntags: [leukerbad-hackathon, t04, playbook]\n---\n# Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. × T04 — Hackathon Playbook\n\n⬆ [Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. profile](../Leadshine.md) · [Brief](Leadshine-T04-1-Brief.md) · [Products and solutions](Leadshine-T04-2-Products-and-Solutions.md)\n\n**Read first:** Leadshine brings parts, not robots. Every concept below assumes a dexterous hand (DH116S, DH116 or DH2116) is available to borrow — **confirm at the booth before committing**. Hard safety rule for all concepts: **the robot hand never grips a person** (worm-gear fingers can self-lock, side grip up to 50 N, no medical certification).\n\n**Hour-zero checklist (same for every concept):**\n1. Which hand, left or right, which bus (CAN FD / EtherCAT / RS485), tactile variant (-S / -M) or none?\n2. Power supply (12–48 V; DH2116 24–60 V) and bus adapter included?\n3. Get the SDK, ROS 2 workspace and URDF on a USB stick from the engineers — matching the unit's generation (before / after 2026-02-10).\n4. Jog the hand with the Windows LHandPro GUI before writing any code.\n\n## Solution approaches\n\n### Concept 1 — Hand-rehab mirror coach (hand only, safest)\n\n- **Problem → value:** hand and finger exercises after stroke, fracture or arthritis flare-ups are repetitive, and therapists are scarce. A robot hand that *demonstrates* each exercise, mirrors the patient's attempt and logs range of motion makes home or clinic practice measurable. No patient contact.\n- **Hardware:** **DH116S** (or DH116) on a desk stand + laptop webcam. Optional: XREAL glasses for an AR overlay of target poses; D-Robotics RDK board as a stand-alone compute unit (SDK supports ARM).\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: camera hand-landmark tracking (any open-source hand tracker) → finger flexion angles.\n  - Planning: map to the **6 active joints** (DH116S ranges: thumb swing 0–91°, thumb flex 0–59°, index / middle / ring 0–72°, little 0–74°); exercise state machine (demo → patient copies → score → next).\n  - Control: LHandProLib Python API, or the ROS 2 Humble service node from `lhandpro_ws`; RViz2 / MJCF twin for development without hardware.\n  - Compute: one Ubuntu 22.04 laptop.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: hardware check with GUI; SDK hello-world; hand tracking running on webcam.\n  - H6–24: retargeting to 6 DoF with smoothing and rate limits; 5 exercises (fist, finger opposition, pinch, spread, tendon glide); repetition counter.\n  - H24–40: scoring (range of motion vs target, left / right comparison), session report, simple large-print UI with voice prompts; mirror-therapy mode (healthy hand tracked, robot shows the mirrored movement).\n  - H40–48: rehearse, record backup video, write the therapist-report slide.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) problem in one sentence + Leukerbad clinic context → (0:30) robot hand demonstrates an exercise, volunteer copies, counter and score update live → (1:30) mirror mode: robot follows the volunteer's hand in real time → (2:15) show the generated progress report → (2:40) what a clinic pilot would need.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** tracking jitter → filter and snap to exercise poses; bus bring-up fails → run the MJCF / RViz2 twin on screen; unit has no ROS 2 package (DH2116) → use the SDK directly.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* moderate (robotic mirror therapy at low cost); *Technical depth* retargeting + scoring; *Impact* clear clinical workflow, measurable; *Leukerbad* direct link to local rehabilitation clinics and spa guests.\n\n### Concept 2 — Gentle hand-over assistant (hand + force-controlled arm)\n\n- **Problem → value:** people with weak grip or limited reach need objects (cup, pill box, glasses, phone) brought and handed over safely. The hard part is the hand-over itself: hold securely, release exactly when the person takes it.\n- **Hardware:** **DH116-M** (tactile) or DH116S-S / -M on a **Flexiv Rizon** or **ROKAE xMate** arm (both listed with force / torque sensing). Depth camera for object and hand detection.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: object detection for 3–4 known items; detect the person's open hand.\n  - Planning: grasp primitives per object (power, pinch, hook) + pick → present → wait → release sequence.\n  - Control: arm via its vendor SDK; hand via LHandProLib / ROS 2 service; release triggered by tangential-force change at the fingertips (or arm wrist force if the hand has no sensors). Speech in / out for commands and confirmation.\n  - Compute: laptop; arm controller as supplied.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm arm + hand availability, flange adapter (3D-print if needed), power and cable routing; both devices moving independently.\n  - H6–24: grasp primitives tuned on real objects; pick-and-present trajectory; read tactile data.\n  - H24–40: pull-to-release logic, speed limits near the person, voice loop, \"medication time\" reminder scenario.\n  - H40–48: reliability runs (target 9 / 10), demo rehearsal, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) spoken request \"bring my pills\" → (0:30) robot picks the pill box, presents it at a fixed safe zone → (1:10) volunteer takes it; hand releases on pull, not before → (1:40) repeat with a cup; show the live force plot → (2:20) safety rules and limits.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** no flange adapter → clamp-mount and reduce payload; no tactile variant → use arm force sensing or a timed release with spoken confirmation; **IP30** → dry, empty containers only; never present objects toward the face.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* tactile release on a low-cost hand; *Technical depth* multi-vendor integration + force logic; *Impact* daily-living assistance; *Leukerbad* hotel / clinic room service for guests with limited mobility.\n\n### Concept 3 — Muscle-signal (EMG) controlled assistive hand\n\n- **Problem → value:** amputees and people with paresis train for months to control a prosthesis or assistive gripper. A bench \"prosthesis trainer\" lets them practise muscle-signal control with a realistic five-finger hand and gives therapists objective feedback.\n- **Hardware:** **DH2116** (finger-level realism) or DH116S + **OriginFlow** EMG capture (see its profile for what is available). Optional XREAL display for feedback.\n- **Architecture / stack:**\n  - Perception: forearm EMG stream → gesture / intent classifier (4–6 classes: rest, fist, pinch, point, open, tripod), calibrated per user in about 2 minutes.\n  - Planning: intent → grasp primitive, with proportional closing from signal amplitude.\n  - Control: LHandProLib (DH2116: motor commands 0–4095, mapping in the manual); game-like training tasks with scoring.\n  - Compute: laptop.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: confirm EMG data access (format, rate, SDK) and hand control separately.\n  - H6–24: per-user calibration + classifier; map classes to hand poses; latency measurement.\n  - H24–40: proportional control, training game (pick the right grip for a shown object), accuracy and latency dashboard.\n  - H40–48: test on 3 different people, rehearse, backup video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) who needs this → (0:30) volunteer calibrates live (shortened) → (1:10) hand follows muscle intent: open, pinch, fist → (1:50) training game with score → (2:30) accuracy / latency numbers and next steps.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** EMG stream not accessible in real time → fall back to camera hand tracking (Concept 1 pipeline) and present EMG as recorded data; classifier unstable → reduce to 3 classes; DH2116 unavailable → use DH116S with 6 DoF.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* high (cross-vendor human-machine interface); *Technical depth* signal processing + ML + control; *Impact* prosthetics and neuro-rehab training; *Leukerbad* rehab-clinic use case.\n\n### Concept 4 — Bench-top single-joint rehab exerciser with a PJ3 module (most ambitious)\n\n- **Problem → value:** wrist, elbow or ankle mobilisation needs slow, repeatable assisted motion. A compact actuator with adjustable assistance / resistance could become a low-cost exerciser.\n- **Hardware:** one **PJ3** module (PJ3-60, 9.5 N·m rated, is the sensible size) + 3D-printed handle and base + USB-CAN adapter + emergency stop cutting power. Optional EMG or camera feedback.\n- **Architecture / stack:** joint position / velocity / current feedback → assist-as-needed controller (follow a slow trajectory, yield when resistance exceeds a limit) → session logging. Control over CAN FD using Leadshine's protocol. Compute: laptop.\n- **Go / no-go gate (H0–2):** proceed **only if** Leadshine engineers provide the protocol document, confirm a torque / current control mode and backdrivability, and lend an adapter. No public SDK exists. Otherwise switch to Concept 1.\n- **48-hour plan:**\n  - H0–6: gate check; spin the module; read position and current.\n  - H6–24: mechanical fixture; torque-limited trajectory mode; hard software limits on angle, speed and torque.\n  - H24–40: assist-as-needed and resistance modes; logging and progress chart.\n  - H40–48: safety review, rehearsal, video.\n- **3-minute demo script:** (0:00) need → (0:30) passive mobilisation: the handle moves a **dummy limb or test weight** → (1:20) a team member holds the handle with torque capped low to show yield-on-resistance → (2:10) session chart → (2:40) path to a certified device.\n- **Risks and fallbacks:** protocol not available → no demo; unknown backdrivability and no certified safety functions → keep torque limits very low, hardware e-stop, mechanical end stops, **demo on a dummy first**; judges may see it as off-the-shelf motor control → emphasise the adaptive controller and data.\n- **Judging angles:** *Innovation* moderate; *Technical depth* high if force control works; *Impact* large market but long regulatory path; *Leukerbad* rehab clinics.\n\n## Cross-vendor combos worth considering\n\n| Combo | What it enables | Integration effort |\n|---|---|---|\n| DH116S + camera only | Mirror coach (Concept 1) | **Low** — one device, one SDK |\n| DH116-M + Flexiv Rizon or ROKAE xMate | Force-aware hand-over, simple feeding-aid or dressing-aid motions | **Medium** — flange adapter, two SDKs, cable routing |\n| DH hand + OriginFlow EMG | Muscle-controlled assistive hand / prosthesis trainer | **Medium** — depends on real-time EMG access |\n| DH hand + XREAL glasses | AR-guided therapy overlay, or first-person tele-care view | **Medium** — separate app; loose coupling over network |\n| DH hand + Galaxea R1 / AGIBOT G1 / Unitree G1 (host robot) | Mobile fetch-and-hand-over, medication round | **High** — replacing a host robot's end-effector in 48 h is risky; only if the vendors already have an adapter and driver |\n| DH hand + D-Robotics RDK X5 | Self-contained edge unit (no laptop) | **Low–medium** — SDK lists ARM support; verify the Linux build runs |\n| DH hand + Dobot desktop / CR arm | Low-cost reach for table-top demos | **Medium** — check payload (hand 359–850 g plus object) |\n| PJ3 + coprod modular hardware | Custom assistive joint or fixture | **High** — no public PJ3 protocol |\n\nNote: WUJI also offers a dexterous hand on site — compare availability, SDK maturity and tactile options before choosing.\n\n## Questions to ask the Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. engineers on site\n\n1. Which hands can teams borrow (model, left / right, bus, tactile -S / -M), how many, and for how long?\n2. Can you give us the current LHandProLib SDK, ROS 2 workspace and URDF / MJCF on a USB stick — and which generation does this unit need?\n3. Does the SDK expose **per-finger force / current limits** and raw tactile values at what update rate? Is there a compliant or torque-limited grip mode?\n4. Are the fingers self-locking when powered off? How do we open the hand in an emergency?\n5. Do you have a flange adapter for the arms on site (Flexiv, ROKAE, Dobot, Unitree, AGIBOT), and a suitable power supply and USB-CAN FD adapter?\n6. Is there a ROS 2 package for the DH2116, and is the data glove (6248XL) available with a real-time interface?\n7. For PJ3: can you share the CAN FD protocol, confirm torque / current control mode, backdrivability and peak torque, and lend a module with an adapter?\n8. Has any customer used your hands or joints in care, rehabilitation, prosthetics or exoskeletons that we may cite?\n\n## Decision rule\n\n- **Pick Leadshine Technology Co., Ltd. for T04 if** a hand is confirmed available with SDK and power in the first hour, and your idea centres on the *hand itself* (therapy mirroring, hand-over, EMG control) — alone on a desk or on a force-controlled arm from another vendor.\n- **Avoid if** you need a complete robot, mobility, autonomy or patient-contact force control out of the box, or if your plan depends on the PJ3 / HJ2 joint modules without a protocol in hand.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [Leadshine company profile](../Leadshine.md)\n- [DH116S user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116 / DH116P user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116_DH116P_Series_Dexterous_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH2116 user manual (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH2116_Series_Dexterous_Hand_User_Manual.pdf)\n- [DH116S LHandPro ROS2 example project description (PDF)](https://store.leadshine.com/cdn/shop/files/DH116S_LHandPro_ROS2_Example_Project_Description.pdf)\n- [DH116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116-series.html)\n- [DH116S series product page](https://en.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh116s-series.html)\n- [DH2116 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/dextrous-hands/dh2116-series.html)\n- [PJ3 series product page](https://www.leadshine.com/robots/integrated-actuators/PJ3-Series.html)\n- [FAIR plus 2026 recap (Leadshine store blog)](https://store.leadshine.com/blogs/news/leadshine-dexterous-hands-and-joint-modules-shine-at-the-shenzhen-fair-plus-2026)\n- [Leadshine at CES 2026 — supply-chain release (PR Newswire Asia)](https://en.prnasia.com/releases/global/leadshine-achieves-great-success-at-ces-2026-entering-us-and-european-robotics-supply-chain-518892.shtml)\n"}});
